rm": "Test-time compute", "definition": "Compute a model spends while answering, such as thinking through steps first."}], "sources": [{"url": "https://openai.com/index/learning-to-reason-with-llms/", "label": "OpenAI, Learning to reason with LLMs, Sep 2024"}]}, {"kind": "info", "id": "limits", "eyebrow": "Open question", "title": "New curve, new limits", "body": "OpenAI says the limits on scaling this approach differ a lot from those of pre-training. It is still studying them.", "sources": [{"url": "https://openai.com/index/learning-to-reason-with-llms/", "label": "OpenAI, Learning to reason with LLMs, Sep 2024"}]}, {"kind": "spotFake", "id": "fake", "prompt": "One of these is false. Tap it.", "statements": ["GPT-4.5 cost $75 per million input tokens in the API.", "o1 scored 74% on the 2024 AIME with one try per problem.", "OpenAI kept GPT-4.5 as its API flagship through 2025."], "fakeIndex": 2, "explanation": "OpenAI said in April 2025 that GPT-4.5 would leave the API by July 14."}, {"kind": "numberSense", "id": "ns-aime", "prompt": "On the 2024 AIME, o1 scored 74%. What percent did GPT-4o score?", "min": 0, "max": 60, "value": 12, "unit": "%", "explanation": "GPT-4o averaged 12%. Thinking longer before answering made the difference."}, {"kind": "theCall", "id": "call", "situation": "Your product must solve hard multi-step math problems on a fixed budget.", "prompt": "Which kind of model do you test first?", "options": ["A reasoning model that thinks before answering", "The largest pre-trained model you can buy", "The smallest model, answering instantly"], "consequences": ["o1 beat GPT-4o on AIME, 74% to 12%, by thinking longer.", "GPT-4.5 was the largest and still trailed reasoning models on math.", "Instant answers are cheap but weak on multi-step problems."], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "For hard reasoning, spending compute at answer time can beat spending it on size."}, {"id": "lens-build_startup", "kind": "info", "title": "Spend compute where it pays", "body": "For hard reasoning, a model that thinks longer can beat a larger one. Match the model to the task, not to its size.", "eyebrow": "For founders", "sources": [{"url": "https://openai.com/index/learning-to-reason-with-llms/", "label": "OpenAI, Learning to reason with LLMs, Sep 2024"}, {"url": "https://techcrunch.com/2025/02/27/openai-unveils-gpt-4-5-orion-its-largest-ai-model-yet", "label": "TechCrunch, OpenAI unveils GPT-4.5, Feb 2025"}]}, {"id": "rule", "kind": "microInsight", "text": "In AI, when bigger stops paying, labs scale thinking time instead.", "eyebrow": "The rule", "sources": [{"url": "https://techcrunch.com/2025/02/27/openai-unveils-gpt-4-5-orion-its-largest-ai-model-yet", "label": "TechCrunch, OpenAI unveils GPT-4.5, Feb 2025"}, {"url": "https://openai.com/index/learning-to-reason-with-llms/", "label": "OpenAI, Learning to reason with LLMs, Sep 2024"}]}, {"id": "say-it", "kind": "sayIt", "takeaway": "GPT-4.5 was OpenAI's largest model, cost 30 times GPT-4o per input token and left the API within months. Labs now scale thinking time, as o1 did when it jumped to 74% on AIME.", "sectionKey": "first_test"}]}$ml$::jsonb),
