t-reports", "label": "Reuters via The Star, OpenAI forecasts cash burn near $280 billion by 2030, Sep 2026"}]}, {"id": "rule", "kind": "microInsight", "text": "In AI, the arms race lasts only as long as revenue or investors keep paying for it.", "eyebrow": "The rule", "sources": [{"url": "https://www.thestar.com.my/tech/tech-news/2026/09/19/openai-forecasts-cash-burn-near-280-billion-by-2030-ft-reports", "label": "Reuters via The Star, OpenAI forecasts cash burn near $280 billion by 2030, Sep 2026"}, {"url": "https://www.bankofengland.co.uk/financial-policy-committee-record/2025/october-2025", "label": "Bank of England, Financial Policy Committee record, Oct 2025"}]}, {"id": "say-it", "kind": "sayIt", "takeaway": "OpenAI projects $278 billion of cash burn through 2030 and about $856 billion of compute. The race depends on revenue growing about tenfold.", "sectionKey": "first_test"}]}$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$8b3807df-5c09-5564-af3d-5cb0db6e6e54$ml$::uuid, $ml$ai$ml$, $ml$A $278 billion bet on revenue$ml$, 'LESSON', ARRAY[$ml$MICRO_LESSON$ml$, $ml$day-42$ml$, $ml$month-2$ml$, $ml$handwritten-v1$ml$, $ml$goal:curiosity$ml$, $ml$level:beginner$ml$, $ml$level:intermediate$ml$, $ml$level:advanced$ml$]::text[], 5, now(), '{}'::jsonb, $ml${"id": "ai-d42-curiosity", "title": "A $278 billion bet on revenue", "exercises": [{"id": "open", "kind": "coldOpen", "kicker": "The race runs on cash it has not earned yet.", "eyebrow": "Day 42 · Where it breaks", "headline": "In September 2026, OpenAI projected it would burn $278 billion in cash from 2026 to 2030.", "fullText": "The Financial Times reported the figures from a company presentation. OpenAI expects to spend about $856 billion on compute and infrastructure by the end of 2030, its largest cost. It forecasts revenue rising from $36 billion this year to $350 billion in 2030. It was on track to use up its March raise by 2028.", "sources": [{"url": "https://www.thestar.com.my/tech/tech-news/2026/09/19/openai-forecasts-cash-burn-near-280-billion-by-2030-ft-reports", "label": "Reuters via The Star, OpenAI forecasts cash burn near $280 billion by 2030, Sep 2026"}]}, {"kind": "microInsight", "id": "commit", "eyebrow": "The commitments", "text": "In November 2025, Sam Altman cited about $1.4 trillion of data center commitments over eight years.", "highlight": "OpenAI then expected above $20 billion of annualized revenue.", "sources": [{"url": "https://techcrunch.com/2025/11/06/sam-altman-says-openai-has-20b-arr-and-about-1-4-trillion-in-data-center-commitments", "label": "TechCrunch, Altman cites $20B ARR and about $1.4 trillion of commitments, Nov 2025"}]}, {"kind": "microInsight", "id": "warning", "eyebrow": "The warning", "text": "Central banks are watching the valuations behind the race.", "highlight": "In October 2025, the Bank of England said the risk of a sharp market correction had increased.", "sources": [{"url": "https://www.bankofengland.co.uk/financial-policy-committee-record/2025/october-2025", "label": "Bank of England, Financial Policy Committee record, Oct 2025"}]}, {"kind": "info", "id": "gap", "eyebrow": "The gap", "title": "Revenue has to grow tenfold", "body": "OpenAI's plan needs revenue to rise from $36 billion to $350 billion by 2030. Revenue would total about $840 billion, against $856 billion of compute.", "sources": [{"url": "https://www.thestar.com.my/tech/tech-news/2026/09/19/openai-forecasts-cash-burn-near-280-billion-by-2030-ft-reports", "label": "Reuters via The Star, OpenAI forecasts cash burn near $280 billion by 2030, Sep 2026"}]}, {"kind": "numberSense", "id": "ns-x", "prompt": "From $36 billion this year to $350 billion in 2030: about how many times larger is that?", "min": 0, "max": 30, "value": 10, "unit": "x", "explanation": "350 ÷ 36 is about 9.7, so roughly tenfold in four years."}, {"kind": "spotFake", "id": "fake", "prompt": "One of these is false. Tap it.", "statements": ["OpenAI projected $278 billion of cash burn from 2026 to 2030.", "The Bank of England warned of a higher risk of a sharp correction.", "OpenAI expects compute to be its smallest cost through 2030."], "fakeIndex": 2, "explanation": "Compute and infrastructure, about $856 billion by 2030, is its largest expense."}, {"kind": "multipleChoice", "id": "review", "prompt": "Week 6 review: at OpenAI in 2024, what share of R&D compute went to final training runs?", "options": ["About 10%", "About 50%", "About 90%"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "About $500 million of $5 billion. Most compute went to research and experiments."}, {"id": "lens-curiosity", "kind": "info", "title": "A tenfold bet", "body": "OpenAI expects to burn $278 billion from 2026 to 2030, betting its revenue grows about tenfold.", "eyebrow": "Why it matters", "sources": [{"url": "https://www.thestar.com.my/tech/tech-news/2026/09/19/openai-forecasts-cash-burn-near-280-billion-by-2030-ft-reports", "label": "Reuters via The Star, OpenAI forecasts cash burn near $280 billion by 2030, Sep 2026"}]}, {"id": "rule", "kind": "microInsight", "text": "In AI, the arms race lasts only as long as revenue or investors keep paying for it.", "eyebrow": "The rule", "sources": [{"url": "https://www.thestar.com.my/tech/tech-news/2026/09/19/openai-forecasts-cash-burn-near-280-billion-by-2030-ft-reports", "label": "Reuters via The Star, OpenAI forecasts cash burn near $280 billion by 2030, Sep 2026"}, {"url": "https://www.bankofengland.co.uk/financial-policy-committee-record/2025/october-2025", "label": "Bank of England, Financial Policy Committee record, Oct 2025"}]}, {"id": "say-it", "kind": "sayIt", "takeaway": "OpenAI projects $278 billion of cash burn through 2030 and about $856 billion of compute. The race depends on revenue growing about tenfold.", "sectionKey": "frontier"}]}$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$504eca7a-18cf-599c-94c9-7493c2c83ed6$ml$::uuid, $ml$ai$ml$, $ml$The small model people preferred$ml$, 'LESSON', ARRAY[$ml$MICRO_LESSON$ml$, $ml$day-43$ml$, $ml$month-2$ml$, $ml$handwritten-v1$ml$, $ml$goal:career$ml$, $ml$level:beginner$ml$, $ml$level:intermediate$ml$, $ml$level:advanced$ml$]::text[], 5, now(), '{}'::jsonb, $ml${"id": "ai-d43-career", "title": "The small model people preferred", "exercises": [{"id": "open", "kind": "coldOpen", "kicker": "What a model learns after pretraining changes what users see.", "eyebrow": "Day 43 · Fine-tuning", "headline": "In 2022, people preferred answers from a 1.3-billion-parameter model to those of the 175-billion-parameter GPT-3.", "fullText": "OpenAI trained GPT-3 further on what people wanted and called the result InstructGPT. Labelers preferred the outputs of its 1.3 billion parameter version to those of the original 175 billion parameter GPT-3, despite 100 times fewer parameters. Size did not make the difference. The training after pretraining did: answers written by people, then their rankings of answers.", "sources": [{"url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/2203.02155", "label": "Ouyang et al., Training language models to follow instructions with human feedback, 2022"}]}, {"kind": "microInsight", "id": "sft", "eyebrow": "Step one", "text": "First, people write good answers to prompts, and the model trains on them.", "highlight": "InstructGPT's supervised fine-tuning set held about 13,000 training prompts.", "keyTerms": [{"term": "Fine-tuning", "definition": "Further training of a pretrained model on a smaller, targeted dataset."}], "sources": [{"url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/2203.02155", "label": "Ouyang et al., Training language models to follow instructions with human feedback, 2022"}]}, {"kind": "microInsight", "id": "rlhf", "eyebrow": "Steps two and three", "text": "Next, people rank several answers, and a reward model learns their taste. The model then trains to score well on it.", "highlight": "That loop is reinforcement learning from human feedback, or RLHF.", "keyTerms": [{"term": "RLHF", "definition": "Training a model against a reward learned from human rankings of its answers."}], "sources": [{"url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/2203.02155", "label": "Ouyang et al., Training language models to follow instructions with human feedback, 2022"}]}, {"kind": "info", "id": "small-step", "eyebrow": "What it is not", "title": "Not a new model, a small extra step", "body": "Almost all of what a model knows comes from pretraining. Fine-tuning shapes how it answers, for a fraction of the compute.", "bullets": ["InstructGPT's RLHF step took 60 petaflop/s-days; GPT-3 took 3,640."], "sources": [{"url": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2305.11206", "label": "Zhou et al., LIMA: Less Is More for Alignment, May 2023"}, {"url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/2203.02155", "label": "Ouyang et al., Training language models to follow instructions with human feedback, 2022"}]}, {"kind": "buildChain", "id": "chain", "prompt": "Put the InstructGPT recipe in order.", "steps": ["Pretrain a model on huge amounts of text", "Fine-tune it on answers people wrote", "Train a reward model on people's rankings", "Optimize the model against that reward"], "explanation": "Pretraining comes first. Then supervised fine-tuning, then a reward model, then reinforcement learning against it."}, {"kind": "sortSignal", "id": "sort", "prompt": "Which stage does each belong to?", "buckets": ["Pretraining", "Fine-tuning", "RLHF"], "items": [{"text": "Learning most of what the model knows", "bucket": 0}, {"text": "Using by far the most compute", "bucket": 0}, {"text": "Training on answers people wrote", "bucket": 1}, {"text": "About 13,000 written prompts", "bucket": 1}, {"text": "People ranking several answers", "bucket": 2}, {"text": "A reward model scoring outputs", "bucket": 2}], "explanation": "Pretraining builds knowledge with most of the compute. Fine-tuning copies written answers, and RLHF learns from rankings."}, {"kind": "multipleChoice", "id": "mc", "prompt": "Why did people prefer the 1.3B InstructGPT to the 175B GPT-3?", "options": ["It had more parameters than GPT-3", "It was trained on what people wanted", "It read more web pages in pretraining"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "It had 100 times fewer parameters. Training on human demonstrations and rankings made the difference."}, {"id": "lens-career", "kind": "info", "title": "Name the stage, not just the model", "body": "Say whether a behavior comes from pretraining, fine-tuning or RLHF. InstructGPT shows the later stages shape what users see.", "eyebrow": "For your interview", "sources": [{"url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/2203.02155", "label": "Ouyang et al., Training language models to follow instructions with human feedback, 2022"}]}, {"id": "rule", "kind": "microInsight", "text": "In AI, what a model learns after pretraining shapes what users actually get.", "eyebrow": "The rule", "sources": [{"url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/2203.02155", "label": "Ouyang et al., Training language models to follow instructions with human feedback, 2022"}, {"url": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2305.11206", "label": "Zhou et al., LIMA: Less Is More for Alignment, May 2023"}]}, {"id": "say-it", "kind": "sayIt", "takeaway": "Fine-tuning trains a pretrained model on answers people wrote, and RLHF trains it on their rankings. A 1.3B InstructGPT beat the 175B GPT-3 with people, for a fraction of the compute.", "sectionKey": "players"}]}$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$44a04ead-c0d5-5730-9b5b-428ab49456dd$ml$::uuid, $ml$ai$ml$, $ml$The small model people preferred$ml$, 'LESSON', ARRAY[$ml$MICRO_LESSON$ml$, $ml$day-43$ml$, $ml$month-2$ml$, $ml$handwritten-v1$ml$, $ml$goal:invest$ml$, $ml$level:beginner$ml$, $ml$level:intermediate$ml$, $ml$level:advanced$ml$]::text[], 5, now(), '{}'::jsonb, $ml${"id": "ai-d43-invest", "title": "The small model people preferred", "exercises": [{"id": "open", "kind": "coldOpen", "kicker": "What a model learns after pretraining changes what users see.", "eyebrow": "Day 43 · Fine-tuning", "headline": "In 2022, people preferred answers from a 1.3-billion-parameter model to those of the 175-billion-parameter GPT-3.", "fullText": "OpenAI trained GPT-3 further on what people wanted and called the result InstructGPT. Labelers preferred the outputs of its 1.3 billion parameter version to those of the original 175 billion parameter GPT-3, despite 100 times fewer parameters. Size did not make the difference. The training after pretraining did: answers written by people, then their rankings of answers.", "sources": [{"url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/2203.02155", "label": "Ouyang et al., Training language models to follow instructions with human feedback, 2022"}]}, {"kind": "microInsight", "id": "sft", "eyebrow": "Step one", "text": "First, people write good answers to prompts, and the model trains on them.", "highlight": "InstructGPT's supervised fine-tuning set held about 13,000 training prompts.", "keyTerms": [{"term": "Fine-tuning", "definition": "Further training of a pretrained model on a smaller, targeted dataset."}], "sources": [{"url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/2203.02155", "label": "Ouyang et al., Training language models to follow instructions with human feedback, 2022"}]}, {"kind": "microInsight", "id": "rlhf", "eyebrow": "Steps two and three", "text": "Next, people rank several answers, and a reward model learns their taste. The model then trains to score well on it.", "highlight": "That loop is reinforcement learning from human feedback, or RLHF.", "keyTerms": [{"term": "RLHF", "definition": "Training a model against a reward learned from human rankings of its answers."}], "sources": [{"url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/2203.02155", "label": "Ouyang et al., Training language models to follow instructions with human feedback, 2022"}]}, {"kind": "info", "id": "small-step", "eyebrow": "What it is not", "title": "Not a new model, a small extra step", "body": "Almost all of what a model knows comes from pretraining. Fine-tuning shapes how it answers, for a fraction of the compute.", "bullets": ["InstructGPT's RLHF step took 60 petaflop/s-days; GPT-3 took 3,640."], "sources": [{"url": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2305.11206", "label": "Zhou et al., LIMA: Less Is More for Alignment, May 2023"}, {"url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/2203.02155", "label": "Ouyang et al., Training language models to follow instructions with human feedback, 2022"}]}, {"kind": "buildChain", "id": "chain", "prompt": "Put the InstructGPT recipe in order.", "steps": ["Pretrain a model on huge amounts of text", "Fine-tune it on answers people wrote", "Train a reward model on people's rankings", "Optimize the model against that reward"], "explanation": "Pretraining comes first. Then supervised fine-tuning, then a reward model, then reinforcement learning against it."}, {"kind": "sortSignal", "id": "sort", "prompt": "Which stage does each belong to?", "buckets": ["Pretraining", "Fine-tuning", "RLHF"], "items": [{"text": "Learning most of what the model knows", "bucket": 0}, {"text": "Using by far the most compute", "bucket": 0}, {"text": "Training on answers people wrote", "bucket": 1}, {"text": "About 13,000 written prompts", "bucket": 1}, {"text": "People ranking several answers", "bucket": 2}, {"text": "A reward model scoring outputs", "bucket": 2}], "explanation": "Pretraining builds knowledge with most of the compute. Fine-tuning copies written answers, and RLHF learns from rankings."}, {"kind": "multipleChoice", "id": "mc", "prompt": "Why did people prefer the 1.3B InstructGPT to the 175B GPT-3?", "options": ["It had more parameters than GPT-3", "It was trained on what people wanted", "It read more web pages in pretraining"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "It had 100 times fewer parameters. Training on human demonstrations and rankings made the difference."}, {"id": "lens-invest", "kind": "info", "title": "Size is not the only lever", "body": "A 1.3B model beat a 175B one in human ratings after feedback training. Post-training can matter more than size.", "eyebrow": "For investors", "sources": [{"url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/2203.02155", "label": "Ouyang et al., Training language models to follow instructions with human feedback, 2022"}]}, {"id": "rule", "kind": "microInsight", "text": "In AI, what a model learns after pretraining shapes what users actually get.", "eyebrow": "The rule", "sources": [{"url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/2203.02155", "label": "Ouyang et al., Training language models to follow instructions with human feedback, 2022"}, {"url": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2305.11206", "label": "Zhou et al., LIMA: Less Is More for Alignment, May 2023"}]}, {"id": "say-it", "kind": "sayIt", "takeaway": "Fine-tuning trains a pretrained model on answers people wrote, and RLHF trains it on their rankings. A 1.3B InstructGPT beat the 175B GPT-3 with people, for a fraction of the compute.", "sectionKey": "watchlist"}]}$ml$::jsonb),
