: "multipleChoice", "id": "mc", "prompt": "Why did people prefer the 1.3B InstructGPT to the 175B GPT-3?", "options": ["It had more parameters than GPT-3", "It was trained on what people wanted", "It read more web pages in pretraining"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "It had 100 times fewer parameters. Training on human demonstrations and rankings made the difference."}, {"id": "lens-build_startup", "kind": "info", "title": "The cheap step is the open one", "body": "GPT-3's pretraining took 3,640 petaflop/s-days; InstructGPT's RLHF step took 60. Shaping a model costs far less than building one.", "eyebrow": "For founders", "sources": [{"url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/2203.02155", "label": "Ouyang et al., Training language models to follow instructions with human feedback, 2022"}]}, {"id": "rule", "kind": "microInsight", "text": "In AI, what a model learns after pretraining shapes what users actually get.", "eyebrow": "The rule", "sources": [{"url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/2203.02155", "label": "Ouyang et al., Training language models to follow instructions with human feedback, 2022"}, {"url": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2305.11206", "label": "Zhou et al., LIMA: Less Is More for Alignment, May 2023"}]}, {"id": "say-it", "kind": "sayIt", "takeaway": "Fine-tuning trains a pretrained model on answers people wrote, and RLHF trains it on their rankings. A 1.3B InstructGPT beat the 175B GPT-3 with people, for a fraction of the compute.", "sectionKey": "money"}]}$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$ff19e58e-0d41-5e12-bb8d-26bb6198c9b5$ml$::uuid, $ml$ai$ml$, $ml$The small model people preferred$ml$, 'LESSON', ARRAY[$ml$MICRO_LESSON$ml$, $ml$day-43$ml$, $ml$month-2$ml$, $ml$handwritten-v1$ml$, $ml$goal:curiosity$ml$, $ml$level:beginner$ml$, $ml$level:intermediate$ml$, $ml$level:advanced$ml$]::text[], 5, now(), '{}'::jsonb, $ml${"id": "ai-d43-curiosity", "title": "The small model people preferred", "exercises": [{"id": "open", "kind": "coldOpen", "kicker": "What a model learns after pretraining changes what users see.", "eyebrow": "Day 43 · Fine-tuning", "headline": "In 2022, people preferred answers from a 1.3-billion-parameter model to those of the 175-billion-parameter GPT-3.", "fullText": "OpenAI trained GPT-3 further on what people wanted and called the result InstructGPT. Labelers preferred the outputs of its 1.3 billion parameter version to those of the original 175 billion parameter GPT-3, despite 100 times fewer parameters. Size did not make the difference. The training after pretraining did: answers written by people, then their rankings of answers.", "sources": [{"url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/2203.02155", "label": "Ouyang et al., Training language models to follow instructions with human feedback, 2022"}]}, {"kind": "microInsight", "id": "sft", "eyebrow": "Step one", "text": "First, people write good answers to prompts, and the model trains on them.", "highlight": "InstructGPT's supervised fine-tuning set held about 13,000 training prompts.", "keyTerms": [{"term": "Fine-tuning", "definition": "Further training of a pretrained model on a smaller, targeted dataset."}], "sources": [{"url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/2203.02155", "label": "Ouyang et al., Training language models to follow instructions with human feedback, 2022"}]}, {"kind": "microInsight", "id": "rlhf", "eyebrow": "Steps two and three", "text": "Next, people rank several answers, and a reward model learns their taste. The model then trains to score well on it.", "highlight": "That loop is reinforcement learning from human feedback, or RLHF.", "keyTerms": [{"term": "RLHF", "definition": "Training a model against a reward learned from human rankings of its answers."}], "sources": [{"url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/2203.02155", "label": "Ouyang et al., Training language models to follow instructions with human feedback, 2022"}]}, {"kind": "info", "id": "small-step", "eyebrow": "What it is not", "title": "Not a new model, a small extra step", "body": "Almost all of what a model knows comes from pretraining. Fine-tuning shapes how it answers, for a fraction of the compute.", "bullets": ["InstructGPT's RLHF step took 60 petaflop/s-days; GPT-3 took 3,640."], "sources": [{"url": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2305.11206", "label": "Zhou et al., LIMA: Less Is More for Alignment, May 2023"}, {"url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/2203.02155", "label": "Ouyang et al., Training language models to follow instructions with human feedback, 2022"}]}, {"kind": "buildChain", "id": "chain", "prompt": "Put the InstructGPT recipe in order.", "steps": ["Pretrain a model on huge amounts of text", "Fine-tune it on answers people wrote", "Train a reward model on people's rankings", "Optimize the model against that reward"], "explanation": "Pretraining comes first. Then supervised fine-tuning, then a reward model, then reinforcement learning against it."}, {"kind": "sortSignal", "id": "sort", "prompt": "Which stage does each belong to?", "buckets": ["Pretraining", "Fine-tuning", "RLHF"], "items": [{"text": "Learning most of what the model knows", "bucket": 0}, {"text": "Using by far the most compute", "bucket": 0}, {"text": "Training on answers people wrote", "bucket": 1}, {"text": "About 13,000 written prompts", "bucket": 1}, {"text": "People ranking several answers", "bucket": 2}, {"text": "A reward model scoring outputs", "bucket": 2}], "explanation": "Pretraining builds knowledge with most of the compute. Fine-tuning copies written answers, and RLHF learns from rankings."}, {"kind": "multipleChoice", "id": "mc", "prompt": "Why did people prefer the 1.3B InstructGPT to the 175B GPT-3?", "options": ["It had more parameters than GPT-3", "It was trained on what people wanted", "It read more web pages in pretraining"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "It had 100 times fewer parameters. Training on human demonstrations and rankings made the difference."}, {"id": "lens-curiosity", "kind": "info", "title": "Bigger did not mean more helpful", "body": "The InstructGPT authors found a bigger model does not follow intent better on its own. Training on human feedback did.", "eyebrow": "Why it matters", "sources": [{"url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/2203.02155", "label": "Ouyang et al., Training language models to follow instructions with human feedback, 2022"}]}, {"id": "rule", "kind": "microInsight", "text": "In AI, what a model learns after pretraining shapes what users actually get.", "eyebrow": "The rule", "sources": [{"url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/2203.02155", "label": "Ouyang et al., Training language models to follow instructions with human feedback, 2022"}, {"url": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2305.11206", "label": "Zhou et al., LIMA: Less Is More for Alignment, May 2023"}]}, {"id": "say-it", "kind": "sayIt", "takeaway": "Fine-tuning trains a pretrained model on answers people wrote, and RLHF trains it on their rankings. A 1.3B InstructGPT beat the 175B GPT-3 with people, for a fraction of the compute.", "sectionKey": "basics"}]}$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$8ab1afcb-3c54-53f3-9a1d-3cdc6b93bc98$ml$::uuid, $ml$ai$ml$, $ml$Who gets paid to teach AI$ml$, 'LESSON', ARRAY[$ml$MICRO_LESSON$ml$, $ml$day-44$ml$, $ml$month-2$ml$, $ml$handwritten-v1$ml$, $ml$goal:career$ml$, $ml$level:beginner$ml$, $ml$level:intermediate$ml$, $ml$level:advanced$ml$]::text[], 5, now(), '{}'::jsonb, $ml${"id": "ai-d44-career", "title": "Who gets paid to teach AI", "exercises": [{"id": "open", "kind": "coldOpen", "kicker": "Teaching models has become a payroll.", "eyebrow": "Day 44 · The money", "headline": "In October 2025, Mercor said it paid its contractors more than $1.5 million a day to help train AI models.", "fullText": "Mercor connects AI labs with domain experts, such as scientists, doctors and lawyers, who train foundation models. It had more than 30,000 experts on its roster, earning over $85 an hour on average. That month it raised $350 million at a $10 billion valuation.", "sources": [{"url": "https://techcrunch.com/2025/10/27/mercor-quintuples-valuation-to-10b-with-350m-series-c/", "label": "TechCrunch, Mercor quintuples valuation to $10B with $350M Series C, Oct 2025"}]}, {"kind": "microInsight", "id": "chain", "eyebrow": "Who pays whom", "text": "Labs pay vendors, and vendors pay the people who write and rank answers.", "highlight": "InstructGPT used about 40 contractors hired on Upwork and through Scale AI.", "sources": [{"url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/2203.02155", "label": "Ouyang et al., Training language models to follow instructions with human feedback, 2022"}]}, {"kind": "microInsight", "id": "margin", "eyebrow": "Where the margin sits", "text": "The vendor keeps the gap between what a lab pays and what workers earn.", "highlight": "OpenAI paid Sama $12.50 an hour; its Kenyan workers took home about $1.32 to $2.", "sources": [{"url": "https://time.com/6247678/openai-chatgpt-kenya-workers/", "label": "TIME, OpenAI used Kenyan workers on less than $2 per hour, Jan 2023"}]}, {"kind": "info", "id": "experts", "eyebrow": "The shift", "title": "From cheap labels to expert judgment", "body": "Feedback work has moved toward highly skilled contractors instead of large pools of low-wage labor. Expert hours cost far more.", "bullets": ["Mercor's experts earned over $85 an hour on average."], "sources": [{"url": "https://www.aol.com/news/exclusive-scale-ais-bigger-rival-152327362.html", "label": "Reuters via AOL, Scale AI's bigger rival Surge AI seeks up to $1 billion, Jul 2025"}, {"url": "https://techcrunch.com/2025/10/27/mercor-quintuples-valuation-to-10b-with-350m-series-c/", "label": "TechCrunch, Mercor quintuples valuation to $10B with $350M Series C, Oct 2025"}]}, {"kind": "numberSense", "id": "ns-share", "prompt": "OpenAI paid Sama $12.50 an hour. If a worker took home $2, what share reached the worker, in percent?", "min": 0, "max": 60, "value": 16, "unit": "%", "explanation": "$2 out of $12.50 is 16%. The vendor kept the rest to cover its costs and margin."}, {"kind": "buildChain", "id": "chain", "prompt": "Follow the money in human feedback.", "steps": ["An AI lab signs a data contract", "A vendor recruits and pays contractors", "Contractors write and rank answers", "The lab trains its model on that feedback"], "explanation": "Money flows from the lab to the vendor to the contractors. The feedback flows back to the lab."}, {"kind": "theCall", "id": "call", "situation": "You run a lab buying feedback data for a medical chatbot.", "prompt": "Whose judgment do you pay for?", "options": ["Doctors who can judge the answers", "The cheapest labelers you can find", "Nobody; the model can rank itself"], "consequences": ["Experts cost more but can tell good answers from bad.", "Cheap raters may not spot a wrong medical answer.", "A model grading itself repeats its own mistakes."], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "Feedback is only as good as the judge. That is why labs now pay experts, not just labelers."}, {"id": "lens-career", "kind": "info", "title": "Feedback work is a way in", "body": "Mercor paid experts over $85 an hour on average to train models. Domain skill plus AI literacy is a real job path.", "eyebrow": "For your interview", "sources": [{"url": "https://techcrunch.com/2025/10/27/mercor-quintuples-valuation-to-10b-with-350m-series-c/", "label": "TechCrunch, Mercor quintuples valuation to $10B with $350M Series C, Oct 2025"}]}, {"id": "rule", "kind": "microInsight", "text": "In AI feedback, the money follows scarce human judgment.", "eyebrow": "The rule", "sources": [{"url": "https://techcrunch.com/2025/10/27/mercor-quintuples-valuation-to-10b-with-350m-series-c/", "label": "TechCrunch, Mercor quintuples valuation to $10B with $350M Series C, Oct 2025"}, {"url": "https://www.aol.com/news/exclusive-scale-ais-bigger-rival-152327362.html", "label": "Reuters via AOL, Scale AI's bigger rival Surge AI seeks up to $1 billion, Jul 2025"}]}, {"id": "say-it", "kind": "sayIt", "takeaway": "Labs pay vendors, and vendors pay the people who write and rank answers. Mercor paid experts over $1.5 million a day in 2025, while one earlier contract left workers about $2 of each $12.50.", "sectionKey": "how_money_moves"}]}$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$86807b7a-8345-5e83-836a-7c6aad889604$ml$::uuid, $ml$ai$ml$, $ml$Who gets paid to teach AI$ml$, 'LESSON', ARRAY[$ml$MICRO_LESSON$ml$, $ml$day-44$ml$, $ml$month-2$ml$, $ml$handwritten-v1$ml$, $ml$goal:invest$ml$, $ml$level:beginner$ml$, $ml$level:intermediate$ml$, $ml$level:advanced$ml$]::text[], 5, now(), '{}'::jsonb, $ml${"id": "ai-d44-invest", "title": "Who gets paid to teach AI", "exercises": [{"id": "open", "kind": "coldOpen", "kicker": "Teaching models has become a payroll.", "eyebrow": "Day 44 · The money", "headline": "In October 2025, Mercor said it paid its contractors more than $1.5 million a day to help train AI models.", "fullText": "Mercor connects AI labs with domain experts, such as scientists, doctors and lawyers, who train foundation models. It had more than 30,000 experts on its roster, earning over $85 an hour on average. That month it raised $350 million at a $10 billion valuation.", "sources": [{"url": "https://techcrunch.com/2025/10/27/mercor-quintuples-valuation-to-10b-with-350m-series-c/", "label": "TechCrunch, Mercor quintuples valuation to $10B with $350M Series C, Oct 2025"}]}, {"kind": "microInsight", "id": "chain", "eyebrow": "Who pays whom", "text": "Labs pay vendors, and vendors pay the people who write and rank answers.", "highlight": "InstructGPT used about 40 contractors hired on Upwork and through Scale AI.", "sources": [{"url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/2203.02155", "label": "Ouyang et al., Training language models to follow instructions with human feedback, 2022"}]}, {"kind": "microInsight", "id": "margin", "eyebrow": "Where the margin sits", "text": "The vendor keeps the gap between what a lab pays and what workers earn.", "highlight": "OpenAI paid Sama $12.50 an hour; its Kenyan workers took home about $1.32 to $2.", "sources": [{"url": "https://time.com/6247678/openai-chatgpt-kenya-workers/", "label": "TIME, OpenAI used Kenyan workers on less than $2 per hour, Jan 2023"}]}, {"kind": "info", "id": "experts", "eyebrow": "The sh