693v1", "label": "Qi et al., Fine-tuning aligned language models compromises safety, Oct 2023"}]}, {"kind": "info", "id": "veto", "eyebrow": "Who can say no", "title": "Hosted fine-tuning keeps a veto", "body": "A provider that hosts fine-tuning can test and watch the results. Anyone holding open weights can fine-tune them alone.", "bullets": ["OpenAI runs automated safety evaluations on fine-tuned GPT-4o models."], "sources": [{"url": "https://openai.com/index/gpt-4o-fine-tuning/", "label": "OpenAI, Fine-tuning now available for GPT-4o, Aug 2024"}, {"url": "https://www.theregister.com/2023/10/12/chatbot_defenses_dissolve/", "label": "The Register, chatbot defenses dissolve with fine-tuning, Oct 2023"}]}, {"kind": "faceOff", "id": "face", "prompt": "Who can still block a harmful fine-tune?", "left": {"name": "A hosted fine-tuning provider", "note": "Runs safety tests on tuned models"}, "right": {"name": "An open-weight model maker", "note": "Weights already downloaded by others"}, "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "A host sees every tuned model and can test or refuse it. Open weights run wherever someone copies them."}, {"kind": "multipleChoice", "id": "mc", "prompt": "How many examples did it take to break GPT-3.5 Turbo's guardrails?", "options": ["About 10,000", "About a million", "About 10"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "Just 10 examples, for less than $0.20 through the fine-tuning service."}, {"kind": "theCall", "id": "call", "situation": "You sell fine-tuning on a closed model. A customer uploads plain customer support chats.", "prompt": "What do you do before shipping the tuned model?", "options": ["Ship it, since the data was harmless", "Run safety tests on the tuned model", "Refuse all fine-tuning requests"], "consequences": ["Harmless datasets weakened safety in the 2023 study.", "Benign data can still erode guardrails, so you check.", "You lose the business fine-tuning serves."], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "Even harmless data can weaken safety. Testing every tuned model is how a host uses its veto."}, {"id": "lens-curiosity", "kind": "info", "title": "Ten examples, under 20 cents", "body": "Researchers undid a chatbot's safety training with 10 examples, for less than $0.20.", "eyebrow": "Why it matters", "sources": [{"url": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2310.03693v1", "label": "Qi et al., Fine-tuning aligned language models compromises safety, Oct 2023"}]}, {"id": "rule", "kind": "microInsight", "text": "In AI, whoever fine-tunes a model last decides how it behaves.", "eyebrow": "The rule", "sources": [{"url": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2310.03693v1", "label": "Qi et al., Fine-tuning aligned language models compromises safety, Oct 2023"}, {"url": "https://www.theregister.com/2023/10/12/chatbot_defenses_dissolve/", "label": "The Register, chatbot defenses dissolve with fine-tuning, Oct 2023"}]}, {"id": "say-it", "kind": "sayIt", "takeaway": "In 2023, 10 examples and under $0.20 undid GPT-3.5 Turbo's guardrails. A host can test and block tuned models; open weights leave the last word to whoever holds them.", "sectionKey": "players"}]}$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$8c2e333f-c61d-5429-a81e-0d26fc604a0e$ml$::uuid, $ml$ai$ml$, $ml$The $600 instruction follower$ml$, 'LESSON', ARRAY[$ml$MICRO_LESSON$ml$, $ml$day-46$ml$, $ml$month-2$ml$, $ml$handwritten-v1$ml$, $ml$goal:career$ml$, $ml$level:beginner$ml$, $ml$level:intermediate$ml$, $ml$level:advanced$ml$]::text[], 5, now(), '{}'::jsonb, $ml${"id": "ai-d46-career", "title": "The $600 instruction follower", "exercises": [{"id": "open", "kind": "coldOpen", "kicker": "One number decides if a fine-tune worked: its win rate.", "eyebrow": "Day 46 · The numbers", "headline": "In March 2023, Stanford fine-tuned a 7-billion-parameter model for under $600. In blind tests it won 90 matchups to 89 against OpenAI's text-davinci-003.", "fullText": "Stanford's Alpaca started from Meta's LLaMA 7B. The team generated 52,000 instruction-following examples with text-davinci-003 for less than $500, then fine-tuned for 3 hours on eight A100 GPUs for less than $100. They judged it with a blind pairwise comparison against the OpenAI model.", "sources": [{"url": "https://crfm.stanford.edu/2023/03/13/alpaca.html", "label": "Stanford CRFM, Alpaca: a strong, replicable instruction-following model, Mar 2023"}]}, {"kind": "microInsight", "id": "winrate", "eyebrow": "The metric", "text": "A win rate is the share of head-to-head comparisons a model wins against a reference. A judge sees two answers and picks one.", "highlight": "Alpaca won 90 and lost 89: a win rate of about 50%, a tie.", "keyTerms": [{"term": "Win rate", "definition": "The share of head-to-head comparisons a model wins against a reference model."}], "sources": [{"url": "https://crfm.stanford.edu/2023/03/13/alpaca.html", "label": "Stanford CRFM, Alpaca: a strong, replicable instruction-following model, Mar 2023"}, {"url": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2404.04475", "label": "Dubois et al., Length-Controlled AlpacaEval, Apr 2024"}]}, {"kind": "microInsight", "id": "few", "eyebrow": "How much data", "text": "Meta's LIMA fine-tuned a 65B model on just 1,000 curated examples, with no RLHF.", "highlight": "Its answers were equal or preferred to GPT-4's in 43% of cases.", "sources": [{"url": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2305.11206", "label": "Zhou et al., LIMA: Less Is More for Alignment, May 2023"}]}, {"kind": "info", "id": "length", "eyebrow": "The catch", "title": "Judges like longer answers", "body": "Automatic judges tend to favor longer outputs, which can inflate a win rate. Controlling for length removes much of that bias.", "bullets": ["Length control lifted AlpacaEval's correlation with Chatbot Arena from 0.94 to 0.98."], "sources": [{"url": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2404.04475", "label": "Dubois et al., Length-Controlled AlpacaEval, Apr 2024"}]}, {"kind": "numberSense", "id": "ns-cost", "prompt": "Alpaca's data cost under $500 and its training under $100. About what was the total, in dollars?", "min": 0, "max": 2000, "value": 600, "unit": "$", "explanation": "Under $600 in all, for a model that tied text-davinci-003 in blind tests."}, {"kind": "multipleChoice", "id": "mc", "prompt": "Alpaca won 90 matchups and lost 89. What does that win rate say?", "options": ["It clearly beat the reference", "It roughly tied the reference", "It lost to the reference"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "A win rate near 50% means the two models were about even for these judges."}, {"kind": "spotFake", "id": "fake", "prompt": "One of these is false. Tap it.", "statements": ["LIMA was fine-tuned on about 1,000 examples.", "Automatic judges tend to favor longer answers.", "A higher win rate always means a better model."], "fakeIndex": 2, "explanation": "A win rate depends on the judge and the reference. Longer answers can inflate it without being better."}, {"id": "lens-career", "kind": "info", "title": "Ask what it won against", "body": "A win rate means little without its reference model and its judge. Ask about both before you quote it.", "eyebrow": "For your interview", "sources": [{"url": "https://crfm.stanford.edu/2023/03/13/alpaca.html", "label": "Stanford CRFM, Alpaca: a strong, replicable instruction-following model, Mar 2023"}, {"url": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2404.04475", "label": "Dubois et al., Length-Controlled AlpacaEval, Apr 2024"}]}, {"id": "rule", "kind": "microInsight", "text": "In fine-tuning, a win rate means something only next to its judge and its reference.", "eyebrow": "The rule", "sources": [{"url": "https://crfm.stanford.edu/2023/03/13/alpaca.html", "label": "Stanford CRFM, Alpaca: a strong, replicable instruction-following model, Mar 2023"}, {"url": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2404.04475", "label": "Dubois et al., Length-Controlled AlpacaEval, Apr 2024"}]}, {"id": "say-it", "kind": "sayIt", "takeaway": "A win rate counts head-to-head wins against a reference. Alpaca tied text-davinci-003 90 to 89 for under $600, but judges that favor long answers can inflate the number.", "sectionKey": "numbers"}]}$ml$::jsonb),
