nchmark in 2022.", "keyTerms": [{"term": "Few-shot", "definition": "A prompt that shows a few solved examples before the real task."}], "sources": [{"url": "https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2005.14165", "label": "Brown et al., Language models are few-shot learners, 2020"}, {"url": "https://ar5iv.arxiv.org/html/2201.11903", "label": "Wei et al. (Google), Chain-of-thought prompting elicits reasoning, 2022"}]}, {"kind": "info", "id": "window", "eyebrow": "The context window", "title": "Everything must fit in the window", "body": "The context window is the most text a model can take in at once. GPT-3's window, in 2020, was 2,048 tokens.", "bullets": ["Meta said its Llama 4 Scout could take 10 million tokens in April 2025."], "sources": [{"url": "https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2005.14165", "label": "Brown et al., Language models are few-shot learners, 2020"}, {"url": "https://ai.meta.com/blog/llama-4-multimodal-intelligence/", "label": "Meta, The Llama 4 herd, Apr 2025"}]}, {"kind": "sortSignal", "id": "sort", "prompt": "Does it change the prompt or the model?", "buckets": ["Changes the prompt", "Changes the model"], "items": [{"text": "Adding \"Let's think step by step\"", "bucket": 0}, {"text": "Showing eight worked examples", "bucket": 0}, {"text": "Fine-tuning on new data", "bucket": 1}, {"text": "Training on people's rankings", "bucket": 1}], "explanation": "Prompts change only the input. GPT-3's few-shot tests used no gradient updates or fine-tuning, while training changes the model."}, {"kind": "numberSense", "id": "ns-points", "prompt": "\"Let's think step by step\" took MultiArith accuracy from 17.7% to 78.7%. How many percentage points is that?", "min": 0, "max": 100, "value": 61, "unit": "points", "explanation": "78.7 minus 17.7 is 61 points, from five words and no new training."}, {"kind": "multipleChoice", "id": "review", "prompt": "Week 7 review: labelers preferred a 1.3B InstructGPT model over 175B GPT-3. What made the difference?", "options": ["A much longer context window", "A larger set of parameters", "Training on human feedback"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "InstructGPT was trained on human feedback and won despite more than 100 times fewer parameters. OpenAI noted such behavior is hard to get through prompt engineering alone."}, {"id": "lens-build_startup", "kind": "info", "title": "Test the prompt before the model", "body": "Five words lifted one score from 17.7% to 78.7%. Try better prompts before paying to train anything.", "eyebrow": "For founders", "sources": [{"url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/2205.11916", "label": "Kojima et al., Large Language Models are Zero-Shot Reasoners, 2022"}]}, {"id": "rule", "kind": "microInsight", "text": "In AI, the prompt is the cheapest lever: it changes the answer without changing the model.", "eyebrow": "The rule", "sources": [{"url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/2205.11916", "label": "Kojima et al., Large Language Models are Zero-Shot Reasoners, 2022"}, {"url": "https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2005.14165", "label": "Brown et al., Language models are few-shot learners, 2020"}]}, {"id": "say-it", "kind": "sayIt", "takeaway": "Prompt engineering changes what you send a model, not the model. Five words lifted one math score from 17.7% to 78.7%, and everything has to fit in the context window.", "sectionKey": "money"}]}$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$1d66fbe9-6b0e-5617-8e52-622b8721706a$ml$::uuid, $ml$ai$ml$, $ml$Five words that changed a math score$ml$, 'LESSON', ARRAY[$ml$MICRO_LESSON$ml$, $ml$day-49$ml$, $ml$month-2$ml$, $ml$handwritten-v1$ml$, $ml$goal:curiosity$ml$, $ml$level:beginner$ml$, $ml$level:intermediate$ml$, $ml$level:advanced$ml$]::text[], 5, now(), '{}'::jsonb, $ml${"id": "ai-d49-curiosity", "title": "Five words that changed a math score", "exercises": [{"id": "open", "kind": "coldOpen", "kicker": "Same model, same weights, better answers.", "eyebrow": "Day 49 · Prompts", "headline": "In 2022, adding \"Let's think step by step\" to a prompt lifted a model's math score from 17.7% to 78.7%.", "fullText": "Researchers tested OpenAI's InstructGPT model, text-davinci-002, on MultiArith, a set of arithmetic word problems. Asked directly, it got 17.7% right. Told to think step by step before answering, it got 78.7%. On the harder GSM8K set, accuracy rose from 10.4% to 40.7%. Nothing inside the model changed. Only the prompt did.", "sources": [{"url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/2205.11916", "label": "Kojima et al., Large Language Models are Zero-Shot Reasoners, 2022"}]}, {"kind": "microInsight", "id": "prompt", "eyebrow": "What it is", "text": "Prompt engineering is choosing the words, examples and order of what you send a model.", "highlight": "It changes the input, never the model itself.", "keyTerms": [{"term": "Prompt", "definition": "The text you give a model: instructions, examples and any material to work on."}], "sources": [{"url": "https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2005.14165", "label": "Brown et al., Language models are few-shot learners, 2020"}, {"url": "https://openai.com/index/instruction-following/", "label": "OpenAI, InstructGPT"}]}, {"kind": "microInsight", "id": "shots", "eyebrow": "Zero or few shots", "text": "A zero-shot prompt gives only an instruction. A few-shot prompt adds worked examples.", "highlight": "Eight worked examples put Google's PaLM 540B at the top of a math benchmark in 2022.", "keyTerms": [{"term": "Few-shot", "definition": "A prompt that shows a few solved examples before the real task."}], "sources": [{"url": "https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2005.14165", "label": "Brown et al., Language models are few-shot learners, 2020"}, {"url": "https://ar5iv.arxiv.org/html/2201.11903", "label": "Wei et al. (Google), Chain-of-thought prompting elicits reasoning, 2022"}]}, {"kind": "info", "id": "window", "eyebrow": "The context window", "title": "Everything must fit in the window", "body": "The context window is the most text a model can take in at once. GPT-3's window, in 2020, was 2,048 tokens.", "bullets": ["Meta said its Llama 4 Scout could take 10 million tokens in April 2025."], "sources": [{"url": "https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2005.14165", "label": "Brown et al., Language models are few-shot learners, 2020"}, {"url": "https://ai.meta.com/blog/llama-4-multimodal-intelligence/", "label": "Meta, The Llama 4 herd, Apr 2025"}]}, {"kind": "sortSignal", "id": "sort", "prompt": "Does it change the prompt or the model?", "buckets": ["Changes the prompt", "Changes the model"], "items": [{"text": "Adding \"Let's think step by step\"", "bucket": 0}, {"text": "Showing eight worked examples", "bucket": 0}, {"text": "Fine-tuning on new data", "bucket": 1}, {"text": "Training on people's rankings", "bucket": 1}], "explanation": "Prompts change only the input. GPT-3's few-shot tests used no gradient updates or fine-tuning, while training changes the model."}, {"kind": "numberSense", "id": "ns-points", "prompt": "\"Let's think step by step\" took MultiArith accuracy from 17.7% to 78.7%. How many percentage points is that?", "min": 0, "max": 100, "value": 61, "unit": "points", "explanation": "78.7 minus 17.7 is 61 points, from five words and no new training."}, {"kind": "multipleChoice", "id": "review", "prompt": "Week 7 review: labelers preferred a 1.3B InstructGPT model over 175B GPT-3. What made the difference?", "options": ["A much longer context window", "A larger set of parameters", "Training on human feedback"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "InstructGPT was trained on human feedback and won despite more than 100 times fewer parameters. OpenAI noted such behavior is hard to get through prompt engineering alone."}, {"id": "lens-curiosity", "kind": "info", "title": "How you ask shapes the answer", "body": "Told to think step by step, one model solved more than four times as many math problems. Asking well is a real skill.", "eyebrow": "Why it matters", "sources": [{"url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/2205.11916", "label": "Kojima et al., Large Language Models are Zero-Shot Reasoners, 2022"}]}, {"id": "rule", "kind": "microInsight", "text": "In AI, the prompt is the cheapest lever: it changes the answer without changing the model.", "eyebrow": "The rule", "sources": [{"url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/2205.11916", "label": "Kojima et al., Large Language Models are Zero-Shot Reasoners, 2022"}, {"url": "https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2005.14165", "label": "Brown et al., Language models are few-shot learners, 2020"}]}, {"id": "say-it", "kind": "sayIt", "takeaway": "Prompt engineering changes what you send a model, not the model. Five words lifted one math score from 17.7% to 78.7%, and everything has to fit in the context window.", "sectionKey": "basics"}]}$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$25478359-a5a2-5224-9dea-7a50ecf86c7d$ml$::uuid, $ml$ai$ml$, $ml$Paying for the same words twice$ml$, 'LESSON', ARRAY[$ml$MICRO_LESSON$ml$, $ml$day-50$ml$, $ml$month-2$ml$, $ml$handwritten-v1$ml$, $ml$goal:career$ml$, $ml$level:beginner$ml$, $ml$level:intermediate$ml$, $ml$level:advanced$ml$]::text[], 5, now(), '{}'::jsonb, $ml${"id": "ai-d50-career", "title": "Paying for the same words twice", "exercises": [{"id": "open", "kind": "coldOpen", "kicker": "A long prompt is billed every time you send it.", "eyebrow": "Day 50 · The money", "headline": "In August 2024, Anthropic said caching a long prompt could cut its cost by up to 90%.", "fullText": "Apps often send the same long instructions or documents with every request, and every token is billed each time. Prompt caching, which Anthropic launched on August 14, 2024, stores that repeated part. In its example of chatting with a 100,000-token book, the cost fell 90% and the wait for the first word of the answer went from 11.5 to 2.4 seconds.", "sources": [{"url": "https://claude.com/resources/articles/prompt-caching", "label": "Anthropic, Prompt caching with Claude"}, {"url": "https://www.maginative.com/article/anthropics-new-prompt-caching-feature-can-cut-costs-up-to-90/", "label": "Maginative, Anthropic prompt caching can cut costs up to 90%, Aug 2024"}]}, {"kind": "microInsight", "id": "meter", "eyebrow": "The meter", "text": "Model makers bill developers per token, and output costs more than input.", "highlight": "Claude Sonnet 4.6 costs $3 per million input tokens and $15 per million output tokens.", "keyTerms": [{"term": "Token", "definition": "A chunk of text, often part of a word, that models read and bill by."}], "sources": [{"url": "https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/about-claude/pricing", "label": "Anthropic, Claude API pricing, Nov 2026"}]}, {"kind": "microInsight", "id": "cache", "eyebrow": "The discount", "text": "Reading a cached prompt costs a tenth of the normal input price on most Claude models.", "highlight": "A five-minute cache write costs 1.25 times, so it pays off after one reuse.", "keyTerms": [{"term": "Prompt caching", "definition": "Storing the repeated start of a prompt so later requests read it cheaply."}], "sources": [{"url": "https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/about-claude/pricing", "label": "Anthropic, Claude API pricing, Nov 2026"}]}, {"kind": "info", "id": "margin", "eyebrow": "Where the margin sits", "title": "Every repeated token is a cost line", "body": "An app pays for its instructions on every call. Caching and a 50% batch discount turn prompt design into a margin decision.", "bullets": ["OpenAI made caching automatic in October 2024, at half price on prompts over 1,024 tokens."], "sources": [{"url": "https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/about-claude/pricing", "label": "Anthropic, Claude API pricing, Nov 2026"}, {"url": "https://openai.com/index/api-prompt-caching", "label": "OpenAI, Prompt caching in the API, Oct 2024"}]}, {"kind": "numberSense", "id": "ns-cache", "prompt": "A cache hit costs 0.1 times the input price. If input costs $3 per million tokens, what do a million cached tokens cost, in cents?", "min": 0, "max": 100, "value": 30, "unit": "cents", "explanation": "$3 times 0.1 is $0.30, the cache price Anthropic lists for Claude Sonnet 4.6."}, {"kind": "theCall", "id": "call", "situation": "Your support bot sends the same 20,000-token manual with every customer question.", "prompt": "What cuts the bill first?", "options": ["Move to the priciest model for better answers", "Cache the manual so repeats cost a tenth", "Send the manual twice so it reads closely"], "consequences": ["A pricier model raises the cost of every token.", "Cache reads cost 0.1 times input on most Claude models.", "Twice the tokens means twice the input bill."], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "The manual never changes, so it is the part to cache. The first write costs a little more, and every reuse after that costs far less."}, {"id": "lens-career", "kind": "info", "title": "Know what a token costs", "body": "Quote one real price, like $3 in and $15 out per million tokens. It shows you think about what each answer costs.", "eyebrow": "For your interview", "sources": [{"url": "https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/about-claude/pricing", "label": "Anthropic, Claude API pricing, Nov 2026"}]}, {"id": "rule", "kind": "microInsight", "text": "In AI apps, every token you resend is billed again, so reuse is where the margin hides.", "eyebrow": "The rule", "sources": [{"url": "https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/about-claude/pricing", "label": "Anthropic, Claude API pricing, Nov 2026"}, {"url": "https://claude.com/resources/articles/prompt-caching", "label": "Anthropic, Prompt caching with Claude"}]}, {"id": "say-it", "kind": "sayIt", "takeaway": "Developers pay per token, and output costs more than input. Caching bills repeated prompt text at a tenth of the price on most Claude models, which turns prompt design into a margin decision.", "sectionKey": "how_money_moves"}]}$ml$::jsonb),
