nthropic, Donating MCP and the Agentic AI Foundation, Dec 2025"}, {"url": "https://blog.modelcontextprotocol.io/posts/2025-12-09-mcp-joins-agentic-ai-foundation/", "label": "MCP blog, MCP joins the Agentic AI Foundation, Dec 2025"}]}, {"kind": "microInsight", "id": "owner", "eyebrow": "The owner", "text": "Whoever holds the data can still say no.", "highlight": "In 2025, Salesforce barred apps like Glean from storing or indexing Slack data long term.", "sources": [{"url": "https://cyprus-mail.com/2025/06/13/salesforce-blocks-ai-rivals-from-using-slack-data-according-to-reports", "label": "Reuters via Cyprus Mail, Salesforce blocks AI rivals from using Slack data, Jun 2025"}]}, {"kind": "info", "id": "leverage", "eyebrow": "Where leverage sits", "title": "Open pipes, closed taps", "body": "A shared standard makes connecting cheap for everyone. The data owner's terms still decide what flows.", "bullets": ["Slack's API terms now bar using that data in large language models."], "sources": [{"url": "https://natlawreview.com/article/salesforce-locks-down-slack-data-time-review-your-slack-api-terms", "label": "National Law Review, Salesforce locks down Slack data, Aug 2025"}, {"url": "https://anthropic.com/news/donating-the-model-context-protocol-and-establishing-of-the-agentic-ai-foundation", "label": "Anthropic, Donating MCP and the Agentic AI Foundation, Dec 2025"}]}, {"kind": "faceOff", "id": "face", "prompt": "Who can stop an AI assistant from indexing a company's Slack history?", "left": {"name": "The AI app maker", "note": "Builds the assistant"}, "right": {"name": "Salesforce, which owns Slack", "note": "Writes the API terms"}, "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "Salesforce changed Slack's terms in 2025, and apps like Glean could no longer index or store that data long term."}, {"kind": "multipleChoice", "id": "mc", "prompt": "What does MCP standardize?", "options": ["How models connect to tools and data", "How models are trained on new data", "How model makers price each token"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "MCP is a shared way to plug models into outside tools and data. ChatGPT, Gemini and Copilot all adopted it."}, {"kind": "theCall", "id": "call", "situation": "Your AI search startup indexes customers' Slack messages. Slack's terms just changed.", "prompt": "What do you do?", "options": ["Keep copying the data and hope nobody checks", "Wait for Salesforce to reverse the change", "Rebuild on data you are allowed to use"], "consequences": ["Breaking the terms risks losing access entirely.", "Your product stalls while you wait.", "The new terms bar persistent copies and indexes of Slack data."], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "The data owner sets the terms. A product built on data it cannot keep has to change its design."}, {"id": "lens-curiosity", "kind": "info", "title": "The model is not the whole story", "body": "An assistant only knows what it is allowed to read. Who controls that access shapes what AI can do for you.", "eyebrow": "Why it matters", "sources": [{"url": "https://cyprus-mail.com/2025/06/13/salesforce-blocks-ai-rivals-from-using-slack-data-according-to-reports", "label": "Reuters via Cyprus Mail, Salesforce blocks AI rivals from using Slack data, Jun 2025"}]}, {"id": "rule", "kind": "microInsight", "text": "In AI apps, power sits with whoever controls the data that fills the context window.", "eyebrow": "The rule", "sources": [{"url": "https://cyprus-mail.com/2025/06/13/salesforce-blocks-ai-rivals-from-using-slack-data-according-to-reports", "label": "Reuters via Cyprus Mail, Salesforce blocks AI rivals from using Slack data, Jun 2025"}, {"url": "https://anthropic.com/news/donating-the-model-context-protocol-and-establishing-of-the-agentic-ai-foundation", "label": "Anthropic, Donating MCP and the Agentic AI Foundation, Dec 2025"}]}, {"id": "say-it", "kind": "sayIt", "takeaway": "MCP made connecting models to data an open standard with more than 97 million monthly SDK downloads. Data owners like Salesforce still decide what flows, so control of the context is control of the product.", "sectionKey": "players"}]}$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$0c614e20-fb67-56a7-827d-516ef2c28933$ml$::uuid, $ml$ai$ml$, $ml$Claimed context versus real context$ml$, 'LESSON', ARRAY[$ml$MICRO_LESSON$ml$, $ml$day-52$ml$, $ml$month-2$ml$, $ml$handwritten-v1$ml$, $ml$goal:career$ml$, $ml$level:beginner$ml$, $ml$level:intermediate$ml$, $ml$level:advanced$ml$]::text[], 5, now(), '{}'::jsonb, $ml${"id": "ai-d52-career", "title": "Claimed context versus real context", "exercises": [{"id": "open", "kind": "coldOpen", "kicker": "The number on the box is not the number that works.", "eyebrow": "Day 52 · The numbers", "headline": "In 2024, NVIDIA researchers tested 17 models that claimed windows of 32,000 tokens or more. Only half held up at 32,000.", "fullText": "The RULER benchmark asks models to find, trace and combine information hidden in long inputs, across 13 tasks. GPT-4 claimed 128,000 tokens, but its effective length, how far it read while still performing well, was 64,000. Its score fell from 96.6% at 4,000 tokens to 81.2% at 128,000. The gap between the claimed and the effective window is what to check before trusting a long input.", "sources": [{"url": "https://arxiv.org/html/2404.06654v3", "label": "NVIDIA, RULER: What's the real context size of your long-context models?, 2024"}]}, {"kind": "microInsight", "id": "effective", "eyebrow": "The metric", "text": "Effective context length is how far a model can read while still performing well.", "highlight": "On RULER, GPT-4's was half of its claimed 128,000 tokens.", "keyTerms": [{"term": "Effective context length", "definition": "The input length a model handles well, often shorter than its advertised window."}], "sources": [{"url": "https://arxiv.org/html/2404.06654v3", "label": "NVIDIA, RULER: What's the real context size of your long-context models?, 2024"}]}, {"kind": "microInsight", "id": "middle", "eyebrow": "Position matters", "text": "Models find facts best at the start or end of a long input.", "highlight": "A 2023 study found accuracy drops when the key fact sits in the middle.", "sources": [{"url": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2307.03172v3", "label": "Liu et al., Lost in the Middle: how language models use long contexts, 2023"}]}, {"kind": "info", "id": "decision", "eyebrow": "The decision it drives", "title": "The number decides what you send", "body": "Past the effective length, more text can hurt. Teams trim inputs and put key facts first or last.", "bullets": ["The middle effect held even for models built for long inputs."], "sources": [{"url": "https://arxiv.org/html/2404.06654v3", "label": "NVIDIA, RULER: What's the real context size of your long-context models?, 2024"}, {"url": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2307.03172v3", "label": "Liu et al., Lost in the Middle: how language models use long contexts, 2023"}]}, {"kind": "numberSense", "id": "ns-eff", "prompt": "GPT-4 claimed a 128,000-token window. How many thousand tokens was its effective length on RULER?", "min": 0, "max": 200, "value": 64, "unit": "K", "explanation": "64,000 tokens, half the claim. Past that, its scores fell below the bar."}, {"kind": "multipleChoice", "id": "mc", "prompt": "Where in a long input is a model most likely to miss a key fact?", "options": ["At the very start", "In the middle", "At the very end"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "Performance is often highest when the fact is at the start or end, and drops in the middle."}, {"kind": "spotFake", "id": "fake", "prompt": "One of these is false. Tap it.", "statements": ["All 17 models held up at their claimed lengths.", "GPT-4 scored lower at 128,000 tokens than at 4,000.", "Facts in the middle of long inputs are found less often."], "fakeIndex": 0, "explanation": "Only half of the 17 models held up at 32,000 tokens, even though all claimed that much or more."}, {"id": "lens-career", "kind": "info", "title": "Ask for the effective length", "body": "GPT-4 claimed 128,000 tokens but held up to 64,000 on RULER. Asking for the tested number marks you as careful.", "eyebrow": "For your interview", "sources": [{"url": "https://arxiv.org/html/2404.06654v3", "label": "NVIDIA, RULER: What's the real context size of your long-context models?, 2024"}]}, {"id": "rule", "kind": "microInsight", "text": "In long context, trust the effective length, not the advertised one.", "eyebrow": "The rule", "sources": [{"url": "https://arxiv.org/html/2404.06654v3", "label": "NVIDIA, RULER: What's the real context size of your long-context models?, 2024"}]}, {"id": "say-it", "kind": "sayIt", "takeaway": "Only half of 17 models claiming 32,000 tokens or more held up at that length, and GPT-4 worked well to 64,000 of its 128,000. Effective length, and where facts sit, decide how much to send.", "sectionKey": "numbers"}]}$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$71b85fc7-6647-589b-804e-079cf9a086b4$ml$::uuid, $ml$ai$ml$, $ml$Claimed context versus real context$ml$, 'LESSON', ARRAY[$ml$MICRO_LESSON$ml$, $ml$day-52$ml$, $ml$month-2$ml$, $ml$handwritten-v1$ml$, $ml$goal:invest$ml$, $ml$level:beginner$ml$, $ml$level:intermediate$ml$, $ml$level:advanced$ml$]::text[], 5, now(), '{}'::jsonb, $ml${"id": "ai-d52-invest", "title": "Claimed context versus real context", "exercises": [{"id": "open", "kind": "coldOpen", "kicker": "The number on the box is not the number that works.", "eyebrow": "Day 52 · The numbers", "headline": "In 2024, NVIDIA researchers tested 17 models that claimed windows of 32,000 tokens or more. Only half held up at 32,000.", "fullText": "The RULER benchmark asks models to find, trace and combine information hidden in long inputs, across 13 tasks. GPT-4 claimed 128,000 tokens, but its effective length, how far it read while still performing well, was 64,000. Its score fell from 96.6% at 4,000 tokens to 81.2% at 128,000. The gap between the claimed and the effective window is what to check before trusting a long input.", "sources": [{"url": "https://arxiv.org/html/2404.06654v3", "label": "NVIDIA, RULER: What's the real context size of your long-context models?, 2024"}]}, {"kind": "microInsight", "id": "effective", "eyebrow": "The metric", "text": "Effective context length is how far a model can read while still performing well.", "highlight": "On RULER, GPT-4's was half of its claimed 128,000 tokens.", "keyTerms": [{"term": "Effective context length", "definition": "The input length a model handles well, often shorter than its advertised window."}], "sources": [{"url": "https://arxiv.org/html/2404.06654v3", "label": "NVIDIA, RULER: What's the real context size of your long-context models?, 2024"}]}, {"kind": "microInsight", "id": "middle", "eyebrow": "Position matters", "text": "Models find facts best at the start or end of a long input.", "highlight": "A 2023 study found accuracy drops when the key fact sits in the middle.", "sources": [{"url": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2307.03172v3", "label": "Liu et al., Lost in the Middle: how language models use long contexts, 2023"}]}, {"kind": "info", "id": "decision", "eyebrow": "The decision it drives", "title": "The number decides what you send", "body": "Past the effective length, more text can hurt. Teams trim inputs and put key facts first or last.", "bullets": ["The middle effect held even for models built for long inputs."], "sources": [{"url": "https://arxiv.org/html/2404.06654v3", "label": "NVIDIA, RULER: What's the real context size of your long-context models?, 2024"}, {"url": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2307.03172v3", "label": "Liu et al., Lost in the Middle: how language models use long contexts, 2023"}]}, {"kind": "numberSense", "id": "ns-eff", "prompt": "GPT-4 claimed a 128,000-token window. How many thousand tokens was its effective length on RULER?", "min": 0, "max": 200, "value": 64, "unit": "K", "explanation": "64,000 tokens, half the claim. Past that, its scores fell below the bar."}, {"kind": "multipleChoice", "id": "mc", "prompt": "Where in a long input is a model most likely to miss a key fact?", "options": ["At the very start", "In the middle", "At the very end"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "Performance is often highest when the fact is at the start or end, and drops in the middle."}, {"kind": "spotFake", "id": "fake", "prompt": "One of these is false. Tap it.", "statements": ["All 17 models held up at their claimed lengths.", "GPT-4 scored lower at 128,000 tokens than at 4,000.", "Facts in the middle of long inputs are found less often."], "fakeIndex": 0, "explanation": "Only half of the 17 models held up at 32,000 tokens, even though all claimed that much or more."}, {"id": "lens-invest", "kind": "info", "title": "Discount the box number", "body": "Only half of 17 long-context models held up at 32,000 tokens. Judge products by tested length, not advertised length.", "eyebrow": "For investors", "sources": [{"url": "https://arxiv.org/html/2404.06654v3", "label": "NVIDIA, RULER: What's the real context size of your long-context models?, 2024"}]}, {"id": "rule", "kind": "microInsight", "text": "In long context, trust the effective length, not the advertised one.", "eyebrow": "The rule", "sources": [{"url": "https://arxiv.org/html/2404.06654v3", "label": "NVIDIA, RULER: What's the real context size of your long-context models?, 2024"}]}, {"id": "say-it", "kind": "sayIt", "takeaway": "Only half of 17 models claiming 32,000 tokens or more held up at that length, and GPT-4 worked well to 64,000 of its 128,000. Effective length, and where facts sit, decide how much to send.", "sectionKey": "numbers"}]}$ml$::jsonb),
