he key fact sits in the middle.", "sources": [{"url": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2307.03172v3", "label": "Liu et al., Lost in the Middle: how language models use long contexts, 2023"}]}, {"kind": "info", "id": "decision", "eyebrow": "The decision it drives", "title": "The number decides what you send", "body": "Past the effective length, more text can hurt. Teams trim inputs and put key facts first or last.", "bullets": ["The middle effect held even for models built for long inputs."], "sources": [{"url": "https://arxiv.org/html/2404.06654v3", "label": "NVIDIA, RULER: What's the real context size of your long-context models?, 2024"}, {"url": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2307.03172v3", "label": "Liu et al., Lost in the Middle: how language models use long contexts, 2023"}]}, {"kind": "numberSense", "id": "ns-eff", "prompt": "GPT-4 claimed a 128,000-token window. How many thousand tokens was its effective length on RULER?", "min": 0, "max": 200, "value": 64, "unit": "K", "explanation": "64,000 tokens, half the claim. Past that, its scores fell below the bar."}, {"kind": "multipleChoice", "id": "mc", "prompt": "Where in a long input is a model most likely to miss a key fact?", "options": ["At the very start", "In the middle", "At the very end"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "Performance is often highest when the fact is at the start or end, and drops in the middle."}, {"kind": "spotFake", "id": "fake", "prompt": "One of these is false. Tap it.", "statements": ["All 17 models held up at their claimed lengths.", "GPT-4 scored lower at 128,000 tokens than at 4,000.", "Facts in the middle of long inputs are found less often."], "fakeIndex": 0, "explanation": "Only half of the 17 models held up at 32,000 tokens, even though all claimed that much or more."}, {"id": "lens-build_startup", "kind": "info", "title": "Long inputs still fail", "body": "Models still lose facts in long inputs. Tools that pick the right passages fill a gap that big windows leave open.", "eyebrow": "For founders", "sources": [{"url": "https://arxiv.org/html/2404.06654v3", "label": "NVIDIA, RULER: What's the real context size of your long-context models?, 2024"}, {"url": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2307.03172v3", "label": "Liu et al., Lost in the Middle: how language models use long contexts, 2023"}]}, {"id": "rule", "kind": "microInsight", "text": "In long context, trust the effective length, not the advertised one.", "eyebrow": "The rule", "sources": [{"url": "https://arxiv.org/html/2404.06654v3", "label": "NVIDIA, RULER: What's the real context size of your long-context models?, 2024"}]}, {"id": "say-it", "kind": "sayIt", "takeaway": "Only half of 17 models claiming 32,000 tokens or more held up at that length, and GPT-4 worked well to 64,000 of its 128,000. Effective length, and where facts sit, decide how much to send.", "sectionKey": "why_now"}]}$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$6db61501-62bc-5581-b670-6daf9739d566$ml$::uuid, $ml$ai$ml$, $ml$Claimed context versus real context$ml$, 'LESSON', ARRAY[$ml$MICRO_LESSON$ml$, $ml$day-52$ml$, $ml$month-2$ml$, $ml$handwritten-v1$ml$, $ml$goal:curiosity$ml$, $ml$level:beginner$ml$, $ml$level:intermediate$ml$, $ml$level:advanced$ml$]::text[], 5, now(), '{}'::jsonb, $ml${"id": "ai-d52-curiosity", "title": "Claimed context versus real context", "exercises": [{"id": "open", "kind": "coldOpen", "kicker": "The number on the box is not the number that works.", "eyebrow": "Day 52 · The numbers", "headline": "In 2024, NVIDIA researchers tested 17 models that claimed windows of 32,000 tokens or more. Only half held up at 32,000.", "fullText": "The RULER benchmark asks models to find, trace and combine information hidden in long inputs, across 13 tasks. GPT-4 claimed 128,000 tokens, but its effective length, how far it read while still performing well, was 64,000. Its score fell from 96.6% at 4,000 tokens to 81.2% at 128,000. The gap between the claimed and the effective window is what to check before trusting a long input.", "sources": [{"url": "https://arxiv.org/html/2404.06654v3", "label": "NVIDIA, RULER: What's the real context size of your long-context models?, 2024"}]}, {"kind": "microInsight", "id": "effective", "eyebrow": "The metric", "text": "Effective context length is how far a model can read while still performing well.", "highlight": "On RULER, GPT-4's was half of its claimed 128,000 tokens.", "keyTerms": [{"term": "Effective context length", "definition": "The input length a model handles well, often shorter than its advertised window."}], "sources": [{"url": "https://arxiv.org/html/2404.06654v3", "label": "NVIDIA, RULER: What's the real context size of your long-context models?, 2024"}]}, {"kind": "microInsight", "id": "middle", "eyebrow": "Position matters", "text": "Models find facts best at the start or end of a long input.", "highlight": "A 2023 study found accuracy drops when the key fact sits in the middle.", "sources": [{"url": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2307.03172v3", "label": "Liu et al., Lost in the Middle: how language models use long contexts, 2023"}]}, {"kind": "info", "id": "decision", "eyebrow": "The decision it drives", "title": "The number decides what you send", "body": "Past the effective length, more text can hurt. Teams trim inputs and put key facts first or last.", "bullets": ["The middle effect held even for models built for long inputs."], "sources": [{"url": "https://arxiv.org/html/2404.06654v3", "label": "NVIDIA, RULER: What's the real context size of your long-context models?, 2024"}, {"url": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2307.03172v3", "label": "Liu et al., Lost in the Middle: how language models use long contexts, 2023"}]}, {"kind": "numberSense", "id": "ns-eff", "prompt": "GPT-4 claimed a 128,000-token window. How many thousand tokens was its effective length on RULER?", "min": 0, "max": 200, "value": 64, "unit": "K", "explanation": "64,000 tokens, half the claim. Past that, its scores fell below the bar."}, {"kind": "multipleChoice", "id": "mc", "prompt": "Where in a long input is a model most likely to miss a key fact?", "options": ["At the very start", "In the middle", "At the very end"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "Performance is often highest when the fact is at the start or end, and drops in the middle."}, {"kind": "spotFake", "id": "fake", "prompt": "One of these is false. Tap it.", "statements": ["All 17 models held up at their claimed lengths.", "GPT-4 scored lower at 128,000 tokens than at 4,000.", "Facts in the middle of long inputs are found less often."], "fakeIndex": 0, "explanation": "Only half of the 17 models held up at 32,000 tokens, even though all claimed that much or more."}, {"id": "lens-curiosity", "kind": "info", "title": "Big memory, spotty recall", "body": "A model can accept a huge input and still miss what sits in the middle. A bigger window is not better reading.", "eyebrow": "Why it matters", "sources": [{"url": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2307.03172v3", "label": "Liu et al., Lost in the Middle: how language models use long contexts, 2023"}]}, {"id": "rule", "kind": "microInsight", "text": "In long context, trust the effective length, not the advertised one.", "eyebrow": "The rule", "sources": [{"url": "https://arxiv.org/html/2404.06654v3", "label": "NVIDIA, RULER: What's the real context size of your long-context models?, 2024"}]}, {"id": "say-it", "kind": "sayIt", "takeaway": "Only half of 17 models claiming 32,000 tokens or more held up at that length, and GPT-4 worked well to 64,000 of its 128,000. Effective length, and where facts sit, decide how much to send.", "sectionKey": "numbers"}]}$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$a1dd9fbb-b5b8-5f1e-bbe9-90fd0f650c2d$ml$::uuid, $ml$ai$ml$, $ml$When the data gives the orders$ml$, 'LESSON', ARRAY[$ml$MICRO_LESSON$ml$, $ml$day-53$ml$, $ml$month-2$ml$, $ml$handwritten-v1$ml$, $ml$goal:career$ml$, $ml$level:beginner$ml$, $ml$level:intermediate$ml$, $ml$level:advanced$ml$]::text[], 5, now(), '{}'::jsonb, $ml${"id": "ai-d53-career", "title": "When the data gives the orders", "exercises": [{"id": "open", "kind": "coldOpen", "kicker": "One email could make an assistant leak secrets.", "eyebrow": "Day 53 · The rules", "headline": "In June 2025, Microsoft fixed EchoLeak, a flaw in Microsoft 365 Copilot rated 9.3 out of 10 for severity.", "fullText": "Researchers at Aim Security found that an attacker could send an employee an innocent-looking email with hidden instructions. When the employee later asked Copilot a work question, Copilot read the email along with internal data and could leak that data, with no click from anyone. Microsoft fixed the flaw, tracked as CVE-2025-32711, in June 2025 and said customers did not need to act. There was no evidence it had been used in the wild.", "sources": [{"url": "https://thehackernews.com/2025/06/zero-click-ai-vulnerability-exposes.html", "label": "The Hacker News, zero-click AI flaw in Microsoft 365 Copilot, Jun 2025"}]}, {"kind": "microInsight", "id": "injection", "eyebrow": "The risk", "text": "Prompt injection hides instructions inside content a model reads, like an email or a web page.", "highlight": "OWASP ranks it first among risks to AI applications in its 2025 list.", "keyTerms": [{"term": "Prompt injection", "definition": "Instructions hidden in content a model reads, which it may follow as orders."}], "sources": [{"url": "https://thehackernews.com/2025/06/zero-click-ai-vulnerability-exposes.html", "label": "The Hacker News, zero-click AI flaw in Microsoft 365 Copilot, Jun 2025"}, {"url": "https://www.hackerone.com/blog/owasp-top-10-llms-2025-how-genai-risks-are-evolving", "label": "HackerOne, the OWASP Top 10 for LLMs 2025"}]}, {"kind": "microInsight", "id": "why", "eyebrow": "Why it persists", "text": "Models keep no firm boundary between instructions and data inside a prompt.", "highlight": "The UK's cyber security agency warned in December 2025 it may never be fully fixed.", "sources": [{"url": "https://www.techradar.com/pro/security/prompt-injection-attacks-might-never-be-properly-mitigated-uk-ncsc-warns", "label": "TechRadar, UK NCSC warns prompt injection may never be fully mitigated, Dec 2025"}, {"url": "https://www.malwarebytes.com/blog/news/2025/12/prompt-injection-is-a-problem-that-may-never-be-fixed-warns-ncsc", "label": "Malwarebytes, prompt injection may never be fixed, warns NCSC, Dec 2025"}]}, {"kind": "info", "id": "design", "eyebrow": "The rule", "title": "Limit what a fooled model can do", "body": "The NCSC calls models confusable deputies. Design so that a hijacked answer cannot do much damage.", "bullets": ["OWASP's 2025 list added system prompt leakage as a new risk."], "sources": [{"url": "https://www.techradar.com/pro/security/prompt-injection-attacks-might-never-be-properly-mitigated-uk-ncsc-warns", "label": "TechRadar, UK NCSC warns prompt injection may never be fully mitigated, Dec 2025"}, {"url": "https://www.hackerone.com/blog/owasp-top-10-llms-2025-how-genai-risks-are-evolving", "label": "HackerOne, the OWASP Top 10 for LLMs 2025"}]}, {"kind": "buildChain", "id": "chain", "prompt": "Put the EchoLeak attack in order.", "steps": ["An attacker emails hidden instructions", "The employee asks Copilot a work question", "Copilot reads the email with internal data", "Internal data leaks without a click"], "explanation": "The employee never opened anything risky. Copilot pulled the email into its context and followed it."}, {"kind": "multipleChoice", "id": "mc", "prompt": "Why is prompt injection so hard to fix?", "options": ["Models can treat any text as an instruction", "Attackers need physical access to servers", "It only affects old, unpatched models"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "Models do not enforce a boundary between instructions and data, so any text they read could steer them."}, {"kind": "theCall", "id": "call", "situation": "Your AI assistant reads customers' inboxes and can send email for them.", "prompt": "Which safeguard matters most?", "options": ["A system prompt telling it to ignore attacks", "A bigger model that is harder to fool", "Approval before it sends or shares anything"], "consequences": ["Orders and data share one channel, so that line can be overridden.", "No model keeps a firm line between data and orders.", "A hijacked answer can do little without a human yes."], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "The NCSC advice is to limit what a compromised output can do, because the model itself can be confused."}, {"id": "lens-career", "kind": "info", "title": "Name LLM01", "body": "Prompt injection tops OWASP's 2025 list of AI application risks. Knowing that, and why, helps in any AI security talk.", "eyebrow": "For your interview", "sources": [{"url": "https://www.hackerone.com/blog/owasp-top-10-llms-2025-how-genai-risks-are-evolving", "label": "HackerOne, the OWASP Top 10 for LLMs 2025"}]}, {"id": "rule", "kind": "microInsight", "text": "In AI apps, treat everything in the context window as untrusted, and limit what a fooled model can do.", "eyebrow": "The rule", "sources": [{"url": "https://www.techradar.com/pro/security/prompt-injection-attacks-might-never-be-properly-mitigated-uk-ncsc-warns", "label": "TechRadar, UK NCSC warns prompt injection may never be fully mitigated, Dec 2025"}, {"url": "https://thehackernews.com/2025/06/zero-click-ai-vulnerability-exposes.html", "label": "The Hacker News, zero-click AI flaw in Microsoft 365 Copilot, Jun 2025"}]}, {"id": "say-it", "kind": "sayIt", "takeaway": "Prompt injection hides orders in content a model reads. EchoLeak scored 9.3 out of 10, OWASP ranks the risk first, and the NCSC says it may never be fully fixed, so limit what a fooled model can do.", "sectionKey": "risk"}]}$ml$::jsonb),
