eCall", "id": "call", "situation": "Your coding agent slips after hours of work. Its context window is almost full.", "prompt": "What do you try?", "options": ["Summarize the history and start a fresh window", "Buy the model with the biggest window", "Paste in the full history again to remind it"], "consequences": ["Compaction keeps what matters and drops the rest.", "A bigger window still gets less reliable as it fills.", "More tokens make it less reliable, not more."], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "Anthropic calls this compaction: summarize a conversation near the limit and continue in a new window."}, {"id": "lens-curiosity", "kind": "info", "title": "Less can be more", "body": "Feeding a model more text can make it worse. The skill now is choosing what to leave out.", "eyebrow": "Why it matters", "sources": [{"url": "https://trychroma.com/research/context-rot", "label": "Chroma, Context Rot technical report, Jul 2025"}]}, {"id": "rule", "kind": "microInsight", "text": "In AI, the frontier skill is not wording the prompt but choosing what the model sees.", "eyebrow": "The rule", "sources": [{"url": "https://anthropic.com/research/effective-context-engineering-for-ai-agents", "label": "Anthropic, Effective context engineering for AI agents, Sep 2025"}, {"url": "https://trychroma.com/research/context-rot", "label": "Chroma, Context Rot technical report, Jul 2025"}]}, {"id": "say-it", "kind": "sayIt", "takeaway": "Prompt engineer faded as a job title by 2025, but the skill moved into context engineering. Longer inputs make models less reliable, so the work is choosing the smallest set of tokens that matters.", "sectionKey": "frontier"}]}$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$a234b476-f069-50d4-8895-d9539c2077cc$ml$::uuid, $ml$ai$ml$, $ml$Open weights, open source, closed$ml$, 'LESSON', ARRAY[$ml$MICRO_LESSON$ml$, $ml$day-55$ml$, $ml$month-2$ml$, $ml$handwritten-v1$ml$, $ml$goal:career$ml$, $ml$level:beginner$ml$, $ml$level:intermediate$ml$, $ml$level:advanced$ml$]::text[], 5, now(), '{}'::jsonb, $ml${"id": "ai-d55-career", "title": "Open weights, open source, closed", "exercises": [{"id": "open", "kind": "coldOpen", "kicker": "You can download a model without seeing how it was made.", "eyebrow": "Day 55 · Open vs closed", "headline": "In August 2025, OpenAI released gpt-oss, its first open-weight language models since GPT-2.", "fullText": "On August 5, 2025, OpenAI put two models on Hugging Face for anyone to download, under the Apache 2.0 license. The larger, gpt-oss-120b, has 117 billion parameters and runs within 80GB of memory, so a single 80GB GPU can host it. OpenAI described the training data only in general terms: mostly English text on science, coding and general knowledge.", "sources": [{"url": "https://openai.com/index/introducing-gpt-oss/", "label": "OpenAI, Introducing gpt-oss, Aug 2025"}, {"url": "https://the-decoder.com/openai-releases-its-first-open-weight-language-models-since-gpt-2-with-gpt-oss/", "label": "The Decoder, OpenAI releases its first open-weight models since GPT-2, Aug 2025"}]}, {"kind": "microInsight", "id": "weights", "eyebrow": "Open weights", "text": "An open-weight model lets you download its trained parameters and run it on your own machines.", "highlight": "The smaller gpt-oss-20b runs within 16GB of memory.", "keyTerms": [{"term": "Weights", "definition": "The numbers a model learned in training, which let you run it."}], "sources": [{"url": "https://openai.com/index/introducing-gpt-oss/", "label": "OpenAI, Introducing gpt-oss, Aug 2025"}]}, {"kind": "microInsight", "id": "source", "eyebrow": "Open source", "text": "Open source asks for more: details of the training data, the full code and the weights, so others can rebuild the model.", "highlight": "By the test the Open Source Initiative set in October 2024, Meta's Llama falls short.", "sources": [{"url": "https://siliconangle.com/2024/10/28/osi-clarifies-makes-ai-systems-open-source-open-models-fall-short/", "label": "SiliconANGLE, OSI defines open source AI and open models fall short, Oct 2024"}]}, {"kind": "info", "id": "closed", "eyebrow": "Closed", "title": "Closed models are rented, not owned", "body": "You reach a closed model through an app or an API, and the weights stay with the lab. Meta launched Muse Spark this way in April 2026.", "bullets": ["Muse Spark had no download, only the Meta AI app and an API preview for partners."], "sources": [{"url": "https://www.artificialintelligence-news.com/news/meta-muse-spark-ai-model-open-source/", "label": "AI News, Meta launches Muse Spark as a proprietary model, Apr 2026"}, {"url": "https://www.deeplearning.ai/the-batch/with-muse-spark-meta-pivots-away-from-its-open-weights-llama-strategy", "label": "The Batch (DeepLearning.AI), With Muse Spark, Meta pivots away from open weights, Apr 2026"}]}, {"kind": "sortSignal", "id": "sort", "prompt": "Can you download it, or only rent access?", "buckets": ["You can download it", "You rent access"], "items": [{"text": "gpt-oss-120b", "bucket": 0}, {"text": "Llama 4 Maverick", "bucket": 0}, {"text": "Mistral Large 3", "bucket": 0}, {"text": "Muse Spark at its April 2026 launch", "bucket": 1}, {"text": "OpenAI's GPT-5.5", "bucket": 1}], "explanation": "gpt-oss, Llama 4 and Mistral Large 3 have downloadable weights. Muse Spark launched app and API only, and GPT-5.5 is a closed model."}, {"kind": "multipleChoice", "id": "mc", "prompt": "What does the Open Source Initiative ask for beyond the weights?", "options": ["A paid license for big commercial users", "Training data details and the code", "A model small enough for one GPU"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "Open source AI needs data details, code and weights, so skilled people could recreate the system."}, {"kind": "theCall", "id": "call", "situation": "You run IT at a law firm. Client files must never leave your own servers.", "prompt": "Which kind of model fits?", "options": ["A closed model through a public API", "An open-weight model on your own hardware", "No AI until every model is open source"], "consequences": ["Every prompt would travel to the lab's servers.", "gpt-oss-120b runs within 80GB of memory on your own machine.", "You would wait while others use what exists today."], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "Open weights let you run a model where your data lives. That is often the deciding reason to pick one."}, {"id": "lens-career", "kind": "info", "title": "Use the three words precisely", "body": "Say open weights, open source or closed, and mean it. By the 2024 definition, calling Llama open source is wrong.", "eyebrow": "For your interview", "sources": [{"url": "https://siliconangle.com/2024/10/28/osi-clarifies-makes-ai-systems-open-source-open-models-fall-short/", "label": "SiliconANGLE, OSI defines open source AI and open models fall short, Oct 2024"}]}, {"id": "rule", "kind": "microInsight", "text": "In AI, open weights let you run a model; open source lets you rebuild it.", "eyebrow": "The rule", "sources": [{"url": "https://siliconangle.com/2024/10/28/osi-clarifies-makes-ai-systems-open-source-open-models-fall-short/", "label": "SiliconANGLE, OSI defines open source AI and open models fall short, Oct 2024"}, {"url": "https://openai.com/index/introducing-gpt-oss/", "label": "OpenAI, Introducing gpt-oss, Aug 2025"}]}, {"id": "say-it", "kind": "sayIt", "takeaway": "Open weights mean you can download and run a model, like OpenAI's gpt-oss from August 2025. Open source also needs data details and code. Closed models stay on the lab's servers.", "sectionKey": "players"}]}$ml$::jsonb),
