2-Chat 70B, using one GPU and under $200 per model.", "highlight": "Its refusal rate fell below 1% on two refusal tests.", "sources": [{"url": "https://huggingface.co/papers/2310.20624", "label": "Lermen et al., LoRA fine-tuning undoes safety training in Llama 2-Chat 70B, 2023"}]}, {"kind": "info", "id": "test", "eyebrow": "The new test", "title": "OpenAI tested the worst case first", "body": "Before gpt-oss shipped, OpenAI tuned it to drop refusals on biology and cyber, as an attacker might. It still fell short of High capability.", "bullets": ["Three independent expert groups reviewed the method."], "sources": [{"url": "https://openai.com/index/introducing-gpt-oss/", "label": "OpenAI, Introducing gpt-oss, Aug 2025"}]}, {"kind": "faceOff", "id": "face", "prompt": "Which can its maker still pull back after release?", "left": {"name": "A closed model", "note": "Runs only on the lab's servers"}, "right": {"name": "An open-weight model", "note": "Weights downloaded by anyone"}, "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "A lab can stop serving a closed model. Downloaded weights can be shared on, and access cannot be rescinded."}, {"kind": "numberSense", "id": "ns-cost", "prompt": "Researchers undid Llama 2-Chat 13B's safety training for under how many dollars?", "min": 0, "max": 1000, "value": 200, "unit": "$", "explanation": "Less than $200. A second team undid the 70B model for under $200 on one GPU."}, {"kind": "theCall", "id": "call", "situation": "You run a lab about to publish a model's weights.", "prompt": "What should come first?", "options": ["Test it with its safety stripped out", "Release it and patch problems later", "Trust its built-in refusals to hold"], "consequences": ["That is the test OpenAI ran before gpt-oss.", "You cannot patch copies on other people's machines.", "Safety training was undone for under $200 in 2023."], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "A release is permanent. Testing the worst case first is the only chance to catch it."}, {"id": "lens-curiosity", "kind": "info", "title": "Sharing only goes one way", "body": "Once people download weights they can share them by other means. A released model cannot be un-released.", "eyebrow": "Why it matters", "sources": [{"url": "https://www.ntia.gov/programs-and-initiatives/artificial-intelligence/open-model-weights-report/background", "label": "NTIA, open model weights report, background"}]}, {"id": "rule", "kind": "microInsight", "text": "In open AI, a release is permanent, so test the worst case before you ship.", "eyebrow": "The rule", "sources": [{"url": "https://www.ntia.gov/programs-and-initiatives/artificial-intelligence/open-model-weights-report/background", "label": "NTIA, open model weights report, background"}, {"url": "https://openai.com/index/introducing-gpt-oss/", "label": "OpenAI, Introducing gpt-oss, Aug 2025"}]}, {"id": "say-it", "kind": "sayIt", "takeaway": "Meta released Muse Spark Open in October 2026, and released weights cannot be recalled. Safety training was undone for under $200 in 2023, so OpenAI tested a worst case fine-tune of gpt-oss before release.", "sectionKey": "frontier"}]}$ml$::jsonb)
INSERT INTO slides (id, stack_id, slide_number, title, body, sources) VALUES
($ml$3ccc874d-4021-5573-a700-66bc4029f468$ml$::uuid, $ml$b4618f26-6fec-5b0d-9967-d048815168f3$ml$::uuid, 1, $ml$Day 31 · The model$ml$, $ml$In June 2018, OpenAI's first GPT trained for one month on 8 GPUs. OpenAI trained a Transformer on a few thousand books, about 5GB of text, to predict the next word. Then it fine-tuned that same model on much smaller labeled datasets, one for each task. The costly first step only had to be done once. The result set new records on a range of language tasks.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://openai.com/index/language-unsupervised/", "label": "OpenAI, Improving language understanding with unsupervised learning, Jun 2018"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$b9db20c9-87b6-595e-a7cd-6bacdfc31552$ml$::uuid, $ml$b4618f26-6fec-5b0d-9967-d048815168f3$ml$::uuid, 2, $ml$G is for generative$ml$, $ml$Generative means the model writes. It picks the most likely next word, then the next. GPT-2 had one objective: predict the next word, given all the words before it.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://www.ibm.com/think/topics/gpt", "label": "IBM, What is GPT (generative pretrained transformer)?"}, {"url": "https://openai.com/index/better-language-models/", "label": "OpenAI, Better language models and their implications, Feb 2019"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$5ac1df43-f2c9-5a29-8201-89b50ef4859c$ml$::uuid, $ml$b4618f26-6fec-5b0d-9967-d048815168f3$ml$::uuid, 3, $ml$P is for pre-trained$ml$, $ml$Pre-trained means it first learns from raw text with no labels. The same model is then fine-tuned on much smaller labeled datasets.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://www.ibm.com/think/topics/gpt", "label": "IBM, What is GPT (generative pretrained transformer)?"}, {"url": "https://openai.com/index/language-unsupervised/", "label": "OpenAI, Improving language understanding with unsupervised learning, Jun 2018"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$28af6fd6-9f7f-5590-a72a-3c0e5670001d$ml$::uuid, $ml$b4618f26-6fec-5b0d-9967-d048815168f3$ml$::uuid, 4, $ml$GPT keeps half of the 2017 design$ml$, $ml$The 2017 Transformer had an encoder and a decoder, six layers each. GPT trains the decoder alone to predict the next word. The decoder's mask lets each prediction see only the words before it.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://arxiv.org/html/1706.03762v7", "label": "Vaswani et al., Attention Is All You Need, 2017"}, {"url": "https://huggingface.co/docs/transformers/model_doc/openai-gpt", "label": "Hugging Face Transformers docs, OpenAI GPT"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$7308a2df-20db-5c67-8f8b-5ac1f1bc43e4$ml$::uuid, $ml$b4618f26-6fec-5b0d-9967-d048815168f3$ml$::uuid, 5, $ml$Spell out the acronym$ml$, $ml$Generative, pre-trained, Transformer. Explaining each word in one line shows you know how these models work.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://www.ibm.com/think/topics/gpt", "label": "IBM, What is GPT (generative pretrained transformer)?"}, {"url": "https://huggingface.co/docs/transformers/model_doc/openai-gpt", "label": "Hugging Face Transformers docs, OpenAI GPT"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$e08403cd-25d2-5f64-8a85-c94837e6403f$ml$::uuid, $ml$b4618f26-6fec-5b0d-9967-d048815168f3$ml$::uuid, 6, $ml$The rule$ml$, $ml$A GPT is a Transformer decoder pre-trained to predict the next word.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://huggingface.co/docs/transformers/model_doc/openai-gpt", "label": "Hugging Face Transformers docs, OpenAI GPT"}, {"url": "https://openai.com/index/better-language-models/", "label": "OpenAI, Better language models and their implications, Feb 2019"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$aee555c0-49d0-518b-9394-e3e97a564cb3$ml$::uuid, $ml$785fb71a-d9d0-5632-82a7-883ddfb08771$ml$::uuid, 1, $ml$Day 31 · The model$ml$, $ml$In June 2018, OpenAI's first GPT trained for one month on 8 GPUs. OpenAI trained a Transformer on a few thousand books, about 5GB of text, to predict the next word. Then it fine-tuned that same model on much smaller labeled datasets, one for each task. The costly first step only had to be done once. The result set new records on a range of language tasks.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://openai.com/index/language-unsupervised/", "label": "OpenAI, Improving language understanding with unsupervised learning, Jun 2018"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$8fa98cc8-90b4-5e2d-a258-030ceb915e0b$ml$::uuid, $ml$785fb71a-d9d0-5632-82a7-883ddfb08771$ml$::uuid, 2, $ml$G is for generative$ml$, $ml$Generative means the model writes. It picks the most likely next word, then the next. GPT-2 had one objective: predict the next word, given all the words before it.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://www.ibm.com/think/topics/gpt", "label": "IBM, What is GPT (generative pretrained transformer)?"}, {"url": "https://openai.com/index/better-language-models/", "label": "OpenAI, Better language models and their implications, Feb 2019"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$e2acc6a3-f71d-51fd-86bc-c1c35c0cbcc5$ml$::uuid, $ml$785fb71a-d9d0-5632-82a7-883ddfb08771$ml$::uuid, 3, $ml$P is for pre-trained$ml$, $ml$Pre-trained means it first learns from raw text with no labels. The same model is then fine-tuned on much smaller labeled datasets.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://www.ibm.com/think/topics/gpt", "label": "IBM, What is GPT (generative pretrained transformer)?"}, {"url": "https://openai.com/index/language-unsupervised/", "label": "OpenAI, Improving language understanding with unsupervised learning, Jun 2018"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$320fa3ac-46e6-5556-a069-dc1b4ca7518e$ml$::uuid, $ml$785fb71a-d9d0-5632-82a7-883ddfb08771$ml$::uuid, 4, $ml$GPT keeps half of the 2017 design$ml$, $ml$The 2017 Transformer had an encoder and a decoder, six layers each. GPT trains the decoder alone to predict the next word. The decoder's mask lets each prediction see only the words before it.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://arxiv.org/html/1706.03762v7", "label": "Vaswani et al., Attention Is All You Need, 2017"}, {"url": "https://huggingface.co/docs/transformers/model_doc/openai-gpt", "label": "Hugging Face Transformers docs, OpenAI GPT"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$1b9c4adc-ed88-52ce-b07e-4bfdd74b6bf4$ml$::uuid, $ml$785fb71a-d9d0-5632-82a7-883ddfb08771$ml$::uuid, 5, $ml$Pre-training is the fixed cost$ml$, $ml$GPT splits training into one costly pre-training step and cheap fine-tunes. That split shapes who can afford to build models.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://openai.com/index/language-unsupervised/", "label": "OpenAI, Improving language understanding with unsupervised learning, Jun 2018"}]$ml$::jsonb),
