($ml$7f47ae78-966a-51da-b22c-43dcf6e856e3$ml$::uuid, $ml$e4301e6e-444f-5df9-ba9b-d252c3d47b0b$ml$::uuid, 1, $ml$Day 31 · The model$ml$, $ml$In June 2018, OpenAI's first GPT trained for one month on 8 GPUs. OpenAI trained a Transformer on a few thousand books, about 5GB of text, to predict the next word. Then it fine-tuned that same model on much smaller labeled datasets, one for each task. The costly first step only had to be done once. The result set new records on a range of language tasks.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://openai.com/index/language-unsupervised/", "label": "OpenAI, Improving language understanding with unsupervised learning, Jun 2018"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$33968b6a-2213-5fbe-a66c-c729f051f099$ml$::uuid, $ml$e4301e6e-444f-5df9-ba9b-d252c3d47b0b$ml$::uuid, 2, $ml$G is for generative$ml$, $ml$Generative means the model writes. It picks the most likely next word, then the next. GPT-2 had one objective: predict the next word, given all the words before it.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://www.ibm.com/think/topics/gpt", "label": "IBM, What is GPT (generative pretrained transformer)?"}, {"url": "https://openai.com/index/better-language-models/", "label": "OpenAI, Better language models and their implications, Feb 2019"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$1bc48b59-8641-573c-a8f9-c9f9814b1aef$ml$::uuid, $ml$e4301e6e-444f-5df9-ba9b-d252c3d47b0b$ml$::uuid, 3, $ml$P is for pre-trained$ml$, $ml$Pre-trained means it first learns from raw text with no labels. The same model is then fine-tuned on much smaller labeled datasets.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://www.ibm.com/think/topics/gpt", "label": "IBM, What is GPT (generative pretrained transformer)?"}, {"url": "https://openai.com/index/language-unsupervised/", "label": "OpenAI, Improving language understanding with unsupervised learning, Jun 2018"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$6dc8d507-aafb-5e64-adad-3d4fbb724c2c$ml$::uuid, $ml$e4301e6e-444f-5df9-ba9b-d252c3d47b0b$ml$::uuid, 4, $ml$GPT keeps half of the 2017 design$ml$, $ml$The 2017 Transformer had an encoder and a decoder, six layers each. GPT trains the decoder alone to predict the next word. The decoder's mask lets each prediction see only the words before it.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://arxiv.org/html/1706.03762v7", "label": "Vaswani et al., Attention Is All You Need, 2017"}, {"url": "https://huggingface.co/docs/transformers/model_doc/openai-gpt", "label": "Hugging Face Transformers docs, OpenAI GPT"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$1dc9673a-b4c9-5d7f-b9a0-67c40df5c4da$ml$::uuid, $ml$e4301e6e-444f-5df9-ba9b-d252c3d47b0b$ml$::uuid, 5, $ml$Guess the next word, at scale$ml$, $ml$A GPT learns by guessing the next word over and over. Writing, answering and summarizing all grow from that one skill.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://openai.com/index/better-language-models/", "label": "OpenAI, Better language models and their implications, Feb 2019"}, {"url": "https://www.ibm.com/think/topics/gpt", "label": "IBM, What is GPT (generative pretrained transformer)?"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$3c0572b3-2077-56c2-b6eb-f3e3f9270176$ml$::uuid, $ml$e4301e6e-444f-5df9-ba9b-d252c3d47b0b$ml$::uuid, 6, $ml$The rule$ml$, $ml$A GPT is a Transformer decoder pre-trained to predict the next word.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://huggingface.co/docs/transformers/model_doc/openai-gpt", "label": "Hugging Face Transformers docs, OpenAI GPT"}, {"url": "https://openai.com/index/better-language-models/", "label": "OpenAI, Better language models and their implications, Feb 2019"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$69324906-8337-5d9f-ba63-72a39e184422$ml$::uuid, $ml$9366c9ae-6b53-570f-9973-b884c3e8c059$ml$::uuid, 1, $ml$Day 32 · The money$ml$, $ml$OpenAI predicted GPT-4's final loss from models trained with up to 10,000 times less compute. The GPT-4 technical report says OpenAI trained much smaller models the same way and extrapolated from them. That predicted GPT-4's final loss, a measure of how wrong its next-word guesses are. It also predicted a coding pass rate from models with at most 1,000 times less compute.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://arxiv.org/html/2303.08774v6", "label": "OpenAI, GPT-4 Technical Report, 2023"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$8bc45a34-c5aa-5642-b61f-705684c2fc5c$ml$::uuid, $ml$9366c9ae-6b53-570f-9973-b884c3e8c059$ml$::uuid, 2, $ml$The curve$ml$, $ml$A scaling law is a curve. Loss falls as a power law as you add parameters, data or compute. In 2020, OpenAI researchers saw such trends hold over more than seven orders of magnitude.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/2001.08361", "label": "Kaplan et al., Scaling Laws for Neural Language Models, 2020"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$50b171c6-b73b-5ce4-bd50-334878596f6e$ml$::uuid, $ml$9366c9ae-6b53-570f-9973-b884c3e8c059$ml$::uuid, 3, $ml$The 2020 recipe$ml$, $ml$The 2020 paper said to spend extra compute mostly on a bigger model. For each 10x more compute: about 5x more parameters, only 2x more data.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/2001.08361", "label": "Kaplan et al., Scaling Laws for Neural Language Models, 2020"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$03eb9c5b-5200-57ed-aa48-896f330ddca2$ml$::uuid, $ml$9366c9ae-6b53-570f-9973-b884c3e8c059$ml$::uuid, 4, $ml$Training is paid once, serving every day$ml$, $ml$For its 8B Llama 3, the Chinchilla-optimal amount was about 200B tokens. Meta trained far past that, and the model kept improving. Meta says smaller models are much more efficient during inference.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://ai.meta.com/blog/meta-llama-3/", "label": "Meta, Introducing Meta Llama 3, Apr 2024"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$f6685902-e425-5f84-9249-f7ad7be15b6b$ml$::uuid, $ml$9366c9ae-6b53-570f-9973-b884c3e8c059$ml$::uuid, 5, $ml$Explain why labs can forecast$ml$, $ml$Labs predict big runs from small ones. GPT-4's loss was predicted from runs with up to 10,000 times less compute.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://arxiv.org/html/2303.08774v6", "label": "OpenAI, GPT-4 Technical Report, 2023"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$b064e6e1-eb57-59c0-af42-749f9036cbaf$ml$::uuid, $ml$9366c9ae-6b53-570f-9973-b884c3e8c059$ml$::uuid, 6, $ml$The rule$ml$, $ml$In AI, scaling laws let labs price a big model before they pay for it.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://arxiv.org/html/2303.08774v6", "label": "OpenAI, GPT-4 Technical Report, 2023"}, {"url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/2001.08361", "label": "Kaplan et al., Scaling Laws for Neural Language Models, 2020"}]$ml$::jsonb),
