($ml$37557cf9-fa13-5efd-a321-accbd47b2357$ml$::uuid, $ml$8b6a172d-606f-5b3e-b58d-41ee88bda9b9$ml$::uuid, 2, $ml$The curve$ml$, $ml$A scaling law is a curve. Loss falls as a power law as you add parameters, data or compute. In 2020, OpenAI researchers saw such trends hold over more than seven orders of magnitude.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/2001.08361", "label": "Kaplan et al., Scaling Laws for Neural Language Models, 2020"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$98638248-aa8c-5afe-a38d-c732264008e2$ml$::uuid, $ml$8b6a172d-606f-5b3e-b58d-41ee88bda9b9$ml$::uuid, 3, $ml$The 2020 recipe$ml$, $ml$The 2020 paper said to spend extra compute mostly on a bigger model. For each 10x more compute: about 5x more parameters, only 2x more data.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/2001.08361", "label": "Kaplan et al., Scaling Laws for Neural Language Models, 2020"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$c68a985a-47de-5701-9d1e-eea2fc683f2e$ml$::uuid, $ml$8b6a172d-606f-5b3e-b58d-41ee88bda9b9$ml$::uuid, 4, $ml$Training is paid once, serving every day$ml$, $ml$For its 8B Llama 3, the Chinchilla-optimal amount was about 200B tokens. Meta trained far past that, and the model kept improving. Meta says smaller models are much more efficient during inference.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://ai.meta.com/blog/meta-llama-3/", "label": "Meta, Introducing Meta Llama 3, Apr 2024"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$63160955-67f1-5bb8-b5fc-d57e444d57fe$ml$::uuid, $ml$8b6a172d-606f-5b3e-b58d-41ee88bda9b9$ml$::uuid, 5, $ml$Forecasting a model$ml$, $ml$Before GPT-4 finished training, a curve fit to much smaller models had already predicted how well it would do.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://arxiv.org/html/2303.08774v6", "label": "OpenAI, GPT-4 Technical Report, 2023"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$f2a84bcb-0f6a-55fc-8c7e-540aff608c63$ml$::uuid, $ml$8b6a172d-606f-5b3e-b58d-41ee88bda9b9$ml$::uuid, 6, $ml$The rule$ml$, $ml$In AI, scaling laws let labs price a big model before they pay for it.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://arxiv.org/html/2303.08774v6", "label": "OpenAI, GPT-4 Technical Report, 2023"}, {"url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/2001.08361", "label": "Kaplan et al., Scaling Laws for Neural Language Models, 2020"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$14d63911-ce92-5b83-a755-4a750a911143$ml$::uuid, $ml$c094d522-6659-578e-9150-985320ed10cf$ml$::uuid, 1, $ml$Day 33 · The power$ml$, $ml$In February 2019, OpenAI held back its 1.5 billion parameter GPT-2, citing concerns about misuse. OpenAI released only a much smaller version, with sampling code. It shared larger versions with partners first, then published the full model on November 5, 2019, as the last step of a staged release. By then it said it had seen no strong evidence of misuse.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://openai.com/index/better-language-models/", "label": "OpenAI, Better language models and their implications, Feb 2019"}, {"url": "https://openai.com/index/gpt-2-1-5b-release/", "label": "OpenAI, GPT-2: 1.5B release, Nov 2019"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$3e9db04c-26b1-551b-a8b5-3a7b193bada3$ml$::uuid, $ml$c094d522-6659-578e-9150-985320ed10cf$ml$::uuid, 2, $ml$The control point$ml$, $ml$Whoever holds the weights decides who can run the model. At first, OpenAI withheld GPT-2's dataset, training code and weights.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://openai.com/index/better-language-models/", "label": "OpenAI, Better language models and their implications, Feb 2019"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$c3d88fde-2b93-52f0-ad02-ca4da56e1cd3$ml$::uuid, $ml$c094d522-6659-578e-9150-985320ed10cf$ml$::uuid, 3, $ml$The API model$ml$, $ml$GPT-3 came as an API in June 2020, not as a download. OpenAI said an API lets it restrict access over time, which open-sourcing cannot.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://openai.com/index/openai-api/", "label": "OpenAI, OpenAI API, Jun 2020"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$b3e1e34e-783c-5b4c-a479-bcf0eafa40d1$ml$::uuid, $ml$c094d522-6659-578e-9150-985320ed10cf$ml$::uuid, 4, $ml$One partner got an exclusive license$ml$, $ml$In September 2020, Microsoft licensed GPT-3 exclusively. Everyone else kept access through OpenAI's API. In 2023, the GPT-4 report gave no model size, hardware or training compute.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://www.infoq.com/news/2020/09/microsoft-license-gpt-3", "label": "InfoQ, Microsoft licenses GPT-3, Sep 2020"}, {"url": "https://arxiv.org/html/2303.08774v6", "label": "OpenAI, GPT-4 Technical Report, 2023"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$9a8db0a1-b3b8-5b08-a309-f2c2f79ee0f2$ml$::uuid, $ml$c094d522-6659-578e-9150-985320ed10cf$ml$::uuid, 5, $ml$Ask who holds the weights$ml$, $ml$In any AI discussion, ask whether a model is open weights or API only. It tells you who controls access.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://openai.com/index/openai-api/", "label": "OpenAI, OpenAI API, Jun 2020"}, {"url": "https://openai.com/index/better-language-models/", "label": "OpenAI, Better language models and their implications, Feb 2019"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$6cb7e03f-b60d-5eb8-bbb7-c335f54882a6$ml$::uuid, $ml$c094d522-6659-578e-9150-985320ed10cf$ml$::uuid, 6, $ml$The rule$ml$, $ml$In AI, whoever holds the weights decides who gets to use the model.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://openai.com/index/better-language-models/", "label": "OpenAI, Better language models and their implications, Feb 2019"}, {"url": "https://openai.com/index/openai-api/", "label": "OpenAI, OpenAI API, Jun 2020"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$06b0df91-30ad-5282-aee6-2c7d9c676c91$ml$::uuid, $ml$bf3cd387-4439-517f-be27-5f1a54b1b3f4$ml$::uuid, 1, $ml$Day 33 · The power$ml$, $ml$In February 2019, OpenAI held back its 1.5 billion parameter GPT-2, citing concerns about misuse. OpenAI released only a much smaller version, with sampling code. It shared larger versions with partners first, then published the full model on November 5, 2019, as the last step of a staged release. By then it said it had seen no strong evidence of misuse.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://openai.com/index/better-language-models/", "label": "OpenAI, Better language models and their implications, Feb 2019"}, {"url": "https://openai.com/index/gpt-2-1-5b-release/", "label": "OpenAI, GPT-2: 1.5B release, Nov 2019"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$3e44799b-ad89-5654-91bb-cab8555b21c8$ml$::uuid, $ml$bf3cd387-4439-517f-be27-5f1a54b1b3f4$ml$::uuid, 2, $ml$The control point$ml$, $ml$Whoever holds the weights decides who can run the model. At first, OpenAI withheld GPT-2's dataset, training code and weights.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://openai.com/index/better-language-models/", "label": "OpenAI, Better language models and their implications, Feb 2019"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$412a37ef-2284-58bb-8dd7-c8ab93d45d76$ml$::uuid, $ml$bf3cd387-4439-517f-be27-5f1a54b1b3f4$ml$::uuid, 3, $ml$The API model$ml$, $ml$GPT-3 came as an API in June 2020, not as a download. OpenAI said an API lets it restrict access over time, which open-sourcing cannot.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://openai.com/index/openai-api/", "label": "OpenAI, OpenAI API, Jun 2020"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$385f0239-eed0-5bc2-a27b-25c3bf2f97b0$ml$::uuid, $ml$bf3cd387-4439-517f-be27-5f1a54b1b3f4$ml$::uuid, 4, $ml$One partner got an exclusive license$ml$, $ml$In September 2020, Microsoft licensed GPT-3 exclusively. Everyone else kept access through OpenAI's API. In 2023, the GPT-4 report gave no model size, hardware or training compute.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://www.infoq.com/news/2020/09/microsoft-license-gpt-3", "label": "InfoQ, Microsoft licenses GPT-3, Sep 2020"}, {"url": "https://arxiv.org/html/2303.08774v6", "label": "OpenAI, GPT-4 Technical Report, 2023"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$06ba89f1-fa08-525e-81f2-c2f0713f5993$ml$::uuid, $ml$bf3cd387-4439-517f-be27-5f1a54b1b3f4$ml$::uuid, 5, $ml$Access is the moat$ml$, $ml$OpenAI kept GPT-3 behind an API and gave Microsoft an exclusive license. Control over access is where the value sat.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://openai.com/index/openai-api/", "label": "OpenAI, OpenAI API, Jun 2020"}, {"url": "https://www.infoq.com/news/2020/09/microsoft-license-gpt-3", "label": "InfoQ, Microsoft licenses GPT-3, Sep 2020"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$00cbd15d-b1b2-5025-a20a-2ad4598dce45$ml$::uuid, $ml$bf3cd387-4439-517f-be27-5f1a54b1b3f4$ml$::uuid, 6, $ml$The rule$ml$, $ml$In AI, whoever holds the weights decides who gets to use the model.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://openai.com/index/better-language-models/", "label": "OpenAI, Better language models and their implications, Feb 2019"}, {"url": "https://openai.com/index/openai-api/", "label": "OpenAI, OpenAI API, Jun 2020"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$dc00fbbe-5131-5e53-8b5f-6c8a1f68b5bd$ml$::uuid, $ml$84923999-39ec-558b-8665-7c030589dc1a$ml$::uuid, 1, $ml$Day 33 · The power$ml$, $ml$In February 2019, OpenAI held back its 1.5 billion parameter GPT-2, citing concerns about misuse. OpenAI released only a much smaller version, with sampling code. It shared larger versions with partners first, then published the full model on November 5, 2019, as the last step of a staged release. By then it said it had seen no strong evidence of misuse.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://openai.com/index/better-language-models/", "label": "OpenAI, Better language models and their implications, Feb 2019"}, {"url": "https://openai.com/index/gpt-2-1-5b-release/", "label": "OpenAI, GPT-2: 1.5B release, Nov 2019"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$4b6868b4-e9fc-5b99-af76-a14371506661$ml$::uuid, $ml$84923999-39ec-558b-8665-7c030589dc1a$ml$::uuid, 2, $ml$The control point$ml$, $ml$Whoever holds the weights decides who can run the model. At first, OpenAI withheld GPT-2's dataset, training code and weights.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://openai.com/index/better-language-models/", "label": "OpenAI, Better language models and their implications, Feb 2019"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$b2c1eec0-dc0c-5323-833f-f104ad368ea3$ml$::uuid, $ml$84923999-39ec-558b-8665-7c030589dc1a$ml$::uuid, 3, $ml$The API model$ml$, $ml$GPT-3 came as an API in June 2020, not as a download. OpenAI said an API lets it restrict access over time, which open-sourcing cannot.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://openai.com/index/openai-api/", "label": "OpenAI, OpenAI API, Jun 2020"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$7a8b9b38-e43e-53a0-ac23-16dacf2deff5$ml$::uuid, $ml$84923999-39ec-558b-8665-7c030589dc1a$ml$::uuid, 4, $ml$One partner got an exclusive license$ml$, $ml$In September 2020, Microsoft licensed GPT-3 exclusively. Everyone else kept access through OpenAI's API. In 2023, the GPT-4 report gave no model size, hardware or training compute.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://www.infoq.com/news/2020/09/microsoft-license-gpt-3", "label": "InfoQ, Microsoft licenses GPT-3, Sep 2020"}, {"url": "https://arxiv.org/html/2303.08774v6", "label": "OpenAI, GPT-4 Technical Report, 2023"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$897b7e76-d5b5-5776-964f-64b3c24fb4d6$ml$::uuid, $ml$84923999-39ec-558b-8665-7c030589dc1a$ml$::uuid, 5, $ml$Know your dependency$ml$, $ml$An API provider can restrict access over time. If your product depends on one, have a plan B.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://openai.com/index/openai-api/", "label": "OpenAI, OpenAI API, Jun 2020"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$5a5258a9-2703-547c-ae51-684d936951a2$ml$::uuid, $ml$84923999-39ec-558b-8665-7c030589dc1a$ml$::uuid, 6, $ml$The rule$ml$, $ml$In AI, whoever holds the weights decides who gets to use the model.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://openai.com/index/better-language-models/", "label": "OpenAI, Better language models and their implications, Feb 2019"}, {"url": "https://openai.com/index/openai-api/", "label": "OpenAI, OpenAI API, Jun 2020"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$ed9a64ad-915f-5795-a12d-ea7d171d7c17$ml$::uuid, $ml$a9112133-4349-5b27-8dd6-a447f31a81e0$ml$::uuid, 1, $ml$Day 33 · The power$ml$, $ml$In February 2019, OpenAI held back its 1.5 billion parameter GPT-2, citing concerns about misuse. OpenAI released only a much smaller version, with sampling code. It shared larger versions with partners first, then published the full model on November 5, 2019, as the last step of a staged release. By then it said it had seen no strong evidence of misuse.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://openai.com/index/better-language-models/", "label": "OpenAI, Better language models and their implications, Feb 2019"}, {"url": "https://openai.com/index/gpt-2-1-5b-release/", "label": "OpenAI, GPT-2: 1.5B release, Nov 2019"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$248db9ad-40a0-5a54-97e8-db3899ea9b4f$ml$::uuid, $ml$a9112133-4349-5b27-8dd6-a447f31a81e0$ml$::uuid, 2, $ml$The control point$ml$, $ml$Whoever holds the weights decides who can run the model. At first, OpenAI withheld GPT-2's dataset, training code and weights.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://openai.com/index/better-language-models/", "label": "OpenAI, Better language models and their implications, Feb 2019"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$53ff19fe-7130-5667-bcf8-ed7cdb1c66d0$ml$::uuid, $ml$a9112133-4349-5b27-8dd6-a447f31a81e0$ml$::uuid, 3, $ml$The API model$ml$, $ml$GPT-3 came as an API in June 2020, not as a download. OpenAI said an API lets it restrict access over time, which open-sourcing cannot.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://openai.com/index/openai-api/", "label": "OpenAI, OpenAI API, Jun 2020"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$712044ec-36bc-529b-aa96-85a7b6d3fc78$ml$::uuid, $ml$a9112133-4349-5b27-8dd6-a447f31a81e0$ml$::uuid, 4, $ml$One partner got an exclusive license$ml$, $ml$In September 2020, Microsoft licensed GPT-3 exclusively. Everyone else kept access through OpenAI's API. In 2023, the GPT-4 report gave no model size, hardware or training compute.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://www.infoq.com/news/2020/09/microsoft-license-gpt-3", "label": "InfoQ, Microsoft licenses GPT-3, Sep 2020"}, {"url": "https://arxiv.org/html/2303.08774v6", "label": "OpenAI, GPT-4 Technical Report, 2023"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$3f906844-bfc5-5ee1-9fb9-f49282aa25f4$ml$::uuid, $ml$a9112133-4349-5b27-8dd6-a447f31a81e0$ml$::uuid, 5, $ml$Too risky to release, once$ml$, $ml$In February 2019, OpenAI held back GPT-2 over misuse fears. In November 2019 it published the full model.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://openai.com/index/better-language-models/", "label": "OpenAI, Better language models and their implications, Feb 2019"}, {"url": "https://openai.com/index/gpt-2-1-5b-release/", "label": "OpenAI, GPT-2: 1.5B release, Nov 2019"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$aff9d984-ba5e-5b16-993e-0132b7738f3f$ml$::uuid, $ml$a9112133-4349-5b27-8dd6-a447f31a81e0$ml$::uuid, 6, $ml$The rule$ml$, $ml$In AI, whoever holds the weights decides who gets to use the model.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://openai.com/index/better-language-models/", "label": "OpenAI, Better language models and their implications, Feb 2019"}, {"url": "https://openai.com/index/openai-api/", "label": "OpenAI, OpenAI API, Jun 2020"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$9887bbbf-9727-56a8-a9fe-3ead7065486a$ml$::uuid, $ml$5b9eefd5-4986-57e5-af94-7a4d1a755875$ml$::uuid, 1, $ml$Day 34 · The numbers$ml$, $ml$Training GPT-3 took 3,640 petaflop/s-days of compute. That is 3,640 days for a machine doing a quadrillion operations every second, about 10 years. The GPT-3 paper counts it simply: about 6 operations per parameter for every training token. Multiply by parameters and tokens and you get about 3.14 × 10^23 operations.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2005.14165", "label": "Brown et al., Language Models are Few-Shot Learners (GPT-3), 2020"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$49b03191-2783-56d5-9e9c-b7d4075bae1c$ml$::uuid, $ml$5b9eefd5-4986-57e5-af94-7a4d1a755875$ml$::uuid, 2, $ml$The rule of thumb$ml$, $ml$Training compute is about 6 × parameters × tokens. The forward pass costs 2 operations per parameter, the backward pass 4 more. Attention is left out because it usually takes under 10% of compute.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2005.14165", "label": "Brown et al., Language Models are Few-Shot Learners (GPT-3), 2020"}, {"url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/2001.08361", "label": "Kaplan et al., Scaling Laws for Neural Language Models, 2020"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$387efb56-0054-56a3-891a-8564b70e24fe$ml$::uuid, $ml$5b9eefd5-4986-57e5-af94-7a4d1a755875$ml$::uuid, 3, $ml$At the frontier$ml$, $ml$The rule still works on the biggest open models. Meta's 405B Llama 3 saw 15.6T tokens and used 3.8 × 10^25 FLOPs.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2407.21783", "label": "Meta, The Llama 3 Herd of Models, 2024"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$6a63b05d-5e20-5ff8-a192-01dc04be65fd$ml$::uuid, $ml$5b9eefd5-4986-57e5-af94-7a4d1a755875$ml$::uuid, 4, $ml$About 121 times more in four years$ml$, $ml$GPT-3 used 3.14 × 10^23 operations in 2020. Llama 3's flagship used 3.8 × 10^25 in 2024. That flagship used almost 50 times the compute of the largest Llama 2.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2005.14165", "label": "Brown et al., Language Models are Few-Shot Learners (GPT-3), 2020"}, {"url": "https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2407.21783", "label": "Meta, The Llama 3 Herd of Models, 2024"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$a6c8dd3a-1b59-56bd-8dd5-569474a54b56$ml$::uuid, $ml$5b9eefd5-4986-57e5-af94-7a4d1a755875$ml$::uuid, 5, $ml$Do the math out loud$ml$, $ml$Six times parameters times tokens gives training compute. Being able to size a run in your head stands out in interviews.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2005.14165", "label": "Brown et al., Language Models are Few-Shot Learners (GPT-3), 2020"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$69dcd45a-e792-5e57-8107-d3738dd26620$ml$::uuid, $ml$5b9eefd5-4986-57e5-af94-7a4d1a755875$ml$::uuid, 6, $ml$The rule$ml$, $ml$In AI, training compute is roughly 6 times parameters times tokens.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2005.14165", "label": "Brown et al., Language Models are Few-Shot Learners (GPT-3), 2020"}, {"url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/2001.08361", "label": "Kaplan et al., Scaling Laws for Neural Language Models, 2020"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$8a808127-bd09-50e2-9ce3-b80ab1035aec$ml$::uuid, $ml$466467a9-cc3a-5d2a-8e6f-f8d4acb78f71$ml$::uuid, 1, $ml$Day 34 · The numbers$ml$, $ml$Training GPT-3 took 3,640 petaflop/s-days of compute. That is 3,640 days for a machine doing a quadrillion operations every second, about 10 years. The GPT-3 paper counts it simply: about 6 operations per parameter for every training token. Multiply by parameters and tokens and you get about 3.14 × 10^23 operations.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2005.14165", "label": "Brown et al., Language Models are Few-Shot Learners (GPT-3), 2020"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$2c7dc4e4-4550-5b9b-aab7-81805226d2f5$ml$::uuid, $ml$466467a9-cc3a-5d2a-8e6f-f8d4acb78f71$ml$::uuid, 2, $ml$The rule of thumb$ml$, $ml$Training compute is about 6 × parameters × tokens. The forward pass costs 2 operations per parameter, the backward pass 4 more. Attention is left out because it usually takes under 10% of compute.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2005.14165", "label": "Brown et al., Language Models are Few-Shot Learners (GPT-3), 2020"}, {"url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/2001.08361", "label": "Kaplan et al., Scaling Laws for Neural Language Models, 2020"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$dccde5f6-6a6b-5a48-9730-e28647c3ae3c$ml$::uuid, $ml$466467a9-cc3a-5d2a-8e6f-f8d4acb78f71$ml$::uuid, 3, $ml$At the frontier$ml$, $ml$The rule still works on the biggest open models. Meta's 405B Llama 3 saw 15.6T tokens and used 3.8 × 10^25 FLOPs.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2407.21783", "label": "Meta, The Llama 3 Herd of Models, 2024"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$5789fe66-cb99-55ff-89a2-10fc06daead0$ml$::uuid, $ml$466467a9-cc3a-5d2a-8e6f-f8d4acb78f71$ml$::uuid, 4, $ml$About 121 times more in four years$ml$, $ml$GPT-3 used 3.14 × 10^23 operations in 2020. Llama 3's flagship used 3.8 × 10^25 in 2024. That flagship used almost 50 times the compute of the largest Llama 2.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2005.14165", "label": "Brown et al., Language Models are Few-Shot Learners (GPT-3), 2020"}, {"url": "https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2407.21783", "label": "Meta, The Llama 3 Herd of Models, 2024"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$a4360f6a-a712-52a5-93c2-2b06a5559fda$ml$::uuid, $ml$466467a9-cc3a-5d2a-8e6f-f8d4acb78f71$ml$::uuid, 5, $ml$Translate size into compute$ml$, $ml$A model's parameters and tokens tell you its compute, and compute drives cost. Llama 3's flagship took 3.8 × 10^25 FLOPs.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2407.21783", "label": "Meta, The Llama 3 Herd of Models, 2024"}, {"url": "https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2005.14165", "label": "Brown et al., Language Models are Few-Shot Learners (GPT-3), 2020"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$f9e582db-82ed-5804-8cc9-350b22d64016$ml$::uuid, $ml$466467a9-cc3a-5d2a-8e6f-f8d4acb78f71$ml$::uuid, 6, $ml$The rule$ml$, $ml$In AI, training compute is roughly 6 times parameters times tokens.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2005.14165", "label": "Brown et al., Language Models are Few-Shot Learners (GPT-3), 2020"}, {"url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/2001.08361", "label": "Kaplan et al., Scaling Laws for Neural Language Models, 2020"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$cc994925-6a91-521b-8d15-fd0f2dcd1aae$ml$::uuid, $ml$3151891c-81dd-5e9f-aebf-9e6b35a3ef31$ml$::uuid, 1, $ml$Day 34 · The numbers$ml$, $ml$Training GPT-3 took 3,640 petaflop/s-days of compute. That is 3,640 days for a machine doing a quadrillion operations every second, about 10 years. The GPT-3 paper counts it simply: about 6 operations per parameter for every training token. Multiply by parameters and tokens and you get about 3.14 × 10^23 operations.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2005.14165", "label": "Brown et al., Language Models are Few-Shot Learners (GPT-3), 2020"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$677e57e3-541d-54a5-89a1-8e69f9dd7a2a$ml$::uuid, $ml$3151891c-81dd-5e9f-aebf-9e6b35a3ef31$ml$::uuid, 2, $ml$The rule of thumb$ml$, $ml$Training compute is about 6 × parameters × tokens. The forward pass costs 2 operations per parameter, the backward pass 4 more. Attention is left out because it usually takes under 10% of compute.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2005.14165", "label": "Brown et al., Language Models are Few-Shot Learners (GPT-3), 2020"}, {"url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/2001.08361", "label": "Kaplan et al., Scaling Laws for Neural Language Models, 2020"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$fc8cfd2e-6a64-5499-baa8-5529c66fd4f6$ml$::uuid, $ml$3151891c-81dd-5e9f-aebf-9e6b35a3ef31$ml$::uuid, 3, $ml$At the frontier$ml$, $ml$The rule still works on the biggest open models. Meta's 405B Llama 3 saw 15.6T tokens and used 3.8 × 10^25 FLOPs.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2407.21783", "label": "Meta, The Llama 3 Herd of Models, 2024"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$286ba8d1-9a37-5b1c-b100-5cc5dad98f4f$ml$::uuid, $ml$3151891c-81dd-5e9f-aebf-9e6b35a3ef31$ml$::uuid, 4, $ml$About 121 times more in four years$ml$, $ml$GPT-3 used 3.14 × 10^23 operations in 2020. Llama 3's flagship used 3.8 × 10^25 in 2024. That flagship used almost 50 times the compute of the largest Llama 2.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2005.14165", "label": "Brown et al., Language Models are Few-Shot Learners (GPT-3), 2020"}, {"url": "https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2407.21783", "label": "Meta, The Llama 3 Herd of Models, 2024"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$d6795388-8c88-5a90-ab91-fd53981274b3$ml$::uuid, $ml$3151891c-81dd-5e9f-aebf-9e6b35a3ef31$ml$::uuid, 5, $ml$Size your run before you rent$ml$, $ml$Multiply 6 by your parameters and tokens before you book GPUs. It tells you whether the plan fits your budget.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2005.14165", "label": "Brown et al., Language Models are Few-Shot Learners (GPT-3), 2020"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$81eead8f-edde-5c16-8080-9226b60499c2$ml$::uuid, $ml$3151891c-81dd-5e9f-aebf-9e6b35a3ef31$ml$::uuid, 6, $ml$The rule$ml$, $ml$In AI, training compute is roughly 6 times parameters times tokens.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2005.14165", "label": "Brown et al., Language Models are Few-Shot Learners (GPT-3), 2020"}, {"url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/2001.08361", "label": "Kaplan et al., Scaling Laws for Neural Language Models, 2020"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$82fd142e-b85e-58c3-8c6c-1bd89b6474be$ml$::uuid, $ml$971bddb7-f64f-5670-b6c4-a841c00ac2f2$ml$::uuid, 1, $ml$Day 34 · The numbers$ml$, $ml$Training GPT-3 took 3,640 petaflop/s-days of compute. That is 3,640 days for a machine doing a quadrillion operations every second, about 10 years. The GPT-3 paper counts it simply: about 6 operations per parameter for every training token. Multiply by parameters and tokens and you get about 3.14 × 10^23 operations.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2005.14165", "label": "Brown et al., Language Models are Few-Shot Learners (GPT-3), 2020"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$b1b7d514-1529-5b68-85bb-04f05d23e7d9$ml$::uuid, $ml$971bddb7-f64f-5670-b6c4-a841c00ac2f2$ml$::uuid, 2, $ml$The rule of thumb$ml$, $ml$Training compute is about 6 × parameters × tokens. The forward pass costs 2 operations per parameter, the backward pass 4 more. Attention is left out because it usually takes under 10% of compute.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2005.14165", "label": "Brown et al., Language Models are Few-Shot Learners (GPT-3), 2020"}, {"url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/2001.08361", "label": "Kaplan et al., Scaling Laws for Neural Language Models, 2020"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$ecdc4597-bff5-5482-9848-f78ff6a09324$ml$::uuid, $ml$971bddb7-f64f-5670-b6c4-a841c00ac2f2$ml$::uuid, 3, $ml$At the frontier$ml$, $ml$The rule still works on the biggest open models. Meta's 405B Llama 3 saw 15.6T tokens and used 3.8 × 10^25 FLOPs.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2407.21783", "label": "Meta, The Llama 3 Herd of Models, 2024"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$e04b7c7d-d607-563e-87a7-782625bf014f$ml$::uuid, $ml$971bddb7-f64f-5670-b6c4-a841c00ac2f2$ml$::uuid, 4, $ml$About 121 times more in four years$ml$, $ml$GPT-3 used 3.14 × 10^23 operations in 2020. Llama 3's flagship used 3.8 × 10^25 in 2024. That flagship used almost 50 times the compute of the largest Llama 2.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2005.14165", "label": "Brown et al., Language Models are Few-Shot Learners (GPT-3), 2020"}, {"url": "https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2407.21783", "label": "Meta, The Llama 3 Herd of Models, 2024"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$54f6a458-3634-5940-b2dc-8255fdf6b946$ml$::uuid, $ml$971bddb7-f64f-5670-b6c4-a841c00ac2f2$ml$::uuid, 5, $ml$Ten years in one run$ml$, $ml$At a quadrillion operations a second, GPT-3's training would take about 10 years. One rule of thumb gets you there.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2005.14165", "label": "Brown et al., Language Models are Few-Shot Learners (GPT-3), 2020"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$69a98ac4-183b-52be-9574-3895a3ce00a0$ml$::uuid, $ml$971bddb7-f64f-5670-b6c4-a841c00ac2f2$ml$::uuid, 6, $ml$The rule$ml$, $ml$In AI, training compute is roughly 6 times parameters times tokens.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2005.14165", "label": "Brown et al., Language Models are Few-Shot Learners (GPT-3), 2020"}, {"url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/2001.08361", "label": "Kaplan et al., Scaling Laws for Neural Language Models, 2020"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$3aaf136e-f1fe-50a5-9d81-e2acce691814$ml$::uuid, $ml$af51a99b-a674-55b4-9eff-1242df73d884$ml$::uuid, 1, $ml$Day 35 · The rules$ml$, $ml$In the EU, a model trained with more than 10^25 operations is presumed to carry systemic risk. Article 51 of the AI Act ties extra duties to training compute. Since August 2, 2025, a provider whose model crosses that line must notify the European Commission within two weeks. It must then run evaluations with adversarial testing, report serious incidents and protect the model with adequate cybersecurity.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://ai-act-service-desk.ec.europa.eu/en/ai-act/article-51", "label": "EU AI Act Service Desk, Article 51"}, {"url": "https://digital-strategy.ec.europa.eu/en/faqs/guidelines-obligations-gpai-providers", "label": "European Commission, FAQ on obligations for GPAI providers"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$e3440de1-b8cb-5aa0-8b57-1cbfcc0f826b$ml$::uuid, $ml$af51a99b-a674-55b4-9eff-1242df73d884$ml$::uuid, 2, $ml$Why compute$ml$, $ml$The law presumes high-impact capabilities from training compute alone. The Commission can move the line as algorithms and hardware improve.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://ai-act-service-desk.ec.europa.eu/en/ai-act/article-51", "label": "EU AI Act Service Desk, Article 51"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$97d99899-efcc-5c4d-aadf-ce121cabefc6$ml$::uuid, $ml$af51a99b-a674-55b4-9eff-1242df73d884$ml$::uuid, 3, $ml$How many$ml$, $ml$GPT-4, released in March 2023, was the first model trained at that scale. By June 2025, Epoch AI counted over 30 models from 12 developers above it.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://epoch.ai/data-insights/models-over-1e25-flop", "label": "Epoch AI, models over 10^25 FLOP, Jun 2025"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$9f44c6cd-0885-5a3b-b21f-c583db8deea9$ml$::uuid, $ml$af51a99b-a674-55b4-9eff-1242df73d884$ml$::uuid, 4, $ml$California drew its line at 10^26$ml$, $ml$SB 53, signed on September 29, 2025, covers models above 10^26 operations. Firms with over $500 million in revenue must publish a safety framework. Compute spent on fine-tuning counts toward the line. Penalties reach $1 million per violation.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://fpf.org/blog/californias-sb-53-the-first-frontier-ai-law-explained/", "label": "Future of Privacy Forum, California's SB 53 explained"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$60c77578-9a98-5ea0-805c-8df131114da6$ml$::uuid, $ml$af51a99b-a674-55b4-9eff-1242df73d884$ml$::uuid, 5, $ml$Know the two lines$ml$, $ml$10^25 in the EU, 10^26 in California. Knowing which models cross which line is useful in policy and compliance roles.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://ai-act-service-desk.ec.europa.eu/en/ai-act/article-51", "label": "EU AI Act Service Desk, Article 51"}, {"url": "https://fpf.org/blog/californias-sb-53-the-first-frontier-ai-law-explained/", "label": "Future of Privacy Forum, California's SB 53 explained"}]$ml$::jsonb),
