($ml$172b8297-b18c-5f9c-8d34-9a19dc6e465d$ml$::uuid, $ml$7f549c2b-c505-5220-a076-c56725af4097$ml$::uuid, 3, $ml$Even by accident$ml$, $ml$Fine-tuning on harmless, common datasets also weakened safety, to a lesser degree. Customers can erode guardrails without meaning to.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2310.03693v1", "label": "Qi et al., Fine-tuning aligned language models compromises safety, Oct 2023"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$7ced88cc-4b09-542c-b74e-f8035c91dce2$ml$::uuid, $ml$7f549c2b-c505-5220-a076-c56725af4097$ml$::uuid, 4, $ml$Hosted fine-tuning keeps a veto$ml$, $ml$A provider that hosts fine-tuning can test and watch the results. Anyone holding open weights can fine-tune them alone. OpenAI runs automated safety evaluations on fine-tuned GPT-4o models.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://openai.com/index/gpt-4o-fine-tuning/", "label": "OpenAI, Fine-tuning now available for GPT-4o, Aug 2024"}, {"url": "https://www.theregister.com/2023/10/12/chatbot_defenses_dissolve/", "label": "The Register, chatbot defenses dissolve with fine-tuning, Oct 2023"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$9616565a-5ba2-50d0-b7ab-dd27c80149f6$ml$::uuid, $ml$7f549c2b-c505-5220-a076-c56725af4097$ml$::uuid, 5, $ml$Ten examples, under 20 cents$ml$, $ml$Researchers undid a chatbot's safety training with 10 examples, for less than $0.20.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2310.03693v1", "label": "Qi et al., Fine-tuning aligned language models compromises safety, Oct 2023"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$464a4c38-5603-5ef8-9526-e2bb6c845fcc$ml$::uuid, $ml$7f549c2b-c505-5220-a076-c56725af4097$ml$::uuid, 6, $ml$The rule$ml$, $ml$In AI, whoever fine-tunes a model last decides how it behaves.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2310.03693v1", "label": "Qi et al., Fine-tuning aligned language models compromises safety, Oct 2023"}, {"url": "https://www.theregister.com/2023/10/12/chatbot_defenses_dissolve/", "label": "The Register, chatbot defenses dissolve with fine-tuning, Oct 2023"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$2101baf7-c86d-58d0-a10c-ce2e65bd2dd4$ml$::uuid, $ml$8c2e333f-c61d-5429-a81e-0d26fc604a0e$ml$::uuid, 1, $ml$Day 46 · The numbers$ml$, $ml$In March 2023, Stanford fine-tuned a 7-billion-parameter model for under $600. In blind tests it won 90 matchups to 89 against OpenAI's text-davinci-003. Stanford's Alpaca started from Meta's LLaMA 7B. The team generated 52,000 instruction-following examples with text-davinci-003 for less than $500, then fine-tuned for 3 hours on eight A100 GPUs for less than $100. They judged it with a blind pairwise comparison against the OpenAI model.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://crfm.stanford.edu/2023/03/13/alpaca.html", "label": "Stanford CRFM, Alpaca: a strong, replicable instruction-following model, Mar 2023"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$27cf4aab-4def-572b-8db9-2eaa207e2b9a$ml$::uuid, $ml$8c2e333f-c61d-5429-a81e-0d26fc604a0e$ml$::uuid, 2, $ml$The metric$ml$, $ml$A win rate is the share of head-to-head comparisons a model wins against a reference. A judge sees two answers and picks one. Alpaca won 90 and lost 89: a win rate of about 50%, a tie.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://crfm.stanford.edu/2023/03/13/alpaca.html", "label": "Stanford CRFM, Alpaca: a strong, replicable instruction-following model, Mar 2023"}, {"url": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2404.04475", "label": "Dubois et al., Length-Controlled AlpacaEval, Apr 2024"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$c24c64b5-0a5b-5bc4-9c2f-8b0bb12b9e1e$ml$::uuid, $ml$8c2e333f-c61d-5429-a81e-0d26fc604a0e$ml$::uuid, 3, $ml$How much data$ml$, $ml$Meta's LIMA fine-tuned a 65B model on just 1,000 curated examples, with no RLHF. Its answers were equal or preferred to GPT-4's in 43% of cases.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2305.11206", "label": "Zhou et al., LIMA: Less Is More for Alignment, May 2023"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$8c8bb87b-0fbd-5570-9216-6aa49c15191d$ml$::uuid, $ml$8c2e333f-c61d-5429-a81e-0d26fc604a0e$ml$::uuid, 4, $ml$Judges like longer answers$ml$, $ml$Automatic judges tend to favor longer outputs, which can inflate a win rate. Controlling for length removes much of that bias. Length control lifted AlpacaEval's correlation with Chatbot Arena from 0.94 to 0.98.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2404.04475", "label": "Dubois et al., Length-Controlled AlpacaEval, Apr 2024"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$b3f0d351-98af-57ea-a737-719e1c6573f3$ml$::uuid, $ml$8c2e333f-c61d-5429-a81e-0d26fc604a0e$ml$::uuid, 5, $ml$Ask what it won against$ml$, $ml$A win rate means little without its reference model and its judge. Ask about both before you quote it.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://crfm.stanford.edu/2023/03/13/alpaca.html", "label": "Stanford CRFM, Alpaca: a strong, replicable instruction-following model, Mar 2023"}, {"url": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2404.04475", "label": "Dubois et al., Length-Controlled AlpacaEval, Apr 2024"}]$ml$::jsonb),
