($ml$a2779501-ad64-569f-a3c3-de97771e85c6$ml$::uuid, $ml$98262f2b-6b87-5cad-99b6-e71b8ed68892$ml$::uuid, 4, $ml$Judges like longer answers$ml$, $ml$Automatic judges tend to favor longer outputs, which can inflate a win rate. Controlling for length removes much of that bias. Length control lifted AlpacaEval's correlation with Chatbot Arena from 0.94 to 0.98.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2404.04475", "label": "Dubois et al., Length-Controlled AlpacaEval, Apr 2024"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$02ddd3c7-ee2b-5e4b-aa04-36bcee0108af$ml$::uuid, $ml$98262f2b-6b87-5cad-99b6-e71b8ed68892$ml$::uuid, 5, $ml$Small data, real results$ml$, $ml$LIMA used 1,000 examples and Alpaca cost under $600. A focused fine-tune is within a small team's reach.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2305.11206", "label": "Zhou et al., LIMA: Less Is More for Alignment, May 2023"}, {"url": "https://crfm.stanford.edu/2023/03/13/alpaca.html", "label": "Stanford CRFM, Alpaca: a strong, replicable instruction-following model, Mar 2023"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$377edcc1-2b85-575e-8a32-8dc5bb51de54$ml$::uuid, $ml$98262f2b-6b87-5cad-99b6-e71b8ed68892$ml$::uuid, 6, $ml$The rule$ml$, $ml$In fine-tuning, a win rate means something only next to its judge and its reference.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://crfm.stanford.edu/2023/03/13/alpaca.html", "label": "Stanford CRFM, Alpaca: a strong, replicable instruction-following model, Mar 2023"}, {"url": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2404.04475", "label": "Dubois et al., Length-Controlled AlpacaEval, Apr 2024"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$669371c5-f38c-5f0e-aadf-fa00906b2df6$ml$::uuid, $ml$e676057b-fab7-5368-abd4-79ad29b235ab$ml$::uuid, 1, $ml$Day 46 · The numbers$ml$, $ml$In March 2023, Stanford fine-tuned a 7-billion-parameter model for under $600. In blind tests it won 90 matchups to 89 against OpenAI's text-davinci-003. Stanford's Alpaca started from Meta's LLaMA 7B. The team generated 52,000 instruction-following examples with text-davinci-003 for less than $500, then fine-tuned for 3 hours on eight A100 GPUs for less than $100. They judged it with a blind pairwise comparison against the OpenAI model.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://crfm.stanford.edu/2023/03/13/alpaca.html", "label": "Stanford CRFM, Alpaca: a strong, replicable instruction-following model, Mar 2023"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$9d5db8c0-1047-5e19-9f61-a65c357f0ba5$ml$::uuid, $ml$e676057b-fab7-5368-abd4-79ad29b235ab$ml$::uuid, 2, $ml$The metric$ml$, $ml$A win rate is the share of head-to-head comparisons a model wins against a reference. A judge sees two answers and picks one. Alpaca won 90 and lost 89: a win rate of about 50%, a tie.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://crfm.stanford.edu/2023/03/13/alpaca.html", "label": "Stanford CRFM, Alpaca: a strong, replicable instruction-following model, Mar 2023"}, {"url": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2404.04475", "label": "Dubois et al., Length-Controlled AlpacaEval, Apr 2024"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$6a8ae425-4e92-5af1-b61d-f55f4d3a8f8e$ml$::uuid, $ml$e676057b-fab7-5368-abd4-79ad29b235ab$ml$::uuid, 3, $ml$How much data$ml$, $ml$Meta's LIMA fine-tuned a 65B model on just 1,000 curated examples, with no RLHF. Its answers were equal or preferred to GPT-4's in 43% of cases.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2305.11206", "label": "Zhou et al., LIMA: Less Is More for Alignment, May 2023"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$0b94f55a-fd5e-507f-84ea-5b0e1d7a6214$ml$::uuid, $ml$e676057b-fab7-5368-abd4-79ad29b235ab$ml$::uuid, 4, $ml$Judges like longer answers$ml$, $ml$Automatic judges tend to favor longer outputs, which can inflate a win rate. Controlling for length removes much of that bias. Length control lifted AlpacaEval's correlation with Chatbot Arena from 0.94 to 0.98.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2404.04475", "label": "Dubois et al., Length-Controlled AlpacaEval, Apr 2024"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$2953c91e-276d-592b-ba24-eea77779de67$ml$::uuid, $ml$e676057b-fab7-5368-abd4-79ad29b235ab$ml$::uuid, 5, $ml$A chatbot for $600$ml$, $ml$In 2023, Stanford built a model that tied an OpenAI model in blind tests, for under $600.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://crfm.stanford.edu/2023/03/13/alpaca.html", "label": "Stanford CRFM, Alpaca: a strong, replicable instruction-following model, Mar 2023"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$ed37852f-c6da-596e-a52b-3887f8f945ee$ml$::uuid, $ml$e676057b-fab7-5368-abd4-79ad29b235ab$ml$::uuid, 6, $ml$The rule$ml$, $ml$In fine-tuning, a win rate means something only next to its judge and its reference.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://crfm.stanford.edu/2023/03/13/alpaca.html", "label": "Stanford CRFM, Alpaca: a strong, replicable instruction-following model, Mar 2023"}, {"url": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2404.04475", "label": "Dubois et al., Length-Controlled AlpacaEval, Apr 2024"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$234661ae-ca53-53f7-ad7b-269f2fa08efb$ml$::uuid, $ml$12a09f68-408e-56cd-b317-8615ba520086$ml$::uuid, 1, $ml$Day 47 · The rule$ml$, $ml$Under EU guidelines from July 18, 2025, fine-tuning a general-purpose model with more than a third of its original training compute makes you its provider. The European Commission's guidelines on general-purpose AI treat this as an indicative rule of thumb under the AI Act. Such a change counts as significant to the model's generality, capabilities or systemic risk. If the original compute is unknown, a fallback threshold based on the general criteria applies. Modest fine-tuning stays below the line.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://www.wilmerhale.com/en/insights/blogs/wilmerhale-privacy-and-cybersecurity-law/20250724-european-commission-issues-guidelines-for-providers-of-general-purpose-ai-models", "label": "WilmerHale, Commission guidelines for general-purpose AI providers, Jul 2025"}, {"url": "https://www.mayerbrown.com/en/insights/publications/2025/08/eu-ai-act-news-rules-on-general-purpose-ai-start-applying-guidelines-and-template-for-summary-of-training-data-finalized", "label": "Mayer Brown, EU AI Act rules on general-purpose AI start applying, Aug 2025"}, {"url": "https://cms.law/en/swe/legal-updates/general-purpose-ai-models-obligations-and-roles-for-providers-and-downstream-modifiers-under-the-eu-ai-act", "label": "CMS, providers and downstream modifiers under the EU AI Act, Jan 2026"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$67a92369-1330-52ef-a02a-cf4298888af7$ml$::uuid, $ml$12a09f68-408e-56cd-b317-8615ba520086$ml$::uuid, 2, $ml$Below the line$ml$, $ml$Modest fine-tuning that does not materially change what a model can do does not make you its provider. InstructGPT's RLHF step used under 2% of GPT-3's pretraining compute.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://cms.law/en/swe/legal-updates/general-purpose-ai-models-obligations-and-roles-for-providers-and-downstream-modifiers-under-the-eu-ai-act", "label": "CMS, providers and downstream modifiers under the EU AI Act, Jan 2026"}, {"url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/2203.02155", "label": "Ouyang et al., Training language models to follow instructions with human feedback, 2022"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$b3ce2f4a-16c4-58dc-8f56-f5f1a46a160f$ml$::uuid, $ml$12a09f68-408e-56cd-b317-8615ba520086$ml$::uuid, 3, $ml$Above the line$ml$, $ml$Cross it, and provider duties apply, but only to your modification. Your documentation, data summary and copyright policy cover only what you added.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/gpai-guidelines-overview/", "label": "artificialintelligenceact.eu, overview of the guidelines for GPAI models"}, {"url": "https://cms.law/en/swe/legal-updates/general-purpose-ai-models-obligations-and-roles-for-providers-and-downstream-modifiers-under-the-eu-ai-act", "label": "CMS, providers and downstream modifiers under the EU AI Act, Jan 2026"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$9fb2fe63-d6d6-5366-bf50-a14b8e925dcd$ml$::uuid, $ml$12a09f68-408e-56cd-b317-8615ba520086$ml$::uuid, 4, $ml$A model's terms bind fine-tuning too$ml$, $ml$Contracts can limit what you build from a model's outputs. OpenAI's terms prohibit developing models that compete with OpenAI. Partly for that reason, Stanford prohibited any commercial use of Alpaca.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://crfm.stanford.edu/2023/03/13/alpaca.html", "label": "Stanford CRFM, Alpaca: a strong, replicable instruction-following model, Mar 2023"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$7d5b2be0-a9bc-5eb5-99c5-c7f9c7bf4fe2$ml$::uuid, $ml$12a09f68-408e-56cd-b317-8615ba520086$ml$::uuid, 5, $ml$Know the one-third line$ml$, $ml$Fine-tuning with over a third of the original compute can make a company the provider in the EU. Legal and ML teams both need to know it.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://www.wilmerhale.com/en/insights/blogs/wilmerhale-privacy-and-cybersecurity-law/20250724-european-commission-issues-guidelines-for-providers-of-general-purpose-ai-models", "label": "WilmerHale, Commission guidelines for general-purpose AI providers, Jul 2025"}, {"url": "https://cms.law/en/swe/legal-updates/general-purpose-ai-models-obligations-and-roles-for-providers-and-downstream-modifiers-under-the-eu-ai-act", "label": "CMS, providers and downstream modifiers under the EU AI Act, Jan 2026"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$15b16a2e-e189-52e3-98b6-9da210e673b1$ml$::uuid, $ml$12a09f68-408e-56cd-b317-8615ba520086$ml$::uuid, 6, $ml$The rule$ml$, $ml$In the EU, how much you change a model decides whether its duties become yours.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://www.wilmerhale.com/en/insights/blogs/wilmerhale-privacy-and-cybersecurity-law/20250724-european-commission-issues-guidelines-for-providers-of-general-purpose-ai-models", "label": "WilmerHale, Commission guidelines for general-purpose AI providers, Jul 2025"}, {"url": "https://cms.law/en/swe/legal-updates/general-purpose-ai-models-obligations-and-roles-for-providers-and-downstream-modifiers-under-the-eu-ai-act", "label": "CMS, providers and downstream modifiers under the EU AI Act, Jan 2026"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$30151bd5-63c4-5843-865f-ceea904bec08$ml$::uuid, $ml$761e8937-30c3-580d-9aaa-3a7a7e0b857e$ml$::uuid, 1, $ml$Day 47 · The rule$ml$, $ml$Under EU guidelines from July 18, 2025, fine-tuning a general-purpose model with more than a third of its original training compute makes you its provider. The European Commission's guidelines on general-purpose AI treat this as an indicative rule of thumb under the AI Act. Such a change counts as significant to the model's generality, capabilities or systemic risk. If the original compute is unknown, a fallback threshold based on the general criteria applies. Modest fine-tuning stays below the line.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://www.wilmerhale.com/en/insights/blogs/wilmerhale-privacy-and-cybersecurity-law/20250724-european-commission-issues-guidelines-for-providers-of-general-purpose-ai-models", "label": "WilmerHale, Commission guidelines for general-purpose AI providers, Jul 2025"}, {"url": "https://www.mayerbrown.com/en/insights/publications/2025/08/eu-ai-act-news-rules-on-general-purpose-ai-start-applying-guidelines-and-template-for-summary-of-training-data-finalized", "label": "Mayer Brown, EU AI Act rules on general-purpose AI start applying, Aug 2025"}, {"url": "https://cms.law/en/swe/legal-updates/general-purpose-ai-models-obligations-and-roles-for-providers-and-downstream-modifiers-under-the-eu-ai-act", "label": "CMS, providers and downstream modifiers under the EU AI Act, Jan 2026"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$0ce3c3de-2937-597a-a365-efac4db2151d$ml$::uuid, $ml$761e8937-30c3-580d-9aaa-3a7a7e0b857e$ml$::uuid, 2, $ml$Below the line$ml$, $ml$Modest fine-tuning that does not materially change what a model can do does not make you its provider. InstructGPT's RLHF step used under 2% of GPT-3's pretraining compute.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://cms.law/en/swe/legal-updates/general-purpose-ai-models-obligations-and-roles-for-providers-and-downstream-modifiers-under-the-eu-ai-act", "label": "CMS, providers and downstream modifiers under the EU AI Act, Jan 2026"}, {"url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/2203.02155", "label": "Ouyang et al., Training language models to follow instructions with human feedback, 2022"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$67fa3adb-f09e-5668-a616-6f2c3fbae9c2$ml$::uuid, $ml$761e8937-30c3-580d-9aaa-3a7a7e0b857e$ml$::uuid, 3, $ml$Above the line$ml$, $ml$Cross it, and provider duties apply, but only to your modification. Your documentation, data summary and copyright policy cover only what you added.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/gpai-guidelines-overview/", "label": "artificialintelligenceact.eu, overview of the guidelines for GPAI models"}, {"url": "https://cms.law/en/swe/legal-updates/general-purpose-ai-models-obligations-and-roles-for-providers-and-downstream-modifiers-under-the-eu-ai-act", "label": "CMS, providers and downstream modifiers under the EU AI Act, Jan 2026"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$fbee7ef0-e505-5df5-baa6-e8788d335a7f$ml$::uuid, $ml$761e8937-30c3-580d-9aaa-3a7a7e0b857e$ml$::uuid, 4, $ml$A model's terms bind fine-tuning too$ml$, $ml$Contracts can limit what you build from a model's outputs. OpenAI's terms prohibit developing models that compete with OpenAI. Partly for that reason, Stanford prohibited any commercial use of Alpaca.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://crfm.stanford.edu/2023/03/13/alpaca.html", "label": "Stanford CRFM, Alpaca: a strong, replicable instruction-following model, Mar 2023"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$18cfa99e-9afc-5972-b65c-fcb8a5da6284$ml$::uuid, $ml$761e8937-30c3-580d-9aaa-3a7a7e0b857e$ml$::uuid, 5, $ml$Heavy tuning brings duties$ml$, $ml$A company that retrains a model deeply takes on provider duties in the EU, with fines up to €15 million or 3% of revenue.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://www.wilmerhale.com/en/insights/blogs/wilmerhale-privacy-and-cybersecurity-law/20250724-european-commission-issues-guidelines-for-providers-of-general-purpose-ai-models", "label": "WilmerHale, Commission guidelines for general-purpose AI providers, Jul 2025"}, {"url": "https://cms.law/en/swe/legal-updates/general-purpose-ai-models-obligations-and-roles-for-providers-and-downstream-modifiers-under-the-eu-ai-act", "label": "CMS, providers and downstream modifiers under the EU AI Act, Jan 2026"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$4fd52277-ea07-5aee-95de-519cdb7a4b13$ml$::uuid, $ml$761e8937-30c3-580d-9aaa-3a7a7e0b857e$ml$::uuid, 6, $ml$The rule$ml$, $ml$In the EU, how much you change a model decides whether its duties become yours.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://www.wilmerhale.com/en/insights/blogs/wilmerhale-privacy-and-cybersecurity-law/20250724-european-commission-issues-guidelines-for-providers-of-general-purpose-ai-models", "label": "WilmerHale, Commission guidelines for general-purpose AI providers, Jul 2025"}, {"url": "https://cms.law/en/swe/legal-updates/general-purpose-ai-models-obligations-and-roles-for-providers-and-downstream-modifiers-under-the-eu-ai-act", "label": "CMS, providers and downstream modifiers under the EU AI Act, Jan 2026"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$e9689692-5052-5066-8a41-3e6842534234$ml$::uuid, $ml$79d88960-a213-5bb0-99c4-02505cc488c9$ml$::uuid, 1, $ml$Day 47 · The rule$ml$, $ml$Under EU guidelines from July 18, 2025, fine-tuning a general-purpose model with more than a third of its original training compute makes you its provider. The European Commission's guidelines on general-purpose AI treat this as an indicative rule of thumb under the AI Act. Such a change counts as significant to the model's generality, capabilities or systemic risk. If the original compute is unknown, a fallback threshold based on the general criteria applies. Modest fine-tuning stays below the line.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://www.wilmerhale.com/en/insights/blogs/wilmerhale-privacy-and-cybersecurity-law/20250724-european-commission-issues-guidelines-for-providers-of-general-purpose-ai-models", "label": "WilmerHale, Commission guidelines for general-purpose AI providers, Jul 2025"}, {"url": "https://www.mayerbrown.com/en/insights/publications/2025/08/eu-ai-act-news-rules-on-general-purpose-ai-start-applying-guidelines-and-template-for-summary-of-training-data-finalized", "label": "Mayer Brown, EU AI Act rules on general-purpose AI start applying, Aug 2025"}, {"url": "https://cms.law/en/swe/legal-updates/general-purpose-ai-models-obligations-and-roles-for-providers-and-downstream-modifiers-under-the-eu-ai-act", "label": "CMS, providers and downstream modifiers under the EU AI Act, Jan 2026"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$8f99a3a4-a305-5d97-bd55-f4cad651493a$ml$::uuid, $ml$79d88960-a213-5bb0-99c4-02505cc488c9$ml$::uuid, 2, $ml$Below the line$ml$, $ml$Modest fine-tuning that does not materially change what a model can do does not make you its provider. InstructGPT's RLHF step used under 2% of GPT-3's pretraining compute.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://cms.law/en/swe/legal-updates/general-purpose-ai-models-obligations-and-roles-for-providers-and-downstream-modifiers-under-the-eu-ai-act", "label": "CMS, providers and downstream modifiers under the EU AI Act, Jan 2026"}, {"url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/2203.02155", "label": "Ouyang et al., Training language models to follow instructions with human feedback, 2022"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$35b8b203-a52b-5af7-b4ba-9c49ee6cde9b$ml$::uuid, $ml$79d88960-a213-5bb0-99c4-02505cc488c9$ml$::uuid, 3, $ml$Above the line$ml$, $ml$Cross it, and provider duties apply, but only to your modification. Your documentation, data summary and copyright policy cover only what you added.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/gpai-guidelines-overview/", "label": "artificialintelligenceact.eu, overview of the guidelines for GPAI models"}, {"url": "https://cms.law/en/swe/legal-updates/general-purpose-ai-models-obligations-and-roles-for-providers-and-downstream-modifiers-under-the-eu-ai-act", "label": "CMS, providers and downstream modifiers under the EU AI Act, Jan 2026"}]$ml$::jsonb),
