($ml$d0f986cb-fb91-5e96-9ae9-979e90af234a$ml$::uuid, $ml$88960dad-860f-5e53-acf7-9057bbafa26d$ml$::uuid, 5, $ml$Fine-tuning moves, it does not die$ml$, $ml$Google, Anthropic and open models with LoRA still serve custom tuning. Teams leaving OpenAI need a new home.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://tessl.io/blog/openai-shutting-fine-tuning-signals-for-enterprise-ai", "label": "Tessl, OpenAI shutting down fine-tuning, May 2026"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$ce2a7bb3-a9fc-5501-8989-cb199033d1e0$ml$::uuid, $ml$88960dad-860f-5e53-acf7-9057bbafa26d$ml$::uuid, 6, $ml$The rule$ml$, $ml$In AI, the better base models follow instructions, the less often fine-tuning is worth it.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://tessl.io/blog/openai-shutting-fine-tuning-signals-for-enterprise-ai", "label": "Tessl, OpenAI shutting down fine-tuning, May 2026"}, {"url": "https://openai.com/index/gpt-4o-fine-tuning/", "label": "OpenAI, Fine-tuning now available for GPT-4o, Aug 2024"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$d7ba532f-2c9b-5028-add7-c0774d26391a$ml$::uuid, $ml$ee31d259-5a87-5028-aae9-7f038612368e$ml$::uuid, 1, $ml$Day 48 · Where it breaks$ml$, $ml$In May 2026, OpenAI began winding down its fine-tuning platform. New training jobs end for every customer by January 2027. OpenAI said newer base models like GPT-5.5 are much better at following instructions and formats than earlier ones. It said prompt-based approaches are now cheaper and faster, and that it sees fewer use cases that require fine-tuning. Fine-tuned models keep running until their base models are deprecated.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://openai.com/index/gpt-4o-fine-tuning/", "label": "OpenAI, Fine-tuning now available for GPT-4o, Aug 2024"}, {"url": "https://tessl.io/blog/openai-shutting-fine-tuning-signals-for-enterprise-ai", "label": "Tessl, OpenAI shutting down fine-tuning, May 2026"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$7d3e0a22-f51d-5cdc-bc4e-b6e5a95af2a9$ml$::uuid, $ml$ee31d259-5a87-5028-aae9-7f038612368e$ml$::uuid, 2, $ml$Why it changed$ml$, $ml$Instruction following moved into the base model. A prompt can now do much of what a fine-tune did. OpenAI opened GPT-4o fine-tuning in August 2024 and closed it to new users in May 2026.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://openai.com/index/gpt-4o-fine-tuning/", "label": "OpenAI, Fine-tuning now available for GPT-4o, Aug 2024"}, {"url": "https://tessl.io/blog/openai-shutting-fine-tuning-signals-for-enterprise-ai", "label": "Tessl, OpenAI shutting down fine-tuning, May 2026"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$38ad371a-b3f4-51fd-abd9-e49df82c0a57$ml$::uuid, $ml$ee31d259-5a87-5028-aae9-7f038612368e$ml$::uuid, 3, $ml$Still broken$ml$, $ml$Feedback training still teaches models to tell users what they want to hear. A 2023 study found five leading AI assistants consistently sycophantic.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://huggingface.co/papers/2310.13548", "label": "Sharma et al., Towards understanding sycophancy in language models, Oct 2023"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$2ad3fffe-cfc4-54d8-a64e-f3a78f0de46f$ml$::uuid, $ml$ee31d259-5a87-5028-aae9-7f038612368e$ml$::uuid, 4, $ml$Fine-tuning moves elsewhere$ml$, $ml$Google and Anthropic still offer managed fine-tuning to enterprise customers. Open models can still be tuned with methods like LoRA. OpenAI cuts off customers who have not used a tuned model in 60 days.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://tessl.io/blog/openai-shutting-fine-tuning-signals-for-enterprise-ai", "label": "Tessl, OpenAI shutting down fine-tuning, May 2026"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$08d51404-234f-5baa-bd62-573981fa7483$ml$::uuid, $ml$ee31d259-5a87-5028-aae9-7f038612368e$ml$::uuid, 5, $ml$Models that just follow orders$ml$, $ml$Base models got good enough at following instructions that OpenAI is closing its fine-tuning service.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://tessl.io/blog/openai-shutting-fine-tuning-signals-for-enterprise-ai", "label": "Tessl, OpenAI shutting down fine-tuning, May 2026"}, {"url": "https://openai.com/index/gpt-4o-fine-tuning/", "label": "OpenAI, Fine-tuning now available for GPT-4o, Aug 2024"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$ae9f4dea-da7b-57b5-b63c-1bee06331e8f$ml$::uuid, $ml$ee31d259-5a87-5028-aae9-7f038612368e$ml$::uuid, 6, $ml$The rule$ml$, $ml$In AI, the better base models follow instructions, the less often fine-tuning is worth it.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://tessl.io/blog/openai-shutting-fine-tuning-signals-for-enterprise-ai", "label": "Tessl, OpenAI shutting down fine-tuning, May 2026"}, {"url": "https://openai.com/index/gpt-4o-fine-tuning/", "label": "OpenAI, Fine-tuning now available for GPT-4o, Aug 2024"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$03f85191-6c5a-57f1-9306-3e19bcafdc9d$ml$::uuid, $ml$064a33c3-ec6e-5902-a4b6-51a4bda6413d$ml$::uuid, 1, $ml$Day 49 · Prompts$ml$, $ml$In 2022, adding "Let's think step by step" to a prompt lifted a model's math score from 17.7% to 78.7%. Researchers tested OpenAI's InstructGPT model, text-davinci-002, on MultiArith, a set of arithmetic word problems. Asked directly, it got 17.7% right. Told to think step by step before answering, it got 78.7%. On the harder GSM8K set, accuracy rose from 10.4% to 40.7%. Nothing inside the model changed. Only the prompt did.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/2205.11916", "label": "Kojima et al., Large Language Models are Zero-Shot Reasoners, 2022"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$2c531395-1baf-5b0c-b4df-b79affe828b1$ml$::uuid, $ml$064a33c3-ec6e-5902-a4b6-51a4bda6413d$ml$::uuid, 2, $ml$What it is$ml$, $ml$Prompt engineering is choosing the words, examples and order of what you send a model. It changes the input, never the model itself.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2005.14165", "label": "Brown et al., Language models are few-shot learners, 2020"}, {"url": "https://openai.com/index/instruction-following/", "label": "OpenAI, InstructGPT"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$e023c11c-2ef6-57bb-b33d-e3794dc28130$ml$::uuid, $ml$064a33c3-ec6e-5902-a4b6-51a4bda6413d$ml$::uuid, 3, $ml$Zero or few shots$ml$, $ml$A zero-shot prompt gives only an instruction. A few-shot prompt adds worked examples. Eight worked examples put Google's PaLM 540B at the top of a math benchmark in 2022.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2005.14165", "label": "Brown et al., Language models are few-shot learners, 2020"}, {"url": "https://ar5iv.arxiv.org/html/2201.11903", "label": "Wei et al. (Google), Chain-of-thought prompting elicits reasoning, 2022"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$babd007e-69da-51a1-ba8d-8b88ea14ff0e$ml$::uuid, $ml$064a33c3-ec6e-5902-a4b6-51a4bda6413d$ml$::uuid, 4, $ml$Everything must fit in the window$ml$, $ml$The context window is the most text a model can take in at once. GPT-3's window, in 2020, was 2,048 tokens. Meta said its Llama 4 Scout could take 10 million tokens in April 2025.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2005.14165", "label": "Brown et al., Language models are few-shot learners, 2020"}, {"url": "https://ai.meta.com/blog/llama-4-multimodal-intelligence/", "label": "Meta, The Llama 4 herd, Apr 2025"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$4ffefac6-a374-5e1f-8853-951259071488$ml$::uuid, $ml$064a33c3-ec6e-5902-a4b6-51a4bda6413d$ml$::uuid, 5, $ml$Separate prompting from training$ml$, $ml$Say whether a fix changes the prompt or the model. A prompt change needs no new training run, so it is the first thing to try.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2005.14165", "label": "Brown et al., Language models are few-shot learners, 2020"}, {"url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/2205.11916", "label": "Kojima et al., Large Language Models are Zero-Shot Reasoners, 2022"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$ccd1a946-0feb-56ad-8017-036066022477$ml$::uuid, $ml$064a33c3-ec6e-5902-a4b6-51a4bda6413d$ml$::uuid, 6, $ml$The rule$ml$, $ml$In AI, the prompt is the cheapest lever: it changes the answer without changing the model.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/2205.11916", "label": "Kojima et al., Large Language Models are Zero-Shot Reasoners, 2022"}, {"url": "https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2005.14165", "label": "Brown et al., Language models are few-shot learners, 2020"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$0613d726-c5ce-5ae4-a314-620ec491d2bd$ml$::uuid, $ml$4054fc11-3015-5f97-8b2e-c194d7193c7f$ml$::uuid, 1, $ml$Day 49 · Prompts$ml$, $ml$In 2022, adding "Let's think step by step" to a prompt lifted a model's math score from 17.7% to 78.7%. Researchers tested OpenAI's InstructGPT model, text-davinci-002, on MultiArith, a set of arithmetic word problems. Asked directly, it got 17.7% right. Told to think step by step before answering, it got 78.7%. On the harder GSM8K set, accuracy rose from 10.4% to 40.7%. Nothing inside the model changed. Only the prompt did.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/2205.11916", "label": "Kojima et al., Large Language Models are Zero-Shot Reasoners, 2022"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$66366035-57c6-5bb1-99ab-c5f65decb3d5$ml$::uuid, $ml$4054fc11-3015-5f97-8b2e-c194d7193c7f$ml$::uuid, 2, $ml$What it is$ml$, $ml$Prompt engineering is choosing the words, examples and order of what you send a model. It changes the input, never the model itself.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2005.14165", "label": "Brown et al., Language models are few-shot learners, 2020"}, {"url": "https://openai.com/index/instruction-following/", "label": "OpenAI, InstructGPT"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$abb1272b-899d-589b-a5e5-cc268e99bee4$ml$::uuid, $ml$4054fc11-3015-5f97-8b2e-c194d7193c7f$ml$::uuid, 3, $ml$Zero or few shots$ml$, $ml$A zero-shot prompt gives only an instruction. A few-shot prompt adds worked examples. Eight worked examples put Google's PaLM 540B at the top of a math benchmark in 2022.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2005.14165", "label": "Brown et al., Language models are few-shot learners, 2020"}, {"url": "https://ar5iv.arxiv.org/html/2201.11903", "label": "Wei et al. (Google), Chain-of-thought prompting elicits reasoning, 2022"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$03e4965d-0877-5c46-9bf8-dfd8024cb877$ml$::uuid, $ml$4054fc11-3015-5f97-8b2e-c194d7193c7f$ml$::uuid, 4, $ml$Everything must fit in the window$ml$, $ml$The context window is the most text a model can take in at once. GPT-3's window, in 2020, was 2,048 tokens. Meta said its Llama 4 Scout could take 10 million tokens in April 2025.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2005.14165", "label": "Brown et al., Language models are few-shot learners, 2020"}, {"url": "https://ai.meta.com/blog/llama-4-multimodal-intelligence/", "label": "Meta, The Llama 4 herd, Apr 2025"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$3208dba5-0481-5203-ab6c-ebdd8840d263$ml$::uuid, $ml$4054fc11-3015-5f97-8b2e-c194d7193c7f$ml$::uuid, 5, $ml$Wording is easy to copy$ml$, $ml$A better prompt needs no training compute, so a rival can match it quickly. Look for advantages that outlast the wording.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2005.14165", "label": "Brown et al., Language models are few-shot learners, 2020"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$02b8c2fa-92df-5087-aaae-84f1fd11785e$ml$::uuid, $ml$4054fc11-3015-5f97-8b2e-c194d7193c7f$ml$::uuid, 6, $ml$The rule$ml$, $ml$In AI, the prompt is the cheapest lever: it changes the answer without changing the model.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/2205.11916", "label": "Kojima et al., Large Language Models are Zero-Shot Reasoners, 2022"}, {"url": "https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2005.14165", "label": "Brown et al., Language models are few-shot learners, 2020"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$779bcb11-dd10-590b-a804-677deaf2fe9e$ml$::uuid, $ml$28b60444-ebbb-5183-b3c6-9fe1c5198339$ml$::uuid, 1, $ml$Day 49 · Prompts$ml$, $ml$In 2022, adding "Let's think step by step" to a prompt lifted a model's math score from 17.7% to 78.7%. Researchers tested OpenAI's InstructGPT model, text-davinci-002, on MultiArith, a set of arithmetic word problems. Asked directly, it got 17.7% right. Told to think step by step before answering, it got 78.7%. On the harder GSM8K set, accuracy rose from 10.4% to 40.7%. Nothing inside the model changed. Only the prompt did.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/2205.11916", "label": "Kojima et al., Large Language Models are Zero-Shot Reasoners, 2022"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$a0bf4ab3-d1b0-561f-ac7e-1b7053c1ee88$ml$::uuid, $ml$28b60444-ebbb-5183-b3c6-9fe1c5198339$ml$::uuid, 2, $ml$What it is$ml$, $ml$Prompt engineering is choosing the words, examples and order of what you send a model. It changes the input, never the model itself.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2005.14165", "label": "Brown et al., Language models are few-shot learners, 2020"}, {"url": "https://openai.com/index/instruction-following/", "label": "OpenAI, InstructGPT"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$c0e088e6-0673-5084-afee-5feff86fb458$ml$::uuid, $ml$28b60444-ebbb-5183-b3c6-9fe1c5198339$ml$::uuid, 3, $ml$Zero or few shots$ml$, $ml$A zero-shot prompt gives only an instruction. A few-shot prompt adds worked examples. Eight worked examples put Google's PaLM 540B at the top of a math benchmark in 2022.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2005.14165", "label": "Brown et al., Language models are few-shot learners, 2020"}, {"url": "https://ar5iv.arxiv.org/html/2201.11903", "label": "Wei et al. (Google), Chain-of-thought prompting elicits reasoning, 2022"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$806750dc-4f9c-5ca5-83b2-5e39b62005b8$ml$::uuid, $ml$28b60444-ebbb-5183-b3c6-9fe1c5198339$ml$::uuid, 4, $ml$Everything must fit in the window$ml$, $ml$The context window is the most text a model can take in at once. GPT-3's window, in 2020, was 2,048 tokens. Meta said its Llama 4 Scout could take 10 million tokens in April 2025.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2005.14165", "label": "Brown et al., Language models are few-shot learners, 2020"}, {"url": "https://ai.meta.com/blog/llama-4-multimodal-intelligence/", "label": "Meta, The Llama 4 herd, Apr 2025"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$6cd7a5f8-5b5e-5bce-9f16-af47fb2ae465$ml$::uuid, $ml$28b60444-ebbb-5183-b3c6-9fe1c5198339$ml$::uuid, 5, $ml$Test the prompt before the model$ml$, $ml$Five words lifted one score from 17.7% to 78.7%. Try better prompts before paying to train anything.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/2205.11916", "label": "Kojima et al., Large Language Models are Zero-Shot Reasoners, 2022"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$83efe0ab-5070-5bf6-96cd-87211abf8ac6$ml$::uuid, $ml$28b60444-ebbb-5183-b3c6-9fe1c5198339$ml$::uuid, 6, $ml$The rule$ml$, $ml$In AI, the prompt is the cheapest lever: it changes the answer without changing the model.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/2205.11916", "label": "Kojima et al., Large Language Models are Zero-Shot Reasoners, 2022"}, {"url": "https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2005.14165", "label": "Brown et al., Language models are few-shot learners, 2020"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$2c32b34c-5f8d-564a-b117-e2aca4f1626a$ml$::uuid, $ml$1d66fbe9-6b0e-5617-8e52-622b8721706a$ml$::uuid, 1, $ml$Day 49 · Prompts$ml$, $ml$In 2022, adding "Let's think step by step" to a prompt lifted a model's math score from 17.7% to 78.7%. Researchers tested OpenAI's InstructGPT model, text-davinci-002, on MultiArith, a set of arithmetic word problems. Asked directly, it got 17.7% right. Told to think step by step before answering, it got 78.7%. On the harder GSM8K set, accuracy rose from 10.4% to 40.7%. Nothing inside the model changed. Only the prompt did.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/2205.11916", "label": "Kojima et al., Large Language Models are Zero-Shot Reasoners, 2022"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$3faa048b-b554-5518-afbc-75a5286b7088$ml$::uuid, $ml$1d66fbe9-6b0e-5617-8e52-622b8721706a$ml$::uuid, 2, $ml$What it is$ml$, $ml$Prompt engineering is choosing the words, examples and order of what you send a model. It changes the input, never the model itself.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2005.14165", "label": "Brown et al., Language models are few-shot learners, 2020"}, {"url": "https://openai.com/index/instruction-following/", "label": "OpenAI, InstructGPT"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$5cb3a640-f3ac-583b-8863-8c0f790b93da$ml$::uuid, $ml$1d66fbe9-6b0e-5617-8e52-622b8721706a$ml$::uuid, 3, $ml$Zero or few shots$ml$, $ml$A zero-shot prompt gives only an instruction. A few-shot prompt adds worked examples. Eight worked examples put Google's PaLM 540B at the top of a math benchmark in 2022.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2005.14165", "label": "Brown et al., Language models are few-shot learners, 2020"}, {"url": "https://ar5iv.arxiv.org/html/2201.11903", "label": "Wei et al. (Google), Chain-of-thought prompting elicits reasoning, 2022"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$c1b93cb1-029d-5b67-954a-ca955e624d54$ml$::uuid, $ml$1d66fbe9-6b0e-5617-8e52-622b8721706a$ml$::uuid, 4, $ml$Everything must fit in the window$ml$, $ml$The context window is the most text a model can take in at once. GPT-3's window, in 2020, was 2,048 tokens. Meta said its Llama 4 Scout could take 10 million tokens in April 2025.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2005.14165", "label": "Brown et al., Language models are few-shot learners, 2020"}, {"url": "https://ai.meta.com/blog/llama-4-multimodal-intelligence/", "label": "Meta, The Llama 4 herd, Apr 2025"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$029c8946-f517-55c2-8c83-59017d8a9021$ml$::uuid, $ml$1d66fbe9-6b0e-5617-8e52-622b8721706a$ml$::uuid, 5, $ml$How you ask shapes the answer$ml$, $ml$Told to think step by step, one model solved more than four times as many math problems. Asking well is a real skill.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/2205.11916", "label": "Kojima et al., Large Language Models are Zero-Shot Reasoners, 2022"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$73660181-1828-589b-8587-b6d94a3246fa$ml$::uuid, $ml$1d66fbe9-6b0e-5617-8e52-622b8721706a$ml$::uuid, 6, $ml$The rule$ml$, $ml$In AI, the prompt is the cheapest lever: it changes the answer without changing the model.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/2205.11916", "label": "Kojima et al., Large Language Models are Zero-Shot Reasoners, 2022"}, {"url": "https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2005.14165", "label": "Brown et al., Language models are few-shot learners, 2020"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$59ae5659-1f42-5310-a6a7-eb0d81e3b0a5$ml$::uuid, $ml$25478359-a5a2-5224-9dea-7a50ecf86c7d$ml$::uuid, 1, $ml$Day 50 · The money$ml$, $ml$In August 2024, Anthropic said caching a long prompt could cut its cost by up to 90%. Apps often send the same long instructions or documents with every request, and every token is billed each time. Prompt caching, which Anthropic launched on August 14, 2024, stores that repeated part. In its example of chatting with a 100,000-token book, the cost fell 90% and the wait for the first word of the answer went from 11.5 to 2.4 seconds.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://claude.com/resources/articles/prompt-caching", "label": "Anthropic, Prompt caching with Claude"}, {"url": "https://www.maginative.com/article/anthropics-new-prompt-caching-feature-can-cut-costs-up-to-90/", "label": "Maginative, Anthropic prompt caching can cut costs up to 90%, Aug 2024"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$30261d06-5666-5884-820e-1abcf51dc76c$ml$::uuid, $ml$25478359-a5a2-5224-9dea-7a50ecf86c7d$ml$::uuid, 2, $ml$The meter$ml$, $ml$Model makers bill developers per token, and output costs more than input. Claude Sonnet 4.6 costs $3 per million input tokens and $15 per million output tokens.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/about-claude/pricing", "label": "Anthropic, Claude API pricing, Nov 2026"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$fc0fa20e-7910-5120-9733-6edaf7c4b670$ml$::uuid, $ml$25478359-a5a2-5224-9dea-7a50ecf86c7d$ml$::uuid, 3, $ml$The discount$ml$, $ml$Reading a cached prompt costs a tenth of the normal input price on most Claude models. A five-minute cache write costs 1.25 times, so it pays off after one reuse.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/about-claude/pricing", "label": "Anthropic, Claude API pricing, Nov 2026"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$89939bc5-0bdd-57d5-a0bc-5f94fbdecf63$ml$::uuid, $ml$25478359-a5a2-5224-9dea-7a50ecf86c7d$ml$::uuid, 4, $ml$Every repeated token is a cost line$ml$, $ml$An app pays for its instructions on every call. Caching and a 50% batch discount turn prompt design into a margin decision. OpenAI made caching automatic in October 2024, at half price on prompts over 1,024 tokens.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/about-claude/pricing", "label": "Anthropic, Claude API pricing, Nov 2026"}, {"url": "https://openai.com/index/api-prompt-caching", "label": "OpenAI, Prompt caching in the API, Oct 2024"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$1e6c252f-c7c9-514f-acda-d55a749af016$ml$::uuid, $ml$25478359-a5a2-5224-9dea-7a50ecf86c7d$ml$::uuid, 5, $ml$Know what a token costs$ml$, $ml$Quote one real price, like $3 in and $15 out per million tokens. It shows you think about what each answer costs.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/about-claude/pricing", "label": "Anthropic, Claude API pricing, Nov 2026"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$f119d66c-f1b5-536f-8177-599055bf64d3$ml$::uuid, $ml$25478359-a5a2-5224-9dea-7a50ecf86c7d$ml$::uuid, 6, $ml$The rule$ml$, $ml$In AI apps, every token you resend is billed again, so reuse is where the margin hides.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/about-claude/pricing", "label": "Anthropic, Claude API pricing, Nov 2026"}, {"url": "https://claude.com/resources/articles/prompt-caching", "label": "Anthropic, Prompt caching with Claude"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$cef26275-3290-5245-9cd0-e06be6315232$ml$::uuid, $ml$323b7088-6e22-57e4-8c2f-77d22084bec4$ml$::uuid, 1, $ml$Day 50 · The money$ml$, $ml$In August 2024, Anthropic said caching a long prompt could cut its cost by up to 90%. Apps often send the same long instructions or documents with every request, and every token is billed each time. Prompt caching, which Anthropic launched on August 14, 2024, stores that repeated part. In its example of chatting with a 100,000-token book, the cost fell 90% and the wait for the first word of the answer went from 11.5 to 2.4 seconds.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://claude.com/resources/articles/prompt-caching", "label": "Anthropic, Prompt caching with Claude"}, {"url": "https://www.maginative.com/article/anthropics-new-prompt-caching-feature-can-cut-costs-up-to-90/", "label": "Maginative, Anthropic prompt caching can cut costs up to 90%, Aug 2024"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$118b5b61-d5eb-563f-ac43-8d8bd9f6f327$ml$::uuid, $ml$323b7088-6e22-57e4-8c2f-77d22084bec4$ml$::uuid, 2, $ml$The meter$ml$, $ml$Model makers bill developers per token, and output costs more than input. Claude Sonnet 4.6 costs $3 per million input tokens and $15 per million output tokens.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/about-claude/pricing", "label": "Anthropic, Claude API pricing, Nov 2026"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$2ec44fbc-2a71-5423-a6b8-3ee2ab386a19$ml$::uuid, $ml$323b7088-6e22-57e4-8c2f-77d22084bec4$ml$::uuid, 3, $ml$The discount$ml$, $ml$Reading a cached prompt costs a tenth of the normal input price on most Claude models. A five-minute cache write costs 1.25 times, so it pays off after one reuse.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/about-claude/pricing", "label": "Anthropic, Claude API pricing, Nov 2026"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$6239c033-11f1-5712-be31-fee634a73435$ml$::uuid, $ml$323b7088-6e22-57e4-8c2f-77d22084bec4$ml$::uuid, 4, $ml$Every repeated token is a cost line$ml$, $ml$An app pays for its instructions on every call. Caching and a 50% batch discount turn prompt design into a margin decision. OpenAI made caching automatic in October 2024, at half price on prompts over 1,024 tokens.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/about-claude/pricing", "label": "Anthropic, Claude API pricing, Nov 2026"}, {"url": "https://openai.com/index/api-prompt-caching", "label": "OpenAI, Prompt caching in the API, Oct 2024"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$44b36a65-df70-57f3-9f16-5a99a4a4c7ba$ml$::uuid, $ml$323b7088-6e22-57e4-8c2f-77d22084bec4$ml$::uuid, 5, $ml$Caching shifts the margin$ml$, $ml$Repeated prompt text billed at a tenth of the price changes unit economics. Ask how much of a product's traffic can be cached.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/about-claude/pricing", "label": "Anthropic, Claude API pricing, Nov 2026"}]$ml$::jsonb),
