($ml$3132b495-deda-5351-8889-efc9aebacaa0$ml$::uuid, $ml$466a7650-13ab-5d94-9036-8309158c9808$ml$::uuid, 1, $ml$Day 54 · Where it breaks$ml$, $ml$In 2025, a Microsoft survey of 31,000 workers in 31 countries ranked prompt engineer second from the bottom of new roles companies planned to add. Two years earlier, prompt engineers had been among the hottest hires in AI, with salaries of up to $200,000. The survey put AI coding, AI data interpretation and AI security at the top instead. The work did not vanish. In September 2025, Anthropic described context engineering, curating everything a model sees, as the natural progression of prompt engineering.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://www.kornferry.com/insights/this-week-in-leadership/the-hottest-job-of-the-year-goes-cold", "label": "Korn Ferry, The hottest job of the year goes cold, Apr 2025"}, {"url": "https://anthropic.com/research/effective-context-engineering-for-ai-agents", "label": "Anthropic, Effective context engineering for AI agents, Sep 2025"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$dc53fa01-aff7-539c-88ff-1251adb90582$ml$::uuid, $ml$466a7650-13ab-5d94-9036-8309158c9808$ml$::uuid, 2, $ml$The new name$ml$, $ml$Context engineering is finding the smallest set of high-signal tokens for a model to see. Anthropic calls it the natural progression of prompt engineering.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://anthropic.com/research/effective-context-engineering-for-ai-agents", "label": "Anthropic, Effective context engineering for AI agents, Sep 2025"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$8d68c5bd-cde4-520c-b52f-2ca21ac3c58c$ml$::uuid, $ml$466a7650-13ab-5d94-9036-8309158c9808$ml$::uuid, 3, $ml$Where it breaks$ml$, $ml$Longer inputs make models less reliable, even on simple tasks. Chroma tested 18 models in July 2025 and named the effect context rot.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://trychroma.com/research/context-rot", "label": "Chroma, Context Rot technical report, Jul 2025"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$34047ca2-f934-5884-b39f-0af02843659e$ml$::uuid, $ml$466a7650-13ab-5d94-9036-8309158c9808$ml$::uuid, 4, $ml$Agents now manage their own context$ml$, $ml$They summarize old turns, keep notes outside the window and hand side tasks to sub-agents. Each keeps the window lean. Sub-agents return short summaries instead of all their work.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://anthropic.com/research/effective-context-engineering-for-ai-agents", "label": "Anthropic, Effective context engineering for AI agents, Sep 2025"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$6624f81e-1b8e-5794-82cd-d0c0aad0c234$ml$::uuid, $ml$466a7650-13ab-5d94-9036-8309158c9808$ml$::uuid, 5, $ml$Test a context tool$ml$, $ml$Agents need summaries, notes and retrieval to stay sharp. Ask teams where long runs break, and test a fix there.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://anthropic.com/research/effective-context-engineering-for-ai-agents", "label": "Anthropic, Effective context engineering for AI agents, Sep 2025"}, {"url": "https://trychroma.com/research/context-rot", "label": "Chroma, Context Rot technical report, Jul 2025"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$9a9bff67-54ff-5447-aac1-8c7eeb6cdb82$ml$::uuid, $ml$466a7650-13ab-5d94-9036-8309158c9808$ml$::uuid, 6, $ml$The rule$ml$, $ml$In AI, the frontier skill is not wording the prompt but choosing what the model sees.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://anthropic.com/research/effective-context-engineering-for-ai-agents", "label": "Anthropic, Effective context engineering for AI agents, Sep 2025"}, {"url": "https://trychroma.com/research/context-rot", "label": "Chroma, Context Rot technical report, Jul 2025"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$7b27b285-f5c4-543c-aa94-29d15b446aa2$ml$::uuid, $ml$bd9c6627-b00f-5d86-8e78-15405b80c530$ml$::uuid, 1, $ml$Day 54 · Where it breaks$ml$, $ml$In 2025, a Microsoft survey of 31,000 workers in 31 countries ranked prompt engineer second from the bottom of new roles companies planned to add. Two years earlier, prompt engineers had been among the hottest hires in AI, with salaries of up to $200,000. The survey put AI coding, AI data interpretation and AI security at the top instead. The work did not vanish. In September 2025, Anthropic described context engineering, curating everything a model sees, as the natural progression of prompt engineering.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://www.kornferry.com/insights/this-week-in-leadership/the-hottest-job-of-the-year-goes-cold", "label": "Korn Ferry, The hottest job of the year goes cold, Apr 2025"}, {"url": "https://anthropic.com/research/effective-context-engineering-for-ai-agents", "label": "Anthropic, Effective context engineering for AI agents, Sep 2025"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$83f1e093-1506-59a8-8695-332857a19df6$ml$::uuid, $ml$bd9c6627-b00f-5d86-8e78-15405b80c530$ml$::uuid, 2, $ml$The new name$ml$, $ml$Context engineering is finding the smallest set of high-signal tokens for a model to see. Anthropic calls it the natural progression of prompt engineering.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://anthropic.com/research/effective-context-engineering-for-ai-agents", "label": "Anthropic, Effective context engineering for AI agents, Sep 2025"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$6f1e8399-ac98-54e9-a024-4ecc2ad27d36$ml$::uuid, $ml$bd9c6627-b00f-5d86-8e78-15405b80c530$ml$::uuid, 3, $ml$Where it breaks$ml$, $ml$Longer inputs make models less reliable, even on simple tasks. Chroma tested 18 models in July 2025 and named the effect context rot.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://trychroma.com/research/context-rot", "label": "Chroma, Context Rot technical report, Jul 2025"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$aa29afe4-32d3-5482-a3e1-783065d05b10$ml$::uuid, $ml$bd9c6627-b00f-5d86-8e78-15405b80c530$ml$::uuid, 4, $ml$Agents now manage their own context$ml$, $ml$They summarize old turns, keep notes outside the window and hand side tasks to sub-agents. Each keeps the window lean. Sub-agents return short summaries instead of all their work.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://anthropic.com/research/effective-context-engineering-for-ai-agents", "label": "Anthropic, Effective context engineering for AI agents, Sep 2025"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$d72e52bc-67bf-5355-95c3-9baace747fdc$ml$::uuid, $ml$bd9c6627-b00f-5d86-8e78-15405b80c530$ml$::uuid, 5, $ml$Less can be more$ml$, $ml$Feeding a model more text can make it worse. The skill now is choosing what to leave out.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://trychroma.com/research/context-rot", "label": "Chroma, Context Rot technical report, Jul 2025"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$46a6885c-09d5-59a1-8b1c-3b1aaac4b81f$ml$::uuid, $ml$bd9c6627-b00f-5d86-8e78-15405b80c530$ml$::uuid, 6, $ml$The rule$ml$, $ml$In AI, the frontier skill is not wording the prompt but choosing what the model sees.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://anthropic.com/research/effective-context-engineering-for-ai-agents", "label": "Anthropic, Effective context engineering for AI agents, Sep 2025"}, {"url": "https://trychroma.com/research/context-rot", "label": "Chroma, Context Rot technical report, Jul 2025"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$1e5966f1-3246-5c08-ab63-acdbde5a579e$ml$::uuid, $ml$a234b476-f069-50d4-8895-d9539c2077cc$ml$::uuid, 1, $ml$Day 55 · Open vs closed$ml$, $ml$In August 2025, OpenAI released gpt-oss, its first open-weight language models since GPT-2. On August 5, 2025, OpenAI put two models on Hugging Face for anyone to download, under the Apache 2.0 license. The larger, gpt-oss-120b, has 117 billion parameters and runs within 80GB of memory, so a single 80GB GPU can host it. OpenAI described the training data only in general terms: mostly English text on science, coding and general knowledge.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://openai.com/index/introducing-gpt-oss/", "label": "OpenAI, Introducing gpt-oss, Aug 2025"}, {"url": "https://the-decoder.com/openai-releases-its-first-open-weight-language-models-since-gpt-2-with-gpt-oss/", "label": "The Decoder, OpenAI releases its first open-weight models since GPT-2, Aug 2025"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$1e5eca7e-4319-5d78-bb2e-3a8d297343c5$ml$::uuid, $ml$a234b476-f069-50d4-8895-d9539c2077cc$ml$::uuid, 2, $ml$Open weights$ml$, $ml$An open-weight model lets you download its trained parameters and run it on your own machines. The smaller gpt-oss-20b runs within 16GB of memory.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://openai.com/index/introducing-gpt-oss/", "label": "OpenAI, Introducing gpt-oss, Aug 2025"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$31d5c9cf-cc5f-502e-b9eb-1374dc78108b$ml$::uuid, $ml$a234b476-f069-50d4-8895-d9539c2077cc$ml$::uuid, 3, $ml$Open source$ml$, $ml$Open source asks for more: details of the training data, the full code and the weights, so others can rebuild the model. By the test the Open Source Initiative set in October 2024, Meta's Llama falls short.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://siliconangle.com/2024/10/28/osi-clarifies-makes-ai-systems-open-source-open-models-fall-short/", "label": "SiliconANGLE, OSI defines open source AI and open models fall short, Oct 2024"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$de7aff78-77b5-5cf6-9212-6a774fcc68bf$ml$::uuid, $ml$a234b476-f069-50d4-8895-d9539c2077cc$ml$::uuid, 4, $ml$Closed models are rented, not owned$ml$, $ml$You reach a closed model through an app or an API, and the weights stay with the lab. Meta launched Muse Spark this way in April 2026. Muse Spark had no download, only the Meta AI app and an API preview for partners.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://www.artificialintelligence-news.com/news/meta-muse-spark-ai-model-open-source/", "label": "AI News, Meta launches Muse Spark as a proprietary model, Apr 2026"}, {"url": "https://www.deeplearning.ai/the-batch/with-muse-spark-meta-pivots-away-from-its-open-weights-llama-strategy", "label": "The Batch (DeepLearning.AI), With Muse Spark, Meta pivots away from open weights, Apr 2026"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$cde88a0d-5223-522d-851c-e5a8cc2dba59$ml$::uuid, $ml$a234b476-f069-50d4-8895-d9539c2077cc$ml$::uuid, 5, $ml$Use the three words precisely$ml$, $ml$Say open weights, open source or closed, and mean it. By the 2024 definition, calling Llama open source is wrong.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://siliconangle.com/2024/10/28/osi-clarifies-makes-ai-systems-open-source-open-models-fall-short/", "label": "SiliconANGLE, OSI defines open source AI and open models fall short, Oct 2024"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$b11b2820-3a1e-5ab5-8241-a67d013173a2$ml$::uuid, $ml$a234b476-f069-50d4-8895-d9539c2077cc$ml$::uuid, 6, $ml$The rule$ml$, $ml$In AI, open weights let you run a model; open source lets you rebuild it.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://siliconangle.com/2024/10/28/osi-clarifies-makes-ai-systems-open-source-open-models-fall-short/", "label": "SiliconANGLE, OSI defines open source AI and open models fall short, Oct 2024"}, {"url": "https://openai.com/index/introducing-gpt-oss/", "label": "OpenAI, Introducing gpt-oss, Aug 2025"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$31de6eac-6866-59be-926b-3322b8d39b69$ml$::uuid, $ml$eaa7605b-42a5-5038-8f6e-b1cea420d7aa$ml$::uuid, 1, $ml$Day 55 · Open vs closed$ml$, $ml$In August 2025, OpenAI released gpt-oss, its first open-weight language models since GPT-2. On August 5, 2025, OpenAI put two models on Hugging Face for anyone to download, under the Apache 2.0 license. The larger, gpt-oss-120b, has 117 billion parameters and runs within 80GB of memory, so a single 80GB GPU can host it. OpenAI described the training data only in general terms: mostly English text on science, coding and general knowledge.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://openai.com/index/introducing-gpt-oss/", "label": "OpenAI, Introducing gpt-oss, Aug 2025"}, {"url": "https://the-decoder.com/openai-releases-its-first-open-weight-language-models-since-gpt-2-with-gpt-oss/", "label": "The Decoder, OpenAI releases its first open-weight models since GPT-2, Aug 2025"}]$ml$::jsonb),
