($ml$9d63c80e-b52c-5e3f-b763-c2a9aea78806$ml$::uuid, $ml$a95c2d04-bd11-521b-92c0-c1704a0413d7$ml$::uuid, 5, $ml$Plan for open to catch up$ml$, $ml$Open models trail the best closed ones by about four months. What needs a top closed model today may run on open weights soon.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://epoch.ai/data-insights/open-closed-eci-gap", "label": "Epoch AI, the open and closed capability gap, May 2026"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$5d251eda-5305-5777-9ffc-3ac8513f39fa$ml$::uuid, $ml$a95c2d04-bd11-521b-92c0-c1704a0413d7$ml$::uuid, 6, $ml$The rule$ml$, $ml$In AI, the open gap is measured in months, and it resets with each closed release.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://epoch.ai/data-insights/open-closed-eci-gap", "label": "Epoch AI, the open and closed capability gap, May 2026"}, {"url": "https://hai.stanford.edu/assets/files/ai_index_report_2026_chapter_2_technical.pdf", "label": "Stanford AI Index 2026, chapter 2, technical performance"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$01aec42e-560c-5f19-a1a6-66d249aff07c$ml$::uuid, $ml$29ee7a26-f1ee-5490-befa-427c7af1ff3d$ml$::uuid, 1, $ml$Day 58 · The numbers$ml$, $ml$In May 2023, the best closed model beat the best open one by 15.2% on a chat leaderboard. By August 2024 the gap was 0.5%. Stanford's 2026 AI Index tracks the top open-weight and closed models on the Arena Leaderboard, where people vote between answers. GPT-4 led Vicuna-13B by 174 points in May 2023. Releases like Llama 3.1 405B cut the gap to 7 points by August 2024. Then new closed models pulled ahead: by March 2026 the gap was 49 points, or 3.4%.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://hai.stanford.edu/assets/files/ai_index_report_2026_chapter_2_technical.pdf", "label": "Stanford AI Index 2026, chapter 2, technical performance"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$1ebc7890-ef0e-5721-a14b-b43099a629ab$ml$::uuid, $ml$29ee7a26-f1ee-5490-befa-427c7af1ff3d$ml$::uuid, 2, $ml$Today$ml$, $ml$In March 2026 the top closed model led the top open one, GLM-5, by 49 points. Six of the top ten models on the leaderboard were closed.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://hai.stanford.edu/assets/files/ai_index_report_2026_chapter_2_technical.pdf", "label": "Stanford AI Index 2026, chapter 2, technical performance"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$d097d028-ed36-5d4c-bc18-59f22cb5413d$ml$::uuid, $ml$29ee7a26-f1ee-5490-befa-427c7af1ff3d$ml$::uuid, 3, $ml$In months$ml$, $ml$Epoch AI measures the gap in time. Since January 2026, open models have trailed the best closed ones by about four months. That equals about 8 points on its capability index.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://epoch.ai/data-insights/open-closed-eci-gap", "label": "Epoch AI, the open and closed capability gap, May 2026"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$aaf47dc6-c311-56d5-a086-b662611cff89$ml$::uuid, $ml$29ee7a26-f1ee-5490-befa-427c7af1ff3d$ml$::uuid, 4, $ml$Each big closed release reopens the gap$ml$, $ml$Open models catch up, then a new closed model jumps ahead. The AI Index describes open systems closing in, then falling behind. DeepSeek-R1 once trailed OpenAI's o1 by just 5 points.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://hai.stanford.edu/assets/files/ai_index_report_2026_chapter_2_technical.pdf", "label": "Stanford AI Index 2026, chapter 2, technical performance"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$79342211-b52f-59f2-9972-fb73ce7ae41c$ml$::uuid, $ml$29ee7a26-f1ee-5490-befa-427c7af1ff3d$ml$::uuid, 5, $ml$A race that keeps resetting$ml$, $ml$Open models nearly caught up in 2024, then fell behind again. Each new closed release moves the finish line.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://hai.stanford.edu/assets/files/ai_index_report_2026_chapter_2_technical.pdf", "label": "Stanford AI Index 2026, chapter 2, technical performance"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$40cdffff-4deb-5ccf-aa5d-204f95d8dfd2$ml$::uuid, $ml$29ee7a26-f1ee-5490-befa-427c7af1ff3d$ml$::uuid, 6, $ml$The rule$ml$, $ml$In AI, the open gap is measured in months, and it resets with each closed release.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://epoch.ai/data-insights/open-closed-eci-gap", "label": "Epoch AI, the open and closed capability gap, May 2026"}, {"url": "https://hai.stanford.edu/assets/files/ai_index_report_2026_chapter_2_technical.pdf", "label": "Stanford AI Index 2026, chapter 2, technical performance"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$75fe67b4-47fb-53ca-8cd9-a19b51e0186a$ml$::uuid, $ml$e53e00b4-8f34-500d-9d62-570817d19e79$ml$::uuid, 1, $ml$Day 59 · The rules$ml$, $ml$Meta's Llama 4 license makes any company with more than 700 million monthly users ask Meta for permission. Meta released Llama 4 on April 5, 2025, under its own community license. Above 700 million monthly active users, a company must request a license, which Meta may grant or refuse at its sole discretion. The license also barred people and companies based in the EU from using or distributing the models.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://huggingface.co/meta-llama/Llama-4-Maverick-17B-128E-Original", "label": "Meta, Llama 4 Maverick model card and license"}, {"url": "https://techcrunch.com/2025/04/05/meta-releases-llama-4-a-new-crop-of-flagship-ai-models", "label": "TechCrunch, Meta releases Llama 4, Apr 2025"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$cffc2ad4-f3e5-5531-b51a-332f0208423a$ml$::uuid, $ml$e53e00b4-8f34-500d-9d62-570817d19e79$ml$::uuid, 2, $ml$More strings$ml$, $ml$Llama 4 users must display "Built with Llama". Any model they derive from it must have a name starting with Llama. Restrictions like these are why the Open Source Initiative says Llama is not open source.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://huggingface.co/meta-llama/Llama-4-Maverick-17B-128E-Original", "label": "Meta, Llama 4 Maverick model card and license"}, {"url": "https://siliconangle.com/2024/10/28/osi-clarifies-makes-ai-systems-open-source-open-models-fall-short/", "label": "SiliconANGLE, OSI defines open source AI and open models fall short, Oct 2024"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$83479144-e13e-57a8-ab64-34b90cccac09$ml$::uuid, $ml$e53e00b4-8f34-500d-9d62-570817d19e79$ml$::uuid, 3, $ml$Standard licenses$ml$, $ml$Many Chinese labs use standard licenses instead. Qwen3 ships under Apache 2.0 and DeepSeek-R1 under MIT. Moonshot's Kimi K2 uses a modified MIT license asking big commercial users for credit.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://hai.stanford.edu/assets/files/hai-digichina-issue-brief-beyond-deepseek-chinas-diverse-open-weight-ai-ecosystem-policy-implications.pdf", "label": "Stanford HAI and DigiChina, Beyond DeepSeek: China's open-weight AI ecosystem, Dec 2025"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$89f75fa3-e34f-5ebb-86f5-b19aed1b19d8$ml$::uuid, $ml$e53e00b4-8f34-500d-9d62-570817d19e79$ml$::uuid, 4, $ml$EU law gives open models a partial pass$ml$, $ml$Under the EU AI Act, free and open-source models can skip some documentation duties. Models with systemic risk get no such pass. Training with more than 10^25 FLOP makes a model presumed to carry systemic risk. Open models must still publish a summary of their training data.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://digital-strategy.ec.europa.eu/en/faqs/guidelines-obligations-gpai-providers", "label": "European Commission, FAQ on obligations for general-purpose AI providers"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$e0cb98f9-83e9-5aa6-ac95-c1347925db1f$ml$::uuid, $ml$e53e00b4-8f34-500d-9d62-570817d19e79$ml$::uuid, 5, $ml$Read the license before the scores$ml$, $ml$Ask which license a model uses before praising its benchmarks. Llama 4 barred EU firms at launch; Qwen3 uses Apache 2.0.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://techcrunch.com/2025/04/05/meta-releases-llama-4-a-new-crop-of-flagship-ai-models", "label": "TechCrunch, Meta releases Llama 4, Apr 2025"}, {"url": "https://hai.stanford.edu/assets/files/hai-digichina-issue-brief-beyond-deepseek-chinas-diverse-open-weight-ai-ecosystem-policy-implications.pdf", "label": "Stanford HAI and DigiChina, Beyond DeepSeek: China's open-weight AI ecosystem, Dec 2025"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$1a9e09f3-9fc2-507b-8a81-effd45cf0e1c$ml$::uuid, $ml$e53e00b4-8f34-500d-9d62-570817d19e79$ml$::uuid, 6, $ml$The rule$ml$, $ml$In open AI, the license decides who may use the model, and how.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://huggingface.co/meta-llama/Llama-4-Maverick-17B-128E-Original", "label": "Meta, Llama 4 Maverick model card and license"}, {"url": "https://hai.stanford.edu/assets/files/hai-digichina-issue-brief-beyond-deepseek-chinas-diverse-open-weight-ai-ecosystem-policy-implications.pdf", "label": "Stanford HAI and DigiChina, Beyond DeepSeek: China's open-weight AI ecosystem, Dec 2025"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$6d1d9df1-ae98-56a7-9a20-bb27c219f0a6$ml$::uuid, $ml$582f4b72-f3df-535b-b4a3-0bb73d091f17$ml$::uuid, 1, $ml$Day 59 · The rules$ml$, $ml$Meta's Llama 4 license makes any company with more than 700 million monthly users ask Meta for permission. Meta released Llama 4 on April 5, 2025, under its own community license. Above 700 million monthly active users, a company must request a license, which Meta may grant or refuse at its sole discretion. The license also barred people and companies based in the EU from using or distributing the models.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://huggingface.co/meta-llama/Llama-4-Maverick-17B-128E-Original", "label": "Meta, Llama 4 Maverick model card and license"}, {"url": "https://techcrunch.com/2025/04/05/meta-releases-llama-4-a-new-crop-of-flagship-ai-models", "label": "TechCrunch, Meta releases Llama 4, Apr 2025"}]$ml$::jsonb),
